window.DD_TESTS = [{"exam":"sat","set":1,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 1","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":true,"file":"sat-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Ecology","th":"นิเวศวิทยา"},"passage":"Ecologist Ruth Mbeki expected the drained wetland she was monitoring to recover slowly, if at all. Within three growing seasons, however, native sedges had returned in such numbers that the site's plant community was nearly ______ that of nearby undisturbed marshes: even experienced field surveyors could not reliably tell the two apart.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["derived from","indistinguishable from","remote from","absent from"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The colon explains the blank: surveyors 'could not reliably tell the two apart,' so the restored community was nearly 'indistinguishable from' the undisturbed marshes. 'Remote from' and 'absent from' suggest difference or distance, the opposite of the point, and 'derived from' (originating in) says nothing about the two looking alike.","th":"ข้อความหลังเครื่องหมาย : อธิบายช่องว่างว่า นักสำรวจ 'แยกสองแห่งออกจากกันไม่ได้' จึงต้องเป็น indistinguishable from (แทบแยกไม่ออกจาก) ส่วน remote from (ห่างไกลจาก) และ absent from (ไม่มีอยู่ใน) สื่อถึงความแตกต่างซึ่งตรงข้ามกับใจความ และ derived from (มีที่มาจาก) ไม่ได้บอกว่าทั้งสองแห่งเหมือนกัน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Genetics and behavior","th":"พันธุศาสตร์และพฤติกรรม"},"passage":"Early studies linking a single gene variant to a tendency toward risk-taking attracted wide public attention, but the association ultimately proved ______: follow-up studies drawing on tens of thousands of participants found that the variant's effect, if it existed at all, was far too small to detect reliably.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["tenuous","conspicuous","intractable","venerable"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The larger studies found an effect too small to detect, so the link turned out to be weak and poorly supported: 'tenuous.' 'Conspicuous' (obvious) is the opposite, 'intractable' means hard to solve or manage, and 'venerable' means respected because of age; none describes a link that fell apart under testing.","th":"งานวิจัยขนาดใหญ่พบว่าผลน้อยเกินกว่าจะตรวจพบ ความเชื่อมโยงจึง tenuous (อ่อน ไม่มั่นคง) ส่วน conspicuous (เด่นชัด) มีความหมายตรงข้าม intractable (แก้ยาก ควบคุมยาก) และ venerable (น่าเคารพเพราะเก่าแก่) ไม่ได้อธิบายความเชื่อมโยงที่พังลงเมื่อถูกทดสอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Agricultural science","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์การเกษตร"},"passage":"Alternate wetting and drying (AWD) is a rice-growing method in which fields are allowed to dry out periodically instead of being kept continuously flooded (CF). Agronomists compared the two methods at three sites. They report that AWD reduced water use at every site but that its effect on yield was not uniform: ______","stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["AWD used less water at Site A (860 mm) than at Site C (900 mm).","the highest yield under continuous flooding was recorded at Site C (6.0 tonnes per hectare).","yield under AWD was higher than yield under continuous flooding at Site A.","yield fell by 0.6 tonnes per hectare under AWD at Site C but changed by only 0.1 tonnes per hectare at Sites A and B."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The statement needs data showing that AWD's effect on yield differed among sites. At Site C yield dropped from 6.0 to 5.4 (0.6 t/ha), while at Site A it changed from 5.2 to 5.1 and at Site B from 4.8 to 4.9 (0.1 t/ha each). The water comparison between sites and the highest CF yield are accurate but say nothing about how AWD changed yield, and at Site A AWD yield (5.1) was lower, not higher, than CF yield (5.2).","th":"ข้อความต้องการข้อมูลที่แสดงว่าผลของ AWD ต่อผลผลิตไม่เท่ากันในแต่ละแหล่ง ที่ Site C ผลผลิตลดจาก 6.0 เหลือ 5.4 (ลด 0.6 ตัน/เฮกตาร์) ขณะที่ Site A เปลี่ยนจาก 5.2 เป็น 5.1 และ Site B จาก 4.8 เป็น 4.9 (เปลี่ยนเพียง 0.1) การเทียบปริมาณน้ำระหว่างแหล่งและผลผลิต CF สูงสุดเป็นข้อมูลจริงแต่ไม่ได้บอกผลของ AWD ต่อผลผลิต และที่ Site A ผลผลิต AWD (5.1) ต่ำกว่า CF (5.2) ไม่ใช่สูงกว่า"},"table":{"title":"Rice yield and water use under two irrigation methods","rows":[["Site","Method","Yield (tonnes per hectare)","Water used (mm)"],["Site A","CF","5.2","1,200"],["Site A","AWD","5.1","860"],["Site B","CF","4.8","1,150"],["Site B","AWD","4.9","830"],["Site C","CF","6.0","1,300"],["Site C","AWD","5.4","900"]]}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Contemporary art","th":"ศิลปะร่วมสมัย"},"passage":"Thai artist Montien Boonma's installation House of Hope—a room hung with thousands of strands of beads made from fragrant medicinal ______ designed to surround visitors with a soothing, healing scent.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["herbs, was","herbs—was","herbs; was","herbs was"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The description 'a room hung with… medicinal herbs' is a supplementary element that begins with a dash, so it must close with a matching dash before the verb 'was.' A comma would mismatch the opening dash, a semicolon separates the subject from its verb, and no punctuation leaves the element unclosed.","th":"ส่วนขยาย a room hung with… medicinal herbs เปิดด้วยขีดยาว (—) จึงต้องปิดด้วยขีดยาวเช่นกันก่อนกริยา was การใช้จุลภาคไม่เข้าคู่กับขีดเปิด อัฒภาคแยกประธานออกจากกริยา และไม่ใส่เครื่องหมายทำให้ส่วนขยายไม่ถูกปิด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Geology","th":"ธรณีวิทยา"},"passage":"Iceland sits directly on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, a boundary where two tectonic plates are slowly pulling apart and molten rock rises to fill the gap. ______ the island experiences frequent volcanic eruptions and is dotted with hot springs.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","As a result,"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Iceland's position on a spreading plate boundary where molten rock rises causes its frequent eruptions and hot springs, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'As a result,' fits. 'In contrast' and 'Nevertheless' signal opposition, and 'For instance' introduces an example of a general claim, which the second sentence is not.","th":"ตำแหน่งของไอซ์แลนด์บนแนวแยกของแผ่นเปลือกโลกที่มีหินหลอมเหลวดันขึ้นมาเป็นสาเหตุของภูเขาไฟและน้ำพุร้อน จึงใช้คำเชื่อมเหตุและผล As a result ส่วน In contrast และ Nevertheless แสดงความขัดแย้ง และ For instance ใช้ยกตัวอย่าง ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับความสัมพันธ์นี้"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"International relations","th":"ความสัมพันธ์ระหว่างประเทศ"},"passage":"The language of the 1998 river-sharing treaty was deliberately ______. By never defining what counted as a \"fair share\" of the water, the negotiators allowed each government to present the agreement to its own citizens as a victory, while postponing the hardest questions to future talks.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["equivocal","redundant","candid","punitive"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The negotiators avoided defining key terms so that each side could read the treaty its own way; the language was open to more than one interpretation: 'equivocal.' 'Candid' (frank) is nearly the opposite, 'redundant' means needlessly repetitive, and 'punitive' means intended to punish; none explains why each side could claim victory.","th":"ผู้เจรจาจงใจไม่นิยามคำสำคัญเพื่อให้แต่ละฝ่ายตีความสนธิสัญญาตามแบบของตน ภาษาจึง equivocal (กำกวม ตีความได้หลายทาง) ส่วน candid (ตรงไปตรงมา) เกือบตรงข้าม redundant (ซ้ำซ้อน) และ punitive (มุ่งลงโทษ) ไม่ได้อธิบายว่าทำไมทุกฝ่ายจึงอ้างชัยชนะได้"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature: fiction","th":"วรรณกรรม: บันเทิงคดี"},"passage":"The following text is from a 1911 novel. Agnes has returned to the village where she grew up after an absence of twenty years.\nThe lane was narrower than she remembered, and the great elm that had once seemed to hold up the sky was only a tree, handsome but ordinary. She waited for the old feeling of homecoming as one waits for a train that is already late. It did not come. Instead she found herself noting, with a surveyor's detachment, which cottages had been re-roofed and which had not.","stem":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To catalog the physical changes the village has undergone during the character's absence.","To explain why the character left the village twenty years earlier.","To suggest that the character deeply regrets her decision to return to the village.","To depict a character's realization that a familiar place no longer stirs the emotions she expected it to."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The details (the narrow lane, the ordinary elm, the waited-for feeling that 'did not come,' her 'surveyor's detachment') all show Agnes expecting emotion and finding none. The physical details serve that purpose rather than being the purpose, as the lane and elm may not have changed at all; no reason for leaving is given; and detachment is not the same as deep regret.","th":"รายละเอียดทั้งหมด (ทางที่แคบลง ต้นเอล์มที่ธรรมดา ความรู้สึกที่รอแต่ 'ไม่มา' และความเฉยชาแบบนักสำรวจ) แสดงว่าแอกเนสคาดหวังความรู้สึกแต่ไม่พบ รายละเอียดทางกายภาพเป็นเครื่องมือ ไม่ใช่จุดประสงค์ (ทางและต้นไม้อาจไม่ได้เปลี่ยนเลย แต่เธอมองต่างไป) บทอ่านไม่ได้บอกเหตุผลที่เธอจากไป และความเฉยชาไม่ใช่ความเสียใจอย่างลึกซึ้ง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-textual","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature: fiction","th":"วรรณกรรม: บันเทิงคดี"},"passage":"The Lantern Field is an 1897 novel by Margaret Ashdown. In the novel, the narrator portrays Mrs. Penrose as a woman who conceals strong feeling behind a show of indifference.","stem":"Which quotation from The Lantern Field most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["\"Mrs. Penrose never wept at funerals, and the village admired her greatly for her composure.\"","\"'It is nothing to me whether the boy writes or not,' said Mrs. Penrose, and she turned the letter face down on the table—but not before she had read it a third time.\"","\"Mrs. Penrose wept openly on the platform when her son's train pulled away, and she did not care who saw her.\"","\"She kept her house as she kept her accounts: orderly, exact, and without a single ornament.\""],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The claim needs both a show of indifference and hidden strong feeling. Mrs. Penrose says the letter is 'nothing' to her (indifference) yet has read it three times (concealed feeling). The funeral quotation shows composure but no hidden emotion, the platform quotation shows feeling displayed openly rather than concealed, and the housekeeping quotation shows neither.","th":"ข้ออ้างต้องมีทั้งการแสดงท่าทีไม่ใส่ใจ และความรู้สึกลึกที่ซ่อนไว้ นางเพนโรสพูดว่าจดหมาย 'ไม่สำคัญ' (ท่าทีไม่ใส่ใจ) แต่ได้อ่านไปแล้วสามรอบ (ความรู้สึกที่ซ่อนอยู่) คำพูดเรื่องงานศพแสดงความสงบแต่ไม่มีความรู้สึกที่ซ่อน คำพูดเรื่องร้องไห้ที่ชานชาลาแสดงความรู้สึกอย่างเปิดเผย และคำพูดเรื่องการดูแลบ้านไม่ตรงกับทั้งสองส่วน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Astronomy","th":"ดาราศาสตร์"},"passage":"The rings of icy debris that astronomers detected around Quaoar, a dwarf planet orbiting far beyond Neptune, ______ much farther from the body than standard models predict rings can survive without clumping together into a moon.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["lie","lies","is lying","has lain"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The subject is the plural noun 'rings'; 'of icy debris,' the 'that' clause, and the appositive about Quaoar are all modifiers. A plural subject takes the plural verb 'lie.' 'Lies,' 'is lying,' and 'has lain' are singular forms that wrongly agree with a nearer noun such as 'debris' or 'Quaoar.'","th":"ประธานคือ rings (พหูพจน์) ส่วน of icy debris อนุประโยค that และส่วนขยายเกี่ยวกับ Quaoar เป็นตัวขยายทั้งหมด จึงใช้กริยาพหูพจน์ lie ส่วน lies, is lying และ has lain เป็นรูปเอกพจน์ที่ผันตามคำนามใกล้ ๆ อย่าง debris หรือ Quaoar อย่างผิด ๆ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Chemistry","th":"เคมี"},"passage":"Nearly all metals are solid at room temperature, which is why they are so useful for building tools and structures. Mercury, ______ is a liquid at room temperature and freezes only at about −39°C.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["therefore,","for example,","however,","in addition,"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Mercury being a liquid contrasts with the fact that nearly all metals are solid, so 'however' fits. 'Therefore' signals a result, 'for example' would present mercury as a typical solid metal, and 'in addition' adds a similar point.","th":"ปรอทเป็นของเหลว ซึ่งขัดกับข้อเท็จจริงที่ว่าโลหะเกือบทั้งหมดเป็นของแข็ง จึงใช้ however ส่วน therefore แสดงผลลัพธ์ for example จะทำให้ปรอทเป็นตัวอย่างของโลหะที่เป็นของแข็ง และ in addition ใช้เพิ่มข้อมูลที่คล้ายกัน"}}]}},{"exam":"sat","set":2,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 2","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"sat-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Civil engineering","th":"วิศวกรรมโยธา"},"passage":"Concrete cracks as it ages, and water seeping through the cracks corrodes the steel bars inside. Engineers are now testing concrete laced with dormant bacterial spores and calcium-rich nutrients. When water enters a crack, the spores revive and produce limestone that seals the gap. Because the bacteria act only when damage occurs, the repair is essentially ______: no inspector needs to find the crack, and no worker needs to fill it.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ornamental","autonomous","temporary","reluctant"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The colon explains the blank: no inspector and no worker is needed, so the repair happens by itself, without outside control — 'autonomous'. 'Temporary' is not supported (nothing says the seal fails), 'ornamental' means decorative, and 'reluctant' (unwilling) contradicts bacteria that act whenever damage occurs.","th":"ข้อความหลังเครื่องหมาย : อธิบายคำที่เว้นไว้ คือไม่ต้องมีผู้ตรวจหาหรือคนงานมาซ่อม การซ่อมจึงเกิดขึ้นเองโดยไม่ต้องมีใครควบคุม = autonomous (ทำงานได้เอง) ส่วน temporary (ชั่วคราว) ไม่มีข้อมูลสนับสนุน ornamental แปลว่าเพื่อการตกแต่ง และ reluctant (ไม่เต็มใจ) ขัดกับการที่แบคทีเรียทำงานทันทีเมื่อเกิดรอยร้าว"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"History of science","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์วิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Historians increasingly portray Charles Darwin as the hub of a vast network of correspondents: pigeon breeders, gardeners, colonial officials, and naturalists on several continents sent him observations that he drew on in On the Origin of Species. Historian Léa Morand cautions, however, against concluding that Darwin merely compiled what others told him. He did not simply ______ his correspondents' reports, she notes; he checked them against one another and against his own experiments, and he discarded many that failed these tests.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["corroborate","repudiate","embellish","defer to"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Morand's point is that Darwin was not passive: instead of just accepting what he was told, he tested the reports and threw many out. 'Defer to' (accept someone's judgment without question) is exactly what he did not 'simply' do. 'Corroborate' (confirm with evidence) already involves checking, so it cannot be contrasted with checking; 'repudiate' (reject) would make the contrast illogical; 'embellish' (add decorative detail) is unrelated to testing.","th":"ประเด็นของ Morand คือ Darwin ไม่ได้รับข้อมูลมาเฉย ๆ แต่ตรวจสอบและทิ้งรายงานจำนวนมาก สิ่งที่เขา 'ไม่ได้ทำเพียงอย่างเดียว' คือการเชื่อตามโดยไม่ตั้งคำถาม = defer to ส่วน corroborate (ยืนยันด้วยหลักฐาน) ก็คือการตรวจสอบอยู่แล้ว จึงนำมาเปรียบเทียบแบบขัดแย้งกับการตรวจสอบไม่ได้ repudiate (ปฏิเสธ) ทำให้ความหมายขัดกัน และ embellish (เสริมแต่ง) ไม่เกี่ยวกับการตรวจสอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Ecology","th":"นิเวศวิทยา"},"passage":"Ecologist Tomasz Wierzbicki studied how far the \"edge effect\"—increased predation on bird nests near the boundary between a forest and open land—extends into a forest. He placed artificial nests at four distances from the edge of a Polish forest and recorded the percentage raided by predators within two weeks. He concluded that the edge effect is concentrated within about 100 meters of the edge, noting that ______","table":{"title":"Artificial nests raided by predators, by distance from the forest edge","rows":[["Distance from edge (m)","Nests placed","Nests raided (%)"],["0–50","120","62"],["50–100","120","47"],["100–200","120","30"],["more than 200","120","28"]]},"stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["nests within 50 meters of the edge were raided more often (62%) than nests at any other distance.","nests 100–200 meters from the edge were raided at nearly the same rate as nests more than 200 meters from the edge (30% versus 28%), while nests closer than 100 meters were raided far more often.","the same number of nests, 120, was placed at each of the four distances from the edge.","the percentage of nests raided fell by a similar amount with each increase in distance, from 62% to 28%."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"To show the effect is concentrated within 100 m, the data must show high predation inside 100 m and little change beyond it: 62% and 47% inside 100 m, but 30% and 28% — almost equal — beyond. The 0–50 m figure alone does not locate where the effect stops; the equal number of nests is about method; and the drops were not similar (15, 17, then only 2 points), so that statement is false.","th":"หากจะแสดงว่าผลกระทบจากขอบป่ากระจุกอยู่ภายใน 100 เมตร ต้องชี้ว่าภายใน 100 เมตรถูกล่ามาก (62% และ 47%) แต่เลย 100 เมตรไปแล้วแทบไม่เปลี่ยน (30% กับ 28%) ตัวเลข 62% อย่างเดียวไม่ได้บอกว่าผลกระทบสิ้นสุดที่ใด จำนวนรัง 120 รังเป็นเรื่องวิธีการ และการลดลงไม่ได้เท่า ๆ กัน (ลด 15, 17 แล้วเพียง 2 จุด) ข้อความนั้นจึงผิด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Architecture","th":"สถาปัตยกรรม"},"passage":"The architect Lina Bo Bardi, who was born in Rome in 1914 but did her most celebrated work after moving to Brazil ______ designed the São Paulo Museum of Art, whose main gallery is raised high above the ground on four massive concrete pillars, leaving an open public plaza underneath.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Brazil designed","Brazil—designed","Brazil; designed","Brazil, designed"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The clause 'who was born in Rome … moving to Brazil' is extra information that opens with a comma after 'Bo Bardi', so it must close with a matching comma before the main verb 'designed'. Leaving it out runs the clause into the verb, a dash does not match the opening comma, and a semicolon wrongly separates the subject from its verb.","th":"อนุประโยค who was born in Rome … moving to Brazil เป็นข้อมูลเสริมที่เปิดด้วยจุลภาคหลัง Bo Bardi จึงต้องปิดด้วยจุลภาคก่อนกริยาหลัก designed ให้เป็นคู่กัน การไม่ใส่เครื่องหมายทำให้ส่วนขยายต่อกับกริยาโดยตรง ขีดยาว (—) ไม่เข้าคู่กับจุลภาคที่เปิดไว้ และ ; แยกประธานออกจากกริยาอย่างผิดหลัก"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Evolutionary biology","th":"ชีววิทยาวิวัฒนาการ"},"passage":"Mammals that eat chili peppers grind the seeds with their molars, destroying them. Birds, by contrast, swallow the seeds whole and later excrete them intact, often far from the parent plant. Capsaicin, the compound that makes chilies taste hot, activates pain receptors in mammals but not in birds. ______ capsaicin may have evolved to deter seed-destroying mammals while leaving seed-dispersing birds free to eat the fruit.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Nevertheless,","Thus,","Likewise,","For example,"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The last sentence draws a conclusion from the facts before it (mammals destroy seeds, birds spread them, capsaicin hurts only mammals), so a cause-and-effect transition, 'Thus', fits. 'Nevertheless' signals contrast, 'Likewise' a similar point, and 'For example' an illustration of a general claim — none of which the sentence provides.","th":"ประโยคสุดท้ายเป็นข้อสรุปจากข้อเท็จจริงก่อนหน้า (สัตว์เลี้ยงลูกด้วยนมทำลายเมล็ด นกช่วยกระจายเมล็ด และสารแคปไซซินทำให้เจ็บเฉพาะสัตว์เลี้ยงลูกด้วยนม) จึงใช้คำเชื่อมแสดงผล Thus ส่วน Nevertheless แสดงความขัดแย้ง Likewise แสดงความคล้ายกัน และ For example ใช้ยกตัวอย่าง ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับหน้าที่ของประโยค"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Oceanography","th":"สมุทรศาสตร์"},"passage":"Until the late 1970s, many oceanographers assumed that the deep seafloor, far beyond the reach of sunlight, was nearly ______ of life. The discovery of hydrothermal vents near the Galápagos Islands in 1977 overturned this view: around the vents, where bacteria draw energy from chemicals in the hot water, scientists found dense colonies of giant tubeworms, clams, and crabs.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["capable","representative","fond","devoid"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The discovery 'overturned' the earlier view by revealing dense colonies of animals, so the old assumption must have been that the deep seafloor had almost no life — it was 'devoid' (empty) of life. 'Capable of life' would not be overturned by finding life, 'representative of life' makes no sense here, and 'fond of' describes affection.","th":"การค้นพบนี้ 'ล้มล้าง' ความเชื่อเดิมด้วยการพบสัตว์จำนวนมาก ความเชื่อเดิมจึงต้องเป็นว่าพื้นทะเลลึกแทบไม่มีสิ่งมีชีวิต = devoid of (ปราศจาก) ถ้าเชื่อเดิมว่า capable of life (มีชีวิตอยู่ได้) การพบสิ่งมีชีวิตก็ไม่ได้ล้มล้างอะไร representative of ไม่มีความหมายในบริบทนี้ และ fond of แปลว่าชอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Neuroscience","th":"ประสาทวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Place cells in the hippocampus of rats fire when an animal occupies a particular spot in its environment, so that, collectively, they form something like an internal map. In a 2023 study, neuroscientist Ruth Adeyemi recorded these cells while rats rested between runs through a maze. The cells fired in rapid sequences resembling those produced when a rat actually runs along a path—sometimes along paths the animal had never run. Adeyemi cautions that the finding does not show that rats imagine or plan journeys; it shows only that the brain can generate route-like patterns of activity while the animal is still.","stem":"Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To argue that rats plan their routes through a maze before they begin moving","To challenge the view that place cells form an internal map of an animal's surroundings","To report a finding about activity in the rat hippocampus while qualifying what that finding can be taken to demonstrate","To explain why rats need to rest between runs through a maze in order to learn its layout"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The text presents Adeyemi's finding (route-like firing while rats rest) and then limits its meaning: it does not show planning or imagination. The claim that rats plan routes is exactly what Adeyemi says the finding does not show; the map view is accepted, not challenged; and the need for rest is never discussed.","th":"บทอ่านเสนอผลการศึกษา (เซลล์ยิงสัญญาณเหมือนกำลังวิ่งตามเส้นทาง ขณะที่หนูพักอยู่) แล้วจำกัดขอบเขตการตีความว่าไม่ได้พิสูจน์ว่าหนูวางแผนหรือจินตนาการ จุดประสงค์จึงเป็น 'รายงานผลการค้นพบพร้อมระบุข้อจำกัดของสิ่งที่ผลนั้นพิสูจน์ได้' การอ้างว่าหนูวางแผนเส้นทางคือสิ่งที่ Adeyemi บอกว่าพิสูจน์ไม่ได้ บทอ่านยอมรับแนวคิดแผนที่ภายใน ไม่ได้โต้แย้ง และไม่ได้พูดถึงความจำเป็นของการพัก"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Agricultural science","th":"เกษตรศาสตร์"},"passage":"Agronomist Wanjiru Kamau grew maize and beans both separately (in monoculture) and together in alternating rows (intercropping). To compare such systems, agronomists use the land equivalent ratio (LER): for each crop, the intercropped yield is divided by the monoculture yield, and the resulting fractions are added together. An LER greater than 1 indicates that intercropping produced more from a given area than growing the same crops separately would have. Kamau concluded that intercropping used land more efficiently, even though each crop yielded less per hectare when intercropped.","table":{"title":"Yields in Kamau's trial (metric tons per hectare)","rows":[["Crop","Monoculture yield","Intercropped yield"],["Maize","6.0","4.5"],["Beans","2.0","1.0"]]},"stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Kamau's conclusion?","choices":["Intercropped maize produced 75% and intercropped beans 50% of their monoculture yields, giving an LER of 1.25.","Intercropped maize yielded 4.5 metric tons per hectare, 1.5 metric tons less than maize grown in monoculture.","Together, the intercropped crops yielded 5.5 metric tons per hectare, less than the 6.0 metric tons per hectare of maize grown in monoculture.","Intercropped beans produced 50% of their monoculture yield, so the LER for the trial was 0.5."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion is that intercropping used land more efficiently, which the text defines as an LER above 1. Maize: 4.5 ÷ 6.0 = 0.75; beans: 1.0 ÷ 2.0 = 0.5; LER = 0.75 + 0.5 = 1.25, which is greater than 1. The 1.5-ton drop in maize shows only that each crop yielded less; comparing 5.5 tons of mixed crops with 6.0 tons of maize ignores the LER method and seems to point the other way; an LER of 0.5 is miscalculated, since it leaves out the maize fraction.","th":"ข้อสรุปคือการปลูกร่วมใช้ที่ดินได้มีประสิทธิภาพกว่า ซึ่งบทอ่านนิยามว่า LER มากกว่า 1 ข้าวโพด 4.5 ÷ 6.0 = 0.75 ถั่ว 1.0 ÷ 2.0 = 0.5 รวม LER = 1.25 > 1 จึงสนับสนุนข้อสรุป ส่วนการที่ข้าวโพดลดลง 1.5 ตันแสดงแค่ว่าผลผลิตแต่ละพืชลดลง การเทียบ 5.5 ตันกับ 6.0 ตันไม่ได้ใช้วิธี LER และดูเหมือนแย้งข้อสรุป และ LER 0.5 คำนวณผิดเพราะไม่ได้รวมสัดส่วนของข้าวโพด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Botany","th":"พฤกษศาสตร์"},"passage":"Iceland's lichens grow on lava fields, sea cliffs, and even the walls of old farmhouses, yet for many years no one attempted to document them comprehensively. Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens across the island, ______","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["a field guide describing nearly 750 lichen species was published by botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir.","botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir's field guide describes nearly 750 lichen species.","botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir published a field guide describing nearly 750 lichen species.","nearly 750 lichen species are described in botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir's field guide."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The opening phrase 'Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens' must be followed immediately by the person who did the collecting. Only the version beginning with 'botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir' does this. The other versions make a field guide, the botanist's guide (a possessive, so the guide is the subject), or lichen species the collectors — dangling modifiers.","th":"วลีนำ Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens ต้องตามด้วยผู้ที่เก็บตัวอย่างทันที คือ botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir ส่วนตัวเลือกอื่นทำให้คู่มือภาคสนาม คู่มือของนักพฤกษศาสตร์ (รูปแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ ประธานจึงเป็นคู่มือ) หรือพันธุ์ไลเคนกลายเป็นผู้เก็บตัวอย่าง ซึ่งเป็น dangling modifier"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Urban history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์เมือง"},"passage":"During the hot summer of 1858, the stench of sewage in the River Thames grew so overpowering that Parliament considered abandoning Westminster. Within weeks, lawmakers funded engineer Joseph Bazalgette's plan for a network of sewers that would carry waste far downstream. The system was meant to remove foul odors, which many physicians of the day believed caused disease. ______ it also largely ended London's cholera outbreaks, since cholera in fact spreads through contaminated drinking water rather than through the air.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["As it happened,","For this reason,","Similarly,","In other words,"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The sewers were built for the wrong reason (to remove smells), yet they ended cholera for a different reason (cleaner water). 'As it happened' introduces this unplanned, fortunate outcome. 'For this reason' would make the mistaken belief about odors the cause of the success; 'Similarly' and 'In other words' signal a parallel point or a restatement, which the sentence is not.","th":"ท่อระบายน้ำถูกสร้างด้วยเหตุผลที่ผิด (เพื่อกำจัดกลิ่น) แต่บังเอิญยุติอหิวาตกโรคได้ด้วยเหตุผลอื่น (น้ำดื่มสะอาดขึ้น) จึงใช้ As it happened (ปรากฏว่า/บังเอิญว่า) ส่วน For this reason ทำให้ความเชื่อผิด ๆ เรื่องกลิ่นกลายเป็นสาเหตุของความสำเร็จ Similarly แสดงความคล้าย และ In other words ใช้กล่าวซ้ำด้วยคำอื่น ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับเนื้อความ"}}]}},{"exam":"sat","set":3,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 3","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"sat-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Planetary science","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์ดาวเคราะห์"},"passage":"Lava tubes form when the outer surface of a lava flow cools and hardens while molten rock keeps moving beneath it; once the eruption ends and the lava drains away, a hollow tunnel remains. Because thick rock walls would shield anyone inside from cosmic radiation, meteorite impacts, and extreme swings in surface temperature, many planetary scientists regard the large lava tubes detected beneath the Moon's surface as ______ locations for future crewed bases.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["hazardous","ornamental","promising","redundant"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The text lists only advantages of lava tubes (protection from radiation, impacts and temperature swings), so scientists must see them as good candidates for bases: 'promising'. 'Hazardous' contradicts the protective qualities described, 'ornamental' (decorative) is irrelevant to a base, and 'redundant' (unnecessary) does not follow from a list of benefits.","th":"บทความกล่าวถึงแต่ข้อดีของอุโมงค์ลาวา (ป้องกันรังสีคอสมิก อุกกาบาต และอุณหภูมิที่แกว่งรุนแรง) นักวิทยาศาสตร์จึงมองว่าเป็นทำเลที่ 'มีแนวโน้มดี' คือ promising ส่วน hazardous (อันตราย) ขัดกับคุณสมบัติด้านการป้องกัน ornamental (ใช้ประดับ) ไม่เกี่ยวกับฐานที่พัก และ redundant (ซ้ำซ้อนไม่จำเป็น) ไม่ได้ตามมาจากรายการข้อดี"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature (fiction)","th":"วรรณกรรม (บันเทิงคดี)"},"passage":"The following text is from a short story.\nMarguerite had rehearsed the conversation for weeks, polishing each sentence until it gleamed. She would be calm; she would be reasonable; she would not mention the piano. But when her brother opened the door wearing their father's gray cardigan, its elbows worn nearly through, every prepared word deserted her. She stood on the step and heard herself remark, absurdly, that the hedge needed trimming. Edmund looked at the hedge, then at her, and something in his face loosened. \"It always did,\" he said, and stepped aside to let her in.","stem":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It marks a shift from Marguerite's careful planning to an unscripted moment, prompted by a detail that recalls the siblings' shared past.","It reveals the specific disagreement that caused Marguerite and Edmund to become estranged.","It explains why Marguerite had decided beforehand that she would not mention the piano.","It suggests that Edmund had anticipated Marguerite's visit and prepared for it as carefully as she had."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The first two sentences show Marguerite's rehearsed plan; the third ('But when her brother opened the door…') shows the plan collapsing when she sees their father's cardigan, a reminder of their shared family past, and the rest of the text follows her unplanned remark. The sentence never names the cause of any quarrel or the reason for avoiding the piano, and it says nothing about Edmund preparing for the visit.","th":"สองประโยคแรกแสดงแผนที่มาร์เกอริตซ้อมมา ประโยคที่สามแสดงว่าแผนพังทลายเมื่อเธอเห็นเสื้อคาร์ดิแกนของพ่อซึ่งเตือนถึงอดีตที่พี่น้องมีร่วมกัน และส่วนที่เหลือเล่าคำพูดที่ไม่ได้เตรียมไว้ ประโยคนี้ไม่ได้บอกสาเหตุของความบาดหมาง ไม่ได้อธิบายเรื่องเปียโน และไม่ได้บอกว่าเอ็ดมันด์เตรียมตัวรอเธอ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"inferences","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Geology","th":"ธรณีวิทยา"},"passage":"Tiny zircon crystals from the Jack Hills of Western Australia are among the oldest known fragments of Earth, some having formed about 4.4 billion years ago. The ratio of oxygen isotopes in several of these crystals matches the ratio found in rocks that formed from material altered by liquid water at Earth's surface, leading some geologists to conclude that oceans existed far earlier than once thought. Other geologists caution that fluids passing through the rocks long after the crystals formed could have changed their oxygen isotope ratios. If such later alteration occurred, then ______","stem":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the crystals must have formed more recently than 4.4 billion years ago.","Earth's earliest oceans must have contained more oxygen than present-day oceans do.","the crystals' isotope ratios would not necessarily reveal whether liquid water was present at Earth's surface 4.4 billion years ago.","the Jack Hills would have been covered by ocean water for most of Earth's history."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion about early oceans depends on the isotope ratios recording conditions when the crystals formed. If fluids changed the ratios later, the ratios might reflect that later event instead, so they could no longer be trusted as evidence about the surface 4.4 billion years ago. Later alteration says nothing about when the crystals formed, about the oxygen content of ancient oceans, or about how long the Jack Hills were under water.","th":"ข้อสรุปเรื่องมหาสมุทรยุคแรกอาศัยว่าอัตราส่วนไอโซโทปบันทึกสภาพตอนที่ผลึกก่อตัว ถ้าของไหลเปลี่ยนอัตราส่วนในภายหลัง อัตราส่วนนั้นอาจสะท้อนเหตุการณ์ภายหลังแทน จึงใช้เป็นหลักฐานเรื่องน้ำบนผิวโลกเมื่อ 4.4 พันล้านปีก่อนไม่ได้อย่างแน่ชัด การเปลี่ยนแปลงภายหลังไม่ได้บอกว่าผลึกเกิดเมื่อใด ไม่ได้บอกปริมาณออกซิเจนในมหาสมุทรโบราณ และไม่ได้บอกว่าแจ็กฮิลส์จมน้ำนานเท่าใด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Music history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์ดนตรี"},"passage":"Clara Schumann was among the most admired concert pianists of the nineteenth century, and her programs helped establish the solo recital as a concert format. Schumann—who first performed in public at the age of nine and toured Europe for more than six decades, often playing works by composers who were still largely ______ also composed songs, chamber music, and a piano concerto that she completed while still a teenager.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["unknown,","unknown—","unknown;","unknown"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The interruption about Schumann's performing career opens with a dash after 'Schumann', so it must close with a matching dash before the main verb 'also composed'. A comma does not pair with an opening dash, a semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, and leaving out punctuation would run the interruption into the main clause.","th":"ข้อความแทรกเกี่ยวกับอาชีพนักเปียโนเปิดด้วยขีดยาว (—) หลัง Schumann จึงต้องปิดด้วยขีดยาวเช่นกันก่อนกริยาหลัก also composed จุลภาคไม่เข้าคู่กับขีดยาว อัฒภาคตัดประธานออกจากกริยา และหากไม่ใส่เครื่องหมาย ข้อความแทรกจะต่อกับประโยคหลักผิดโครงสร้าง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Materials science","th":"วัสดุศาสตร์"},"passage":"Engineers have long been interested in the dragline silk that orb-weaving spiders use for the frames of their webs and for their safety lines. Weight for weight, this silk is stronger than steel, and spiders produce it at room temperature from a water-based solution of proteins. ______ it can stretch to roughly 130 percent of its original length before breaking, allowing a web to absorb the impact of a fast-flying insect without tearing.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["However,","Therefore,","Moreover,","For example,"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence adds a second impressive property (stretchiness) to the first (strength), so an additive transition, 'Moreover', fits. 'However' would wrongly signal a contrast; 'Therefore' would claim that strength causes stretchiness; 'For example' would present the stretchiness as an instance of strength, which it is not.","th":"ประโยคนี้เพิ่มคุณสมบัติเด่นข้อที่สอง (ยืดได้) ต่อจากข้อแรก (แข็งแรง) จึงใช้คำเชื่อมแสดงการเพิ่มเติม Moreover ส่วน However แสดงความขัดแย้งซึ่งไม่มี Therefore อ้างว่าความแข็งแรงเป็นสาเหตุของการยืด และ For example ทำให้การยืดกลายเป็นตัวอย่างของความแข็งแรง ซึ่งไม่ถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Behavioral economics","th":"เศรษฐศาสตร์พฤติกรรม"},"passage":"When employers automatically enroll new workers in a retirement savings plan, allowing them to opt out if they wish, participation rates are often well above 80 percent; when workers must actively sign up, rates are frequently far lower. Economists attribute much of this gap to inertia. Rather than ______ a decision that has already been made on their behalf, most people simply let it stand, even when changing it would take only a few minutes.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ratify","contest","anticipate","publicize"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence contrasts letting a decision 'stand' with doing something to it; the logical opposite of accepting a default is challenging or overriding it: 'contest'. 'Ratify' (formally approve) is a trap: approving a decision is close to letting it stand, so 'rather than ratify' makes no sense. You cannot 'anticipate' a decision that has already been made, and 'publicize' has nothing to do with inertia.","th":"ประโยคเปรียบการปล่อยให้การตัดสินใจคงอยู่ กับการทำบางอย่างต่อการตัดสินใจนั้น สิ่งที่ตรงข้ามกับการยอมรับค่าเริ่มต้นคือการคัดค้านหรือเปลี่ยนแปลง จึงใช้ contest ส่วน ratify (ให้สัตยาบัน/รับรอง) เป็นตัวลวง เพราะการรับรองก็ใกล้เคียงกับการปล่อยไว้ จึงขัดกับ rather than คาดการณ์ (anticipate) การตัดสินใจที่เกิดขึ้นแล้วไม่ได้ และการเผยแพร่ (publicize) ไม่เกี่ยวกับความเฉื่อย"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"European history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์ยุโรป"},"passage":"Popular accounts often credit Johannes Gutenberg's printing press with igniting the Protestant Reformation almost single-handedly. Historian Margit Ellery contends that such accounts confuse a necessary condition with a sufficient one. To be sure, Martin Luther's pamphlets circulated in hundreds of thousands of copies within a few years of 1517, a scale unimaginable a century earlier. But presses had operated in German-speaking lands for more than half a century without provoking comparable upheaval; what changed, Ellery argues, was the convergence of print with the political ambitions of territorial princes and a literate urban public hungry for religious writing in its own language.","stem":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It acknowledges evidence that appears to support the view Ellery questions before the text explains why that evidence is not decisive.","It presents the main evidence Ellery uses to show that the printing press was irrelevant to the Reformation.","It introduces a counterargument to Ellery's claim that the text ultimately accepts as correct.","It explains why Luther chose to publish his ideas in pamphlets rather than in longer books."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'To be sure' signals a concession: the huge circulation of Luther's pamphlets seems to back the popular view that print caused the Reformation. The next sentence ('But …') limits that evidence: presses had existed for decades without such upheaval, so print was necessary but not sufficient. Ellery never calls the press irrelevant, the text does not end up accepting the popular view, and Luther's choice of format is never discussed.","th":"To be sure เป็นสัญญาณของการยอมรับบางส่วน คือยอดพิมพ์จุลสารของลูเทอร์ดูเหมือนสนับสนุนความเชื่อที่ว่าแท่นพิมพ์ทำให้เกิดการปฏิรูปศาสนา ประโยคถัดไป (But …) จำกัดน้ำหนักของหลักฐานนั้น เพราะแท่นพิมพ์มีมาหลายสิบปีโดยไม่เกิดความปั่นป่วนแบบนี้ แท่นพิมพ์จึงจำเป็นแต่ไม่เพียงพอ เอลเลอรีไม่ได้บอกว่าแท่นพิมพ์ไม่เกี่ยวข้อง บทความไม่ได้ยอมรับมุมมองยอดนิยมในตอนท้าย และไม่ได้พูดถึงเหตุผลที่ลูเทอร์เลือกพิมพ์เป็นจุลสาร"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-textual","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Cognitive psychology","th":"จิตวิทยาการรู้คิด"},"passage":"In several experiments, museum visitors who photographed artworks later recalled fewer details of those artworks than visitors who simply looked at them, a pattern known as the photo-taking-impairment effect. Psychologist Noor Haddad hypothesizes that the effect arises from cognitive offloading: people who take photos implicitly expect the camera to remember for them and so devote less effort to forming their own memories. To test her hypothesis, Haddad told some visitors that their photos would be saved and told others that each photo would be deleted automatically as soon as it was taken.","stem":"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Haddad's hypothesis?","choices":["Visitors who expected to keep their photos recalled fewer details than visitors who were told that their photos would be deleted.","Visitors who took photos reported enjoying their museum visit about as much as visitors who did not take photos.","Visitors who photographed artworks still recalled fewer details than non-photographers when tested a week later.","Visitors who were told that their photos would be deleted immediately recalled as few details as visitors who expected to keep their photos."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"If offloading is the cause, visitors who know their photos will vanish cannot rely on the camera, so they should remember more, like non-photographers. Finding that they remembered just as little as those keeping their photos means the impairment occurs even without any expectation that the camera will remember, which weakens the hypothesis. Keepers recalling less than deleters is what the hypothesis predicts (support). Enjoyment is irrelevant, and a lasting impairment does not distinguish offloading from other explanations.","th":"หากสาเหตุคือการฝากความจำไว้กับกล้อง ผู้ที่รู้ว่าภาพจะถูกลบทันทีพึ่งกล้องไม่ได้ จึงควรจำได้มากขึ้นเหมือนผู้ที่ไม่ถ่ายภาพ ถ้าพบว่าพวกเขาจำได้น้อยเท่ากับผู้ที่เก็บภาพไว้ แปลว่าการจำได้น้อยเกิดขึ้นแม้ไม่ได้คาดหวังว่ากล้องจะจำแทน จึงทำให้สมมติฐานอ่อนลง การที่ผู้เก็บภาพจำได้น้อยกว่าผู้ที่ภาพถูกลบตรงกับสมมติฐาน (สนับสนุน) ความเพลิดเพลินไม่เกี่ยวข้อง และผลที่คงอยู่หนึ่งสัปดาห์ไม่ได้แยกการฝากความจำออกจากคำอธิบายอื่น"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Developmental psychology","th":"จิตวิทยาพัฒนาการ"},"passage":"In the original \"marshmallow test\" studies of the 1960s and 1970s, children who waited longer for a second treat tended to have higher test scores as adolescents. In 2018, a team of researchers repeated the study with a much larger and more diverse sample of children. The ______ conclusion was that the link between a young child's ability to delay gratification and later academic achievement, while real, was considerably weaker than the original studies had suggested.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["researcher's","researchers","researchers's","researchers'"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion belongs to several researchers (the team), so the plural possessive 'researchers'' is needed. 'Researcher's' is singular possessive (one researcher), 'researchers' is a plain plural with no possession, and 'researchers's' is not a standard form.","th":"ข้อสรุปเป็นของนักวิจัยหลายคน (ทั้งทีม) จึงต้องใช้รูปแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของพหูพจน์ researchers' ส่วน researcher's เป็นเจ้าของเอกพจน์ (นักวิจัยคนเดียว) researchers เป็นพหูพจน์ที่ไม่แสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ และ researchers's ไม่ใช่รูปมาตรฐาน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"History of medicine","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์การแพทย์"},"passage":"Long before Edward Jenner developed his smallpox vaccine in 1796, practitioners in China, India, the Ottoman Empire, and parts of Africa practiced variolation, deliberately exposing healthy people to material taken from smallpox sores. The procedure carried real risks: a small proportion of variolated patients developed severe smallpox and died, and they could spread the disease to others. ______ death rates among people who were variolated were far lower than among people who caught smallpox naturally, which explains why the practice persisted for centuries.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Likewise,","Even so,","In other words,","As a result,"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The previous sentence lists the dangers of variolation; the next sentence gives a contrasting point (it was still far safer than natural infection). 'Even so' signals that contrast. 'Likewise' would introduce a similar point, 'In other words' a restatement of the risks, and 'As a result' would absurdly claim that the risks caused the lower death rates.","th":"ประโยคก่อนหน้าบอกอันตรายของการปลูกฝีแบบโบราณ ประโยคถัดไปเสนอประเด็นตรงข้าม คือยังปลอดภัยกว่าการติดเชื้อเองมาก จึงใช้ Even so ส่วน Likewise ใช้เสนอประเด็นที่คล้ายกัน In other words ใช้กล่าวซ้ำด้วยคำอื่น และ As a result จะสื่อผิดว่าความเสี่ยงทำให้อัตราการตายต่ำลง"}}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":1,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 1 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 1 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":true,"file":"ielts-1","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Archives in the ice","topic":{"en":"Climate science and glaciology","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์ภูมิอากาศและธารน้ำแข็ง"},"difficulty":1,"labelled":true,"paragraphs":["The ice sheets of Antarctica and Greenland are the most detailed archives of the Earth's past climate that scientists possess. Each year's snowfall is buried by the next, and over millennia the accumulated layers are compressed into ice that may be more than three kilometres thick. Locked inside are traces of the atmosphere and dust of the time when the snow fell. The longest continuous record recovered so far reaches back roughly 800,000 years, and several international teams are now drilling in the hope of extending it to well over a million.","Snow does not become ice overnight. Freshly fallen snow is mostly air. As it is buried, the crystals are rounded and packed more tightly into a granular material known as firn, through which air can still move between the grains. Only at a depth of somewhere between 50 and 100 metres do the pores finally close, sealing small samples of air into bubbles. One consequence is easily overlooked: because air continues to circulate through the firn for decades or centuries after the snow itself has fallen, the gas in any bubble is younger than the ice that surrounds it. At sites where little snow accumulates, the difference can amount to several thousand years, and it must be calculated with care before the records of gas and of ice can be compared.","Drilling teams generally select a dome or a ridge on the ice sheet, where there is minimal horizontal flow, so that the layers lie undisturbed in the order in which they were laid down. A hollow drill extracts a cylinder of ice, which is raised in sections of around three metres and stored at minus 20 degrees Celsius or colder. A particular difficulty arises at intermediate depths, typically between about 600 and 1,200 metres. Here the bubbles are under great pressure, and when the ice is brought up and the confining weight above it is removed, it tends to crack and even shatter. Ice from this so-called brittle zone is therefore commonly left to rest in a storage trench beneath the surface, sometimes for a whole year, while the pressure slowly equalises before it is cut.","Establishing the age of each layer is the next challenge. In the upper part of a core, annual layers can be counted much like tree rings, using visible bands as well as chemical signals that peak in a particular season. Deeper down, however, the weight of the overlying ice stretches the layers so thin that they can no longer be distinguished, and researchers turn instead to computer models of how the ice has flowed. These models are anchored by fixed points such as layers of volcanic ash or sulphate from eruptions whose dates are known independently. Very large eruptions deposit material in both hemispheres, which allows cores from Greenland and Antarctica to be placed on a common timescale.","Past temperatures can be read from the water itself. Molecules containing heavier isotopes of oxygen and hydrogen condense more readily than lighter ones, so the colder the air, the smaller the share of heavy molecules that reaches the polar snow. By measuring this ratio, scientists can estimate the temperature at which the snow formed. The method has pitfalls. The relationship between the ratio and temperature is usually calibrated using differences between places today, and there is no guarantee that it held in the past. If, for instance, a greater proportion of the year's snow fell in summer during some earlier period, the record would suggest a warmer climate than actually existed. An independent check comes from the borehole itself: the ice retains a faint memory of past surface temperatures, which can be measured with sensitive thermometers lowered down the hole. In central Greenland, such measurements indicated that the coldest part of the last glacial period had been substantially colder than the isotopes alone had implied.","Greenhouse gases are measured as well. When samples are crushed in a vacuum, the air released from the bubbles can be analysed directly. Over the past 800,000 years, the concentration of carbon dioxide rose and fell between roughly 180 parts per million in glacial periods and about 280 in warm intervals; today it exceeds 420. Antarctic ice is preferred for these measurements. Greenland ice contains more dust and other impurities, and chemical reactions between them can generate additional carbon dioxide inside the ice, distorting the record, although Greenland cores remain invaluable for methane and for precise dating.","The search is now on for ice that is 1.5 million years old or more. Such a record would cover a puzzling period about a million years ago when the rhythm of ice ages changed from cycles of roughly 41,000 years to cycles of around 100,000 years. At the base of the thickest ice sheets, heat escaping from the rock below can melt the oldest layers away. Candidate sites are identified through airborne radar surveys that reveal the internal structure of the ice and the temperature conditions at its base. Even where ancient ice survives, the deepest layers are so compressed that a single metre may contain more than 10,000 years."],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"text":"The air sealed into a layer of ice is of the same age as the frozen snow that encloses it.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Sections of core from intermediate depths are usually cut up as soon as they reach the surface.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Volcanic material is found more frequently in Greenland cores than in Antarctic ones.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"A change in the season when most snow falls could make a past climate appear warmer than it was.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"Temperature measurements made in a borehole suggested that the last ice age had been less severe than isotope evidence indicated.","answer":["FALSE"]}]},{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G. NB You may use any letter more than once.","questions":[{"text":"a way of testing an estimate by using a separate source of evidence","answer":["E"]},{"text":"a means of matching records taken from opposite ends of the globe","answer":["D"]},{"text":"the reason why some ice is not processed immediately after it is drilled","answer":["C"]},{"text":"an assumption about the present that may not apply to the distant past","answer":["E"]}]},{"type":"flowchart-completion","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","wordLimit":{"words":2,"number":false},"context":"From snowfall to dated sample\n\nFresh snow is buried by later snowfall\n↓\nGrains become rounder and are packed into (10) ______, through which air can still circulate\n↓\nAt 50–100 metres the pores close and air is trapped in (11) ______\n↓\nDrilling takes place on a dome or ridge where there is little (12) ______, so the layers stay in order\n↓\nIn the upper core, (13) ______ are counted; deeper down, flow models are fixed to dated eruptions","questions":[{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["firn"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["bubbles"]},{"text":"(12) ______","answer":["horizontal flow"]},{"text":"(13) ______","answer":["annual layers"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below shows the average number of hours per week that boys and girls aged 13–17 in one country spent on five activities outside school hours in 2010 and 2025.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Average hours per week spent on activities outside school, ages 13–17","rows":[["Activity","Boys 2010","Girls 2010","Boys 2025","Girls 2025"],["Social media and messaging","4.5","6.0","13.0","17.5"],["Homework and private study","7.0","8.5","6.5","8.0"],["Sport and exercise","6.5","4.0","4.5","3.0"],["Reading for pleasure","3.0","4.5","1.5","2.5"],["Paid part-time work","2.5","2.0","1.0","1.5"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"opinion","prompt":"Now that artificial intelligence tools can produce fluent essays in seconds, some educators argue that schools should stop setting written homework altogether and assess students' writing only under supervision in the classroom.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":2,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 2 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 2 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"ielts-2","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Frozen assets: the rise and fall of the ice trade","difficulty":2,"topic":{"en":"Economic history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์เศรษฐกิจ"},"paragraphs":["Before the age of electricity, keeping anything cold in a hot climate was a luxury reserved for the very wealthy. Rulers in Persia, China and the Mediterranean had stored winter snow in pits and thick-walled buildings for centuries, but the quantities were small and the cold rarely travelled far. That changed in the nineteenth century, when an unlikely industry grew up around a resource that seemed, to most people, entirely worthless: the ice that formed each winter on the lakes and ponds of New England. Within a few decades, frozen water cut from those ponds was being sold in the Caribbean, in South America and as far away as India.","The industry is usually traced to Frederic Tudor, a young Boston merchant who in 1806 sent a ship loaded with ice to the Caribbean island of Martinique. His contemporaries thought the plan absurd, and the first voyages appeared to prove them right. Much of the cargo melted before it could be sold, and the islanders who received the rest had little idea what to do with it. Tudor's difficulty, however, was not the crossing itself; ice packed tightly in a ship's hold melts far more slowly than most people expected. The real problem lay at the destination, where there was nowhere to keep the ice once it had been unloaded. Only after years of heavy losses, including a spell in prison for debt, did he begin to build insulated ice houses in the ports he supplied.","Two developments turned this fragile business into a profitable one. The first was the choice of insulation. Early shippers had experimented with straw, hay and rice husks, but sawdust proved the most effective, and it had the additional merit of being almost free, since the timber mills of Maine produced it in enormous quantities as waste. The second was the mechanisation of harvesting. Cutting ice by hand with axes and saws produced blocks of uneven size that wasted space in storage and in transit. A horse-drawn cutter, introduced by one of Tudor's suppliers, Nathaniel Wyeth, scored the frozen surface of a pond in a regular grid, so that workers could split it into identical blocks that could be stacked with almost no gaps between them. Tightly stacked blocks melted more slowly, because less of their surface was exposed to warm air.","With these improvements, the trade expanded rapidly. In 1833 a cargo of ice left Boston for Calcutta, a voyage of around four months that took the ship twice across the equator; more than half of the ice was still frozen when it arrived. By the middle of the century, ships were carrying ice to ports throughout the tropics, and the ponds around Boston had become so valuable that the rights to harvest them were bought and sold like farmland. The writer Henry David Thoreau, who lived for two years beside Walden Pond, recorded watching a large team of labourers remove ice from its surface one winter, and reflected that the water he drank might one day cool a glass in India.","Yet the largest market for natural ice was never overseas. As cities in the United States grew, so did the demand for ice at home. Households bought it to fill wooden iceboxes, in which food could be kept fresh for several days, while brewers needed it to produce lager, a style of beer that must be fermented at low temperatures. Meat-packers in the Midwest packed ice into railway wagons to send fresh beef to the cities of the east coast, an innovation that changed the national diet. In Europe a similar pattern emerged, although most of the supply came not from America but from Norway, whose lakes provided ice that was shipped across the North Sea to British ports in huge quantities.","The success of the industry concealed two weaknesses. The first was its dependence on the weather. A mild winter could leave the ponds too thin to cut, and in such years the price of ice rose sharply; the shortages that followed were known as ice famines. The second weakness was purity. As towns grew along rivers and lakes, the water that froze into ice was increasingly polluted by sewage and industrial waste, and doctors began to warn that the same ice that preserved food could also spread disease. Public confidence, once lost, proved hard to restore.","These problems created an opening for a competitor. Machines that produced ice by compressing and expanding gases had existed since the middle of the century, but they had been expensive and unreliable. As the technology improved, factory-made ice, known in the trade as plant ice, became cheaper; made from filtered water, it could be produced in any season and in any town, close to the customers who needed it. The arrival of affordable electric refrigerators in private homes during the 1920s and 1930s removed the need for regular ice deliveries, and an industry that had once employed tens of thousands of people faded almost entirely from public memory."],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.\n\nQuestions 1–5","questions":[{"text":"Tudor's early ventures failed chiefly because the ice melted quickly during the sea voyage.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"One reason for using sawdust as insulation was its low cost.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"Blocks of ice cut by hand were generally heavier than those cut with Wyeth's equipment.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"European demand for ice was met largely by imports from the United States.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Warnings from doctors led to a ban on selling ice taken from polluted rivers.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]}]},{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G.\n\nQuestions 6–8","questions":[{"text":"a contrast between the assumed cause of a commercial failure and its actual cause","answer":["B"]},{"text":"a reference to methods of preserving cold that existed long before the ice trade","answer":["A"]},{"text":"an explanation of why a rival product was more convenient for buyers","answer":["G"]}]},{"type":"note-completion","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.\n\nQuestions 9–11","wordLimit":{"words":1,"number":false},"context":"Uses of natural ice within the United States\n• Households: kept food fresh in (9) ______\n• Brewers: needed low temperatures to make (10) ______\n• Meat-packers: sent beef east in railway (11) ______","questions":[{"text":"(9) ______","answer":["iceboxes"]},{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["lager"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["wagons"]}]},{"type":"short-answer","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.\n\nQuestions 12–13","wordLimit":{"words":2,"number":false},"questions":[{"text":"What name was given to the shortages that followed unusually warm winters?","answer":["ice famines"]},{"text":"What term did the industry use for ice manufactured by machines?","answer":["plant ice"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below gives information about public transport in five cities in 2024.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Public transport in five cities, 2024","rows":[["City","Population (millions)","Rail/metro network (km)","Public transport trips per day (millions)","Commuters using public transport (%)","Single fare (US$)"],["Halvern","8.4","410","9.8","63","1.80"],["Marlow Bay","6.9","40","1.7","21","0.60"],["Corrin","5.2","95","2.9","38","0.90"],["Tessaly","3.6","210","3.1","55","2.60"],["Velden","1.8","0","0.9","34","1.20"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"two-part","prompt":"In many countries, people are living longer than ever before, yet a growing share of those extra years is spent in poor health.\n\nWhy is this happening? What can individuals and governments do to make sure that longer lives are also healthier ones?\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":3,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 3 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 3 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"ielts-3","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Lessons from Roman concrete","difficulty":2,"labelled":true,"topic":{"en":"Materials science and engineering","th":"วัสดุศาสตร์และวิศวกรรม"},"paragraphs":["Concrete is the most widely used manufactured material on Earth; after water, it is often said to be the substance that people consume in the greatest quantity. Its essential ingredient, cement, is made by heating limestone and clay to around 1,450°C in a kiln, a process that releases carbon dioxide both from the fuel that is burnt and from the limestone itself as it breaks down. Cement manufacture is estimated to account for roughly 8 per cent of global carbon dioxide emissions. Modern reinforced concrete is also surprisingly short-lived: many bridges and buildings from the twentieth century required major repairs within fifty to a hundred years. It is a striking irony, then, that some structures built by the Romans two thousand years ago are still standing, and that a few appear to be stronger now than when they were completed.","Roman builders did not use Portland cement, which was patented in England only in 1824. Instead, they combined lime, produced by heating limestone at lower temperatures than modern kilns reach, with volcanic ash, the finest of which came from deposits around the Bay of Naples. The ash, rich in silica and alumina, reacted with the lime and water to form a binding paste. Writers of the period describe how this mixture was packed down together with chunks of rock or broken brick, rather than poured as a liquid in the way that modern concrete is. The result was denser than might be expected, and its chemistry differed fundamentally from that of the modern material.","The most remarkable examples are the harbour walls and piers that Roman engineers built directly in the sea. For modern concrete, seawater is an enemy: salt penetrates the material and corrodes the steel bars inside, and as the steel rusts and swells, the surrounding concrete cracks. Roman marine concrete contains no steel, and analysis of cores drilled from ancient harbours has revealed something more surprising still. As seawater seeped through the structures over centuries, it dissolved part of the volcanic material and allowed rare minerals to crystallise in the gaps, among them a plate-shaped mineral called aluminous tobermorite. These interlocking crystals appear to have reinforced the material from within, so that contact with the sea, rather than weakening it, gradually made it more cohesive.","A second puzzle concerned the small white lumps of lime scattered through many samples of Roman concrete. Researchers had long dismissed these 'lime clasts' as signs of careless mixing or poor raw materials. More recent studies have proposed another explanation: that the Romans mixed their concrete 'hot', adding quicklime directly instead of first combining it with water. The intense heat of this reaction would have left behind lumps of brittle, calcium-rich material. When a crack later formed and water entered it, the lumps could dissolve and recrystallise as calcium carbonate, sealing the crack. To test the idea, one team made new samples using the hot-mixing method, cracked them deliberately and ran water through them. Within about two weeks the cracks had closed, whereas in otherwise identical samples made without quicklime the water continued to flow.","It would be wrong, however, to conclude that Roman concrete is simply superior. It is considerably weaker in compression than its modern equivalent and takes far longer to reach its full strength, which would be unacceptable on a modern building site, where the timber or metal moulds must be removed and work on the next floor begun within days. Nor could it be used for the slender beams and tall towers of contemporary architecture, which depend on the steel reinforcement that Roman builders did without. Moreover, suitable volcanic ash occurs only in certain regions, and supplying it in the quantities that today's construction industry consumes would be impractical.","What engineers hope to borrow, therefore, is not the Roman recipe but some of its principles. One line of research adds volcanic ash or industrial by-products such as fly ash from power stations to modern mixes, reducing the amount of cement required and, with it, carbon emissions. Another seeks to reproduce the self-healing effect by other means. Some experimental concretes contain dormant bacteria, packed with a food source in tiny capsules; when a crack lets in water, the bacteria become active and produce limestone that fills the gap. Others contain capsules of a liquid healing agent that burst open when the concrete around them cracks.","Whether such materials are adopted widely will depend less on chemistry than on economics and regulation. Self-healing concretes currently cost considerably more than conventional mixes, and building codes, which are based on decades of experience with Portland cement, are slow to accept materials whose long-term behaviour is unknown. Supporters argue that the higher initial price should be weighed against the cost of repairs over a structure's whole life, which can exceed the cost of building it in the first place. If they are right, the most valuable lesson of Roman concrete may be one of perspective: a material should be judged not only by how quickly it can be built with, but by how long it lasts."],"groups":[{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G.","questions":[{"text":"the date when the modern type of cement was first given legal protection","answer":["B"]},{"text":"a feature of the material that was once taken as evidence of poor workmanship","answer":["D"]},{"text":"a reason why the ancient material would delay modern construction projects","answer":["E"]}]},{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"text":"The carbon dioxide released in making cement comes only from the fuel burnt in the kilns.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Roman writers recorded the exact proportions of lime and ash used in their concrete.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"In Roman harbour structures, new minerals formed as a result of seawater passing through the concrete.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"The hot-mixing experiment was carried out on samples taken from ancient buildings.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Concrete containing capsules of healing agent was developed after concrete containing bacteria.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]}]},{"type":"table-completion","instructions":"Complete the table below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","wordLimit":{"words":1,"number":false},"context":"Roman and modern concrete compared\nFeature | Roman concrete | Modern concrete\nBinder | lime combined with volcanic (9) ______ | Portland cement\nHow it is placed | packed down with chunks of rock or brick | poured as a liquid\nEffect of seawater | crystals strengthen it from within | salt attacks the steel (10) ______ inside\nMain drawbacks | slow to reach full strength; weaker in (11) ______ | short-lived; high carbon emissions","questions":[{"text":"(9) ______","answer":["ash"]},{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["bars"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["compression"]}]},{"type":"mcq-multi","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"text":"Which TWO ways of improving modern concrete are being investigated, according to the passage?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"replacing part of the cement with volcanic ash or industrial waste"},{"key":"B","text":"removing steel reinforcement from tall buildings"},{"key":"C","text":"heating limestone to higher temperatures in the kiln"},{"key":"D","text":"importing volcanic ash from the Bay of Naples"},{"key":"E","text":"adding inactive bacteria that produce limestone when water gets in"}],"answer":["A","E"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below gives information about the use of public libraries in one country in 2005, 2015 and 2025.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Public libraries in one country, 2005–2025","rows":[["","2005","2015","2025"],["Number of libraries","4,620","4,130","3,580"],["Visits (millions)","312","268","241"],["Printed books borrowed (millions)","405","290","176"],["E-books and audiobooks borrowed (millions)","0.4","38","121"],["Average opening hours per week","46","41","43"],["Events held (thousands)","58","84","131"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"discussion","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should fund scientific research only when it is likely to produce practical benefits, such as new medicines or technologies. Others believe that research driven purely by curiosity deserves equal public support, even if it has no obvious use.\n\nDiscuss both these views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"gate","set":1,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":true,"file":"gate-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to a question about a seat","th":"การตอบคำถามเรื่องที่นั่งว่าง"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Excuse me, is this seat free?\"\nB: \"______\"","choices":["No, I'm not free today.","Yes, it's very expensive.","Sorry, my friend is sitting there.","You're welcome."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Here \"free\" means \"not being used\". \"Sorry, my friend is sitting there.\" politely says the seat is taken. \"It's very expensive\" mixes up free (no cost) with free (empty), and \"I'm not free today\" talks about the speaker's time.","th":"คำว่า free ในที่นี้หมายถึง \"ว่าง ไม่มีคนนั่ง\" คำตอบ \"Sorry, my friend is sitting there.\" บอกอย่างสุภาพว่ามีคนนั่งแล้ว ส่วน \"It's very expensive\" สับสนกับความหมาย \"ฟรี ไม่เสียเงิน\" และ \"I'm not free today\" พูดถึงเวลาว่างของผู้พูด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Giving an opinion","th":"การแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"What do you think of the new school canteen?\"\nB: \"______ The food is much better than last year's.\"","choices":["It's up to you.","I don't think so.","I'm really impressed.","I'd rather not."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B gives a positive opinion supported by \"The food is much better\". \"I'm really impressed.\" fits. \"I don't think so.\" answers a yes/no question, \"It's up to you.\" lets someone else decide, and \"I'd rather not.\" refuses an offer.","th":"B แสดงความเห็นในทางบวก และให้เหตุผลว่าอาหารดีขึ้นมาก \"I'm really impressed.\" (ประทับใจมาก) จึงเหมาะ ส่วน \"I don't think so.\" ใช้ตอบคำถามใช่/ไม่ใช่ \"It's up to you.\" ให้อีกฝ่ายตัดสินใจ และ \"I'd rather not.\" เป็นการปฏิเสธ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing an adjective that fits the context","th":"การเลือกคำคุณศัพท์ให้เข้ากับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Engineers closed the old bridge because it was no longer ______ enough to carry heavy trucks safely.\"","choices":["fragile","sturdy","flexible","hollow"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Sturdy\" means strong and solid. The bridge was closed because it was no longer strong enough. \"Fragile\" (easily broken) is the opposite meaning, and \"hollow\" and \"flexible\" do not describe the ability to carry heavy loads.","th":"\"sturdy\" แปลว่า แข็งแรง มั่นคง สะพานถูกปิดเพราะไม่แข็งแรงพอจะรับรถบรรทุก ส่วน \"fragile\" (เปราะ แตกง่าย) มีความหมายตรงข้าม \"hollow\" (กลวง) และ \"flexible\" (ยืดหยุ่น) ไม่ได้บอกความสามารถในการรับน้ำหนัก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Make or do collocations","th":"การใช้ make และ do ให้ถูกคู่คำ"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My little brother always ______ a lot of noise when he plays video games.\"","choices":["takes","gives","does","makes"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"In English we say \"make a noise\". \"Do\" goes with homework or exercise, \"take\" with a photo or a break, and \"give\" with advice or a speech.","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษใช้ \"make a noise\" (ส่งเสียงดัง) ส่วน \"do\" ใช้กับการบ้านหรือการออกกำลังกาย \"take\" ใช้กับถ่ายรูปหรือพัก และ \"give\" ใช้กับคำแนะนำหรือการกล่าวสุนทรพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present continuous for actions happening now","th":"present continuous สำหรับเหตุการณ์ที่กำลังเกิดขึ้น"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Look! The children ______ in the river.\"","choices":["swam","swim","have swum","are swimming"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Look!\" shows the action is happening now, so we use the present continuous: \"are swimming\". \"Swim\" is for habits and \"swam\" is past.","th":"\"Look!\" แสดงว่ากำลังเกิดขึ้นขณะพูด จึงใช้ present continuous คือ \"are swimming\" ส่วน \"swim\" ใช้กับกิจวัตร และ \"swam\" เป็นอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Future passive","th":"passive voice ในอนาคต"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The new sports hall ______ by the mayor next Monday.\"","choices":["will open","will be opened","is opening","opened"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The hall does not open itself; the mayor opens it (\"by the mayor\"), and \"next Monday\" is future. So we need the future passive: \"will be opened\".","th":"ห้องกีฬาไม่ได้เปิดเอง แต่ถูกนายกเทศมนตรีเปิด (\"by the mayor\") และ \"next Monday\" เป็นอนาคต จึงใช้ passive ในอนาคตคือ \"will be opened\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement in the present simple","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยาใน present simple"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every morning my sister [[go]] to school [[by]] bus with [[her]] best friend, who [[lives]] next door.","choices":["go","by","her","lives"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"My sister\" is third person singular, so the verb needs -es: \"goes\", not \"go\". \"By bus\", \"her\" and \"lives\" are correct.","th":"\"My sister\" เป็นประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 กริยาต้องเติม -es เป็น \"goes\" ไม่ใช่ \"go\" ส่วน \"by bus\", \"her\" และ \"lives\" ถูกแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"No will in an if-clause","th":"ไม่ใช้ will ในอนุประโยค if"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: You [[will]] pass the test [[easily]] if you [[will study]] hard [[enough]].","choices":["will","easily","will study","enough"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"In a first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple: \"if you study\", not \"if you will study\". \"Will pass\" in the main clause is correct.","th":"ในเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1 อนุประโยค if ใช้ present simple คือ \"if you study\" ไม่ใช่ \"if you will study\" ส่วน \"will pass\" ในประโยคหลักถูกแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a verb that fits the meaning","th":"การเลือกคำกริยาให้เข้ากับความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["promise","produce","provide","protect"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Roots that stand against powerful waves keep the shore safe: they \"protect the shore from\" waves. \"Protect … from\" is a fixed pattern. \"Produce\", \"provide\" and \"promise\" do not fit the meaning.","th":"รากที่ต้านคลื่นแรงช่วยปกป้องชายฝั่ง ใช้ \"protect the shore from\" (ปกป้อง…จาก) ซึ่งเป็นโครงสร้างคงที่ ส่วน \"produce\" (ผลิต) \"provide\" (จัดหาให้) และ \"promise\" (สัญญา) ไม่เข้ากับความหมาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Adjective–noun collocation","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ที่ใช้คู่กับคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["wide","thick","tall","intense"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Strong heat is described as \"intense heat\". \"Thick\", \"tall\" and \"wide\" describe size or shape and do not go with \"heat\".","th":"ความร้อนจัดใช้ว่า \"intense heat\" ส่วน \"thick\" (หนา) \"tall\" (สูง) และ \"wide\" (กว้าง) ใช้บอกขนาดหรือรูปร่าง ไม่ใช้กับ heat"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the purpose of a notice","th":"การระบุจุดประสงค์ของประกาศ"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"What is the main purpose of this notice?","choices":["To tell people that the library will close for Songkran","To advertise a weekend science camp at Khao Yai","To invite young readers to join a reading challenge and explain how it works","To ask local artists to design tote bags for the library"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Most of the notice explains who can join the Summer Reading Challenge, how to collect stamps and what the prizes are. The camp, the Songkran closure and the tote bags are only details.","th":"ประกาศส่วนใหญ่อธิบายว่าใครเข้าร่วมกิจกรรมได้ สะสมตราประทับอย่างไร และมีรางวัลอะไร จุดประสงค์หลักจึงเป็นการเชิญชวนนักอ่านรุ่นเยาว์ให้เข้าร่วมกิจกรรมการอ่าน ส่วนค่ายวิทยาศาสตร์ การปิดช่วงสงกรานต์ และกระเป๋าผ้าเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Understanding a phrase from its explanation","th":"การเข้าใจวลีจากคำอธิบายในบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"In paragraph 4, a \"vicious circle\" is","choices":["a situation in which one problem causes another that makes the first problem worse","a group of students who are easily annoyed","a study timetable that repeats every week","a period of deep sleep early in the night"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The writer explains it after the colon: tiredness makes a student slow, so he falls behind and stays up later, which makes him even more tired. Each problem feeds the next one.","th":"ผู้เขียนอธิบายไว้หลังเครื่องหมายทวิภาค คือ ความเหนื่อยทำให้ทำงานช้า ตามไม่ทัน ต้องนอนดึกขึ้น แล้วก็ยิ่งเหนื่อยกว่าเดิม ปัญหาหนึ่งทำให้อีกปัญหาแย่ลงวนเป็นวงจร"}}]}},{"exam":"gate","set":2,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"gate-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Answering 'Do you mind if …?'","th":"การตอบคำถาม Do you mind if …?"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Do you mind if I sit here? The other seats are taken.\"\nB: \"______\"","choices":["Not at all. Go ahead.","Yes, please sit down.","No, I don't sit here.","Sure, I mind."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Do you mind …?\" asks whether something would bother you, so \"Not at all. Go ahead.\" means \"it doesn't bother me, please sit\". \"Yes, please sit down.\" is a common trap: \"yes\" here would mean \"I do mind\".","th":"\"Do you mind …?\" ถามว่ารังเกียจไหม การอนุญาตจึงต้องตอบว่า \"Not at all. Go ahead.\" (ไม่รังเกียจเลย เชิญเลย) ส่วน \"Yes, please sit down.\" เป็นกับดัก เพราะ yes ในที่นี้แปลว่า \"รังเกียจ\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Answering a How often question","th":"การตอบคำถาม How often"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"How often do you go to the gym?\"\nB: \"______\"","choices":["About three times a week.","Since last year.","For two hours.","By bike."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"How often\" asks about frequency, so \"About three times a week.\" is correct. \"For two hours\" answers \"How long\", \"Since last year\" answers \"How long have you…\", and \"By bike\" answers \"How do you get there\".","th":"\"How often\" ถามความถี่ จึงตอบว่า \"About three times a week.\" ส่วน \"For two hours\" ตอบ How long \"Since last year\" ตอบ How long have you… และ \"By bike\" ตอบว่าไปอย่างไร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Collocation: do someone a favour","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กัน: do someone a favour"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Could you ______ me a favour and hold the door open for a moment?\"","choices":["make","give","do","take"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The fixed expression is \"do someone a favour\" (help someone). \"Make\", \"give\" and \"take\" do not go with \"a favour\" in this meaning.","th":"สำนวนคงที่คือ \"do someone a favour\" (ช่วยเหลือ) ส่วน \"make\", \"give\" และ \"take\" ไม่ใช้คู่กับ a favour ในความหมายนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Three-word phrasal verb run out of","th":"กริยาวลี run out of"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"We've ______ sugar, so I can't bake the cake until Mum goes shopping.\"","choices":["run over","run into","run out of","run away from"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Run out of\" means to have none left, which explains why the cake cannot be baked. \"Run into\" means meet by chance, \"run over\" means hit with a vehicle, and \"run away from\" means escape.","th":"\"run out of\" แปลว่า หมด จึงอบเค้กไม่ได้ ส่วน \"run into\" แปลว่า บังเอิญพบ \"run over\" แปลว่า ขับรถทับ และ \"run away from\" แปลว่า หนีจาก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"How much with uncountable nouns","th":"การใช้ How much กับนามนับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"How ______ sugar would you like in your tea?\"","choices":["few","many","much","lot"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Sugar\" is uncountable, so we ask \"How much\". \"How many\" is for countable nouns, and \"How lot\" and \"How few\" are not used in this question.","th":"\"sugar\" เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ จึงถามว่า \"How much\" ส่วน \"How many\" ใช้กับนามนับได้ และ \"How lot\" กับ \"How few\" ไม่ใช้ในคำถามนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Would rather + past simple","th":"would rather + กริยาช่องที่ 2"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I'd rather you ______ the window. It's freezing in here.\"","choices":["won't open","didn't open","don't open","not open"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"When \"would rather\" is followed by another person (\"you\"), the verb goes into the past simple even though the meaning is present: \"I'd rather you didn't open\".","th":"เมื่อ \"would rather\" ตามด้วยบุคคลอื่น (\"you\") กริยาต้องเป็นรูปอดีต แม้ความหมายเป็นปัจจุบัน คือ \"I'd rather you didn't open\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with do/does","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับ do/does"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My grandmother [[don't]] like [[spicy]] food, so we [[usually]] cook [[something]] mild for her.","choices":["don't","spicy","usually","something"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"My grandmother\" is third person singular, so the negative is \"doesn't like\", not \"don't like\".","th":"\"My grandmother\" เป็นประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 รูปปฏิเสธต้องเป็น \"doesn't like\" ไม่ใช่ \"don't like\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Forget + to-infinitive","th":"forget + to + กริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Each of the students [[has]] to bring [[their]] own pencil, [[but]] Tom forgot [[bring]] his.","choices":["has","their","but","bring"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Forget\" is followed by \"to\" + verb when we mean not doing a task: \"forgot to bring\". \"Each … has\" is correct because \"each\" is singular.","th":"\"forget\" ต้องตามด้วย to + กริยา เมื่อหมายถึงลืมทำสิ่งใด คือ \"forgot to bring\" ส่วน \"Each … has\" ถูกแล้ว เพราะ each เป็นเอกพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Linking phrase: above all","th":"วลีเชื่อม above all"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nWhen our school decided to turn the empty roof of the library into a vegetable garden, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot and windy and, (1) ______, nobody knew whether it could carry the weight of wet soil. Before a single seed was planted, an engineer was invited to inspect the building. She reported that the roof was strong enough, (2) ______ the soil was kept in light plastic boxes rather than heavy concrete beds.\n\nThe first year was not a success. Half of the seedlings dried out during the April holidays because no one (3) ______ to water them. (4) ______ giving up, the students designed a simple system: old water bottles, each with a tiny hole in its cap, were pushed upside down into the soil so that water dripped out slowly over several days.\n\nBy the second year the garden was producing more basil, chillies and morning glory than the canteen could use. The extra vegetables were sold to parents every Friday, and the money (5) ______ was spent on more seeds and boxes. The students also kept a careful (6) ______ of how much water each box needed, and their results were later shown at a provincial science fair.\n\nWhat began as an experiment that few people believed in has become one of the most (7) ______ places in the school. There is now a waiting list of classes who want to hold their science lessons on the roof, and the head teacher says she wishes she (8) ______ the idea years ago.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["above all","at all","in all","after all"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The writer lists problems (hot, windy) and then the most important one (the weight of soil). \"Above all\" means \"most importantly\". \"After all\" gives a reason, \"at all\" is used in negatives and questions, and \"in all\" means \"in total\".","th":"ผู้เขียนไล่ปัญหา (ร้อน ลมแรง) แล้วตามด้วยปัญหาสำคัญที่สุด (น้ำหนักดิน) \"above all\" แปลว่า ที่สำคัญที่สุด ส่วน \"after all\" ใช้ให้เหตุผล \"at all\" ใช้ในประโยคปฏิเสธหรือคำถาม และ \"in all\" แปลว่า รวมทั้งหมด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Reduced relative clause","th":"อนุประโยคขยายนามแบบละ that/which"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nWhen our school decided to turn the empty roof of the library into a vegetable garden, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot and windy and, (1) ______, nobody knew whether it could carry the weight of wet soil. Before a single seed was planted, an engineer was invited to inspect the building. She reported that the roof was strong enough, (2) ______ the soil was kept in light plastic boxes rather than heavy concrete beds.\n\nThe first year was not a success. Half of the seedlings dried out during the April holidays because no one (3) ______ to water them. (4) ______ giving up, the students designed a simple system: old water bottles, each with a tiny hole in its cap, were pushed upside down into the soil so that water dripped out slowly over several days.\n\nBy the second year the garden was producing more basil, chillies and morning glory than the canteen could use. The extra vegetables were sold to parents every Friday, and the money (5) ______ was spent on more seeds and boxes. The students also kept a careful (6) ______ of how much water each box needed, and their results were later shown at a provincial science fair.\n\nWhat began as an experiment that few people believed in has become one of the most (7) ______ places in the school. There is now a waiting list of classes who want to hold their science lessons on the roof, and the head teacher says she wishes she (8) ______ the idea years ago.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["was raised","raised them","they raised","raising them"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"The money (that) they raised\" is a relative clause with \"that\" left out, and \"was spent\" is the main verb. \"Was raised\" would give two main verbs in a row (\"was raised was spent\"), and \"raising them\" and \"raised them\" add an object that does not belong.","th":"\"the money (that) they raised\" เป็นอนุประโยคขยายนามที่ละ that ไว้ และ \"was spent\" เป็นกริยาหลัก ส่วน \"was raised\" จะทำให้มีกริยาหลักซ้อนกันสองตัว (\"was raised was spent\") และ \"raising them\" กับ \"raised them\" มีกรรมเกินมาโดยไม่จำเป็น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Finding a reason stated in the text","th":"การหาเหตุผลที่ระบุไว้ในบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"From: Ms Darin (Grade 6 Coordinator)\nTo: Grade 6 parents and students\nSubject: Change to the Grade 6 field trip – please read\n\nDear parents and students,\n\nAs many of you know, our Grade 6 field trip to the Mae Klong river was planned for Friday 14 November. Unfortunately, the boat company we booked has told us that two of its boats need urgent repairs and will not be ready in time. Rather than squeeze everyone onto the remaining boats, which would not be safe, we have decided to change the plan.\n\nThe trip will now take place one week later, on Friday 21 November. The timetable stays the same: the coach leaves school at 7.15 a.m. sharp and returns at about 5.30 p.m. Lunch is still included, but please note that the floating market visit has been replaced by a visit to a coconut sugar farm, because the market is closed on that day for a local festival.\n\nIf your child cannot attend on the new date, please let me know by Monday 10 November. The full fee of 650 baht will be returned, or, if you prefer, it can be kept towards the Grade 6 camp in February.\n\nStudents should bring a hat, sun cream, a refillable water bottle and a small amount of pocket money (no more than 200 baht). Please do not bring expensive electronic devices.\n\nThank you for your understanding. I know many of you were looking forward to the original date, and I am sorry for the disappointment.\n\nBest wishes,\nMs Darin","stem":"Why has the field trip been moved to a new date?","choices":["Too many students signed up for the trip.","The floating market is closed on 14 November.","Some of the boats need repairs and will not be ready in time.","The coach company cancelled the booking."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 2 says \"two of its boats need urgent repairs and will not be ready in time\", and using only the other boats would not be safe. The market closure is on the new date, 21 November.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 2 บอกว่าเรือสองลำต้องซ่อมด่วนและจะไม่ทันวันเดินทาง และการอัดทุกคนลงเรือที่เหลือไม่ปลอดภัย ส่วนตลาดน้ำปิดในวันใหม่ คือ 21 พฤศจิกายน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Understanding why a detail is included","th":"การเข้าใจเหตุผลที่ผู้เขียนใส่รายละเอียด"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Farmers With Six Legs\n\nLong before humans planted their first crops, some insects were already farming. Leafcutter ants, which live in the rainforests of Central and South America, are among the most impressive of these tiny farmers.\n\nAs their name suggests, leafcutter ants cut pieces of leaf and carry them back to their nest, often walking in long lines that look like a moving green river. Surprisingly, however, they do not eat the leaves. Instead, they chew them into a soft paste and use it to grow a special fungus deep underground. The fungus is their only food, and it cannot survive anywhere except inside their nests.\n\nA single colony can contain several million ants, and each one has a job that depends on its size. The largest ants guard the nest. Medium-sized workers cut and carry leaves. The smallest, which are hardly bigger than a grain of sand, care for the fungus gardens and remove anything that might harm them. Some of these tiny workers even ride on the leaves during the journey home, protecting the carriers from flies that try to lay eggs on them.\n\nLike human farmers, the ants must also deal with pests. A harmful mould sometimes attacks their gardens. To fight it, the ants carry bacteria on their bodies that produce a natural medicine. Scientists are now studying these bacteria in the hope of discovering new medicines for people.\n\nIt is easy to dismiss ants as small and unimportant. Yet these insects solved the problems of farming millions of years before we did, and they may still have something to teach us.","stem":"Why does the writer mention that scientists are studying the ants' bacteria?","choices":["To prove that the mould is dangerous to people","To show that the ants' methods may be useful to humans","To warn readers that ants can spread disease","To explain why ant colonies grow so large"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The bacteria make a natural medicine against mould, and scientists hope to find \"new medicines for people\". This supports the final idea that ants \"may still have something to teach us\".","th":"แบคทีเรียสร้างยาธรรมชาติต้านเชื้อรา และนักวิทยาศาสตร์หวังจะค้นพบ \"new medicines for people\" ซึ่งสนับสนุนข้อสรุปตอนท้ายว่ามดอาจยังมีสิ่งที่สอนมนุษย์ได้"}}]}},{"exam":"gate","set":3,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"gate-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to thanks","th":"การตอบรับคำขอบคุณ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Thank you so much for helping me carry these boxes.\"\nB: \"______\"","choices":["I'm afraid not.","Yes, you are.","Thanks a lot.","It was no trouble at all."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"It was no trouble at all.\" is a polite reply to thanks. \"Thanks a lot.\" would be thanking A back, which makes no sense, and the other two replies do not answer thanks.","th":"\"It was no trouble at all.\" (ไม่ลำบากเลย) ใช้ตอบรับคำขอบคุณอย่างสุภาพ ส่วน \"Thanks a lot.\" เป็นการขอบคุณกลับซึ่งไม่สมเหตุสมผล และอีกสองคำตอบไม่ได้ตอบคำขอบคุณ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Expressing doubt","th":"การแสดงความไม่แน่ใจหรือสงสัย"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Do you think it'll rain this afternoon?\"\nB: \"______ The sky's completely clear.\"","choices":["It's raining cats and dogs.","I hope so.","I think so.","I doubt it."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A clear sky means rain is unlikely, so \"I doubt it.\" fits. \"I think so.\" and \"I hope so.\" suggest rain will come, and \"It's raining cats and dogs.\" means it is raining heavily now.","th":"ท้องฟ้าแจ่มใสแสดงว่าฝนไม่น่าจะตก จึงตอบว่า \"I doubt it.\" (ไม่น่าจะตก) ส่วน \"I think so.\" และ \"I hope so.\" บอกว่าฝนอาจตก และ \"It's raining cats and dogs.\" แปลว่า ฝนกำลังตกหนัก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Forming a noun from a verb","th":"การสร้างคำนามจากคำกริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Word formation","th":"การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The ______ of the new bridge across the river took almost three years.\"","choices":["constructed","constructive","construction","construct"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"After \"the\" and before \"of\" we need a noun: \"construction\". \"Constructive\" is an adjective, and \"constructed\" and \"construct\" are verb forms.","th":"หลัง \"the\" และหน้า \"of\" ต้องใช้คำนาม คือ \"construction\" ส่วน \"constructive\" เป็นคำคุณศัพท์ และ \"constructed\" กับ \"construct\" เป็นรูปกริยา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Alike, like and similar","th":"การใช้ alike, like และ similar"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The twin sisters look so ______ that even their teachers mix them up.\"","choices":["like","alike","likely","similar to"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Look alike\" (without an object) means look the same. \"Look like\" and \"similar to\" need an object after them, and \"likely\" means probable.","th":"\"look alike\" (ไม่มีกรรมตามหลัง) แปลว่า หน้าตาเหมือนกัน ส่วน \"look like\" และ \"similar to\" ต้องมีกรรมตามหลัง และ \"likely\" แปลว่า น่าจะเป็นไปได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Adjective + preposition: good at","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ + บุพบท: good at"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My sister is very good ______ drawing animals.\"","choices":["in","at","on","for"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"We say \"good at\" a skill or activity. \"Good for\" means healthy or useful for something, which is a different meaning.","th":"ความเก่งในทักษะหรือกิจกรรมใช้ \"good at\" ส่วน \"good for\" แปลว่า ดีต่อหรือมีประโยชน์ต่อ ซึ่งความหมายต่างกัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inversion after hardly","th":"การสลับที่หลัง hardly"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Hardly ______ the platform when the doors closed and the train pulled away.\"","choices":["we had reached","we reached","have we reached","had we reached"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"When \"Hardly\" starts the sentence, the auxiliary comes before the subject, and the pattern is \"Hardly had + subject + past participle … when …\".","th":"เมื่อขึ้นต้นประโยคด้วย \"Hardly\" ต้องสลับกริยาช่วยไว้หน้าประธาน รูปแบบคือ \"Hardly had + ประธาน + V3 … when …\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Uncountable nouns that look plural","th":"นามนับไม่ได้ที่ดูเหมือนพหูพจน์"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The news [[were]] so surprising [[that]] nobody [[knew]] what [[to say]].","choices":["were","that","knew","to say"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"News\" looks plural but is uncountable, so it takes a singular verb: \"The news was\".","th":"\"news\" ดูเหมือนพหูพจน์ แต่เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ จึงใช้กริยาเอกพจน์ คือ \"The news was\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Third conditional","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: If I [[had known]] you were in hospital, I [[would visit]] you [[instead of]] going [[to]] the cinema.","choices":["had known","would visit","instead of","to"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The if-clause uses the past perfect for an unreal past, so the main clause needs \"would have visited\", not \"would visit\".","th":"อนุประโยค if ใช้ past perfect แสดงสิ่งที่ไม่ได้เกิดขึ้นในอดีต ประโยคหลักจึงต้องใช้ \"would have visited\" ไม่ใช่ \"would visit\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Contrast conjunction but","th":"คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้ง but"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nEvery second Saturday, our school hall turns into a repair café. Neighbours bring broken lamps, torn jackets and radios that have fallen silent, and volunteers, many of them retired engineers, try to bring them back to life. The idea is simple, (1) ______ its effects are surprisingly wide. Each object that is fixed is one less piece of rubbish sent to landfill, and visitors often leave with more than a working toaster. (2) ______ watching the volunteers at work, they learn how everyday machines are put together, and many go home determined to try small repairs (3) ______.\n\nNot every story ends happily. Some items are (4) ______ repair, and the volunteers are honest about it. (5) ______, a failed repair still has value, because the owner now understands why the object broke and can choose a better-made replacement. Last year the café kept more than 400 kilograms of goods out of the bin. The organisers admit that this figure is tiny (6) ______ the amount the city throws away every single week. Yet they argue that the numbers miss the point. If more people (7) ______ to value the things they already own, they would buy less and throw away less, and that kind of change is far harder to (8) ______ than kilograms of rubbish.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["because","so","unless","but"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Simple\" and \"surprisingly wide\" effects are a contrast, so \"but\" fits. \"So\" gives a result, \"because\" a reason, and \"unless\" a condition.","th":"ความคิดที่ \"เรียบง่าย\" กับผลที่ \"กว้างไกลเกินคาด\" ขัดแย้งกัน จึงใช้ \"but\" ส่วน \"so\" บอกผล \"because\" บอกเหตุ และ \"unless\" บอกเงื่อนไข"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Linking adverb of contrast","th":"คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้งระหว่างประโยค"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nEvery second Saturday, our school hall turns into a repair café. Neighbours bring broken lamps, torn jackets and radios that have fallen silent, and volunteers, many of them retired engineers, try to bring them back to life. The idea is simple, (1) ______ its effects are surprisingly wide. Each object that is fixed is one less piece of rubbish sent to landfill, and visitors often leave with more than a working toaster. (2) ______ watching the volunteers at work, they learn how everyday machines are put together, and many go home determined to try small repairs (3) ______.\n\nNot every story ends happily. Some items are (4) ______ repair, and the volunteers are honest about it. (5) ______, a failed repair still has value, because the owner now understands why the object broke and can choose a better-made replacement. Last year the café kept more than 400 kilograms of goods out of the bin. The organisers admit that this figure is tiny (6) ______ the amount the city throws away every single week. Yet they argue that the numbers miss the point. If more people (7) ______ to value the things they already own, they would buy less and throw away less, and that kind of change is far harder to (8) ______ than kilograms of rubbish.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["Similarly","For instance","Nevertheless","Therefore"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"A failed repair sounds bad, yet it \"still has value\". This is a contrast, so \"Nevertheless\" (however) fits. \"Therefore\" shows result, \"For instance\" gives an example, and \"Similarly\" shows likeness.","th":"การซ่อมไม่สำเร็จฟังดูไม่ดี แต่ก็ \"still has value\" จึงเป็นความขัดแย้ง ใช้ \"Nevertheless\" (อย่างไรก็ตาม) ส่วน \"Therefore\" บอกผล \"For instance\" ยกตัวอย่าง และ \"Similarly\" บอกความคล้าย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Locating details in an advert","th":"การหารายละเอียดในโฆษณา"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"VOLUNTEERS WANTED: JUNIOR KEEPERS PROGRAMME\nSai Ngam Wildlife Rescue Centre\n\nEvery year our centre cares for more than 300 injured or orphaned animals, from slow lorises rescued from illegal pet sellers to baby otters separated from their mothers in floods. Most of them will eventually be released into the wild, and we need your help to get them there.\n\nThis school holiday, we are looking for twenty Junior Keepers aged 11–15 to join us for a five-day programme (Monday to Friday, 9.00 a.m.–3.30 p.m.).\n\nWhat will you do?\n• Prepare food for animals that are recovering\n• Build \"enrichment\" toys that keep animals active and curious\n• Help our vets record each animal's weight and progress\n• Learn how we prepare animals for life back in the forest\n\nWhat you will NOT do\nTo help animals stay wild, Junior Keepers will not hold, stroke or play with any animal. The less contact our patients have with people, the better their chances after release.\n\nCost: 2,500 baht, which includes lunch, a T-shirt and a certificate. All money goes directly to animal care. Students from our partner schools receive a 20% discount.\n\nHow to apply: Fill in the online form and write 100–150 words explaining why you want to join. Places are limited, and we choose volunteers based on their answers, not on who applies first. Applications close on 30 September.\n\nParents are welcome to join us for the closing session on Friday afternoon.","stem":"Which of these will Junior Keepers do during the programme?","choices":["Make objects that keep the animals active","Release the animals into the forest on Friday","Stroke the animals to help them relax","Take animals home at the weekend"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The list includes \"Build 'enrichment' toys that keep animals active and curious\". Volunteers will not touch the animals, and nothing says they take animals home or release them.","th":"รายการกิจกรรมระบุว่า \"Build 'enrichment' toys that keep animals active and curious\" อาสาสมัครจะไม่สัมผัสสัตว์ และไม่มีการกล่าวว่าจะพาสัตว์กลับบ้านหรือปล่อยสัตว์เอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting an analogy","th":"การตีความการเปรียบเทียบ"},"topic":{"en":"Opinion article","th":"บทความแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"Is Homework Worth It?\nby Tanawat, Grade 6\n\nEvery evening, millions of students across Thailand sit at their desks with a pile of worksheets. Some people now argue that primary schools should stop giving homework altogether. I understand why, but I believe that would be a mistake.\n\nCritics have a point when they say that too much homework is harmful. When I was in Grade 4, I sometimes worked until ten o'clock at night, copying answers I did not understand just to finish on time. That kind of homework taught me nothing except how to be tired. It also left no time for sport, music, or simply talking to my family, all of which matter for a child's development.\n\nHowever, the problem in that case was the amount and the type of homework, not homework itself. This year my teacher gives us short tasks of no more than thirty minutes, and they are different from what we do in class. Last month, for example, we interviewed an older relative about how our neighbourhood had changed. I learned more from talking to my grandfather than from any worksheet, and the project could never have been done in a classroom.\n\nGood homework also builds habits that we will need later. In secondary school there will be no teacher standing beside us, reminding us what to do next. Students who have learned to plan their own time will have a clear advantage.\n\nSo instead of banning homework, schools should make it better: shorter, more varied and connected to real life. Getting rid of it completely would be like throwing away a whole basket of mangoes because a few of them are bad.","stem":"What does the final sentence about the basket of mangoes mean?","choices":["Schools should give students healthy fruit instead of homework.","Most homework that students receive is bad.","Bad homework should always be thrown away first.","It would be foolish to get rid of something useful just because part of it is bad."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The good mangoes stand for useful homework and the bad ones for poor homework. Throwing away the whole basket would waste the good part, just as banning all homework would.","th":"มะม่วงดีเปรียบกับการบ้านที่มีประโยชน์ มะม่วงเสียเปรียบกับการบ้านที่ไม่ดี การทิ้งทั้งตะกร้าทำให้เสียของดีไปด้วย เช่นเดียวกับการยกเลิกการบ้านทั้งหมด"}}]}},{"exam":"satit","set":1,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":true,"file":"satit-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response in a dialogue","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Thank you so much for helping me carry these boxes.\nB: ______","choices":["Same to you.","Yes, please.","You're welcome.","Excuse me."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'You're welcome' is the normal reply to 'thank you'. 'Yes, please' accepts an offer, 'Same to you' returns a wish such as 'Have a nice day', and 'Excuse me' gets attention or apologises.","th":"You're welcome เป็นคำตอบเมื่อมีคนขอบคุณ ส่วน Yes, please ใช้ตอบรับข้อเสนอ Same to you ใช้ตอบคำอวยพร และ Excuse me ใช้ขอโทษหรือเรียกความสนใจ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Refusing a request politely","th":"การปฏิเสธคำขออย่างสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Could I borrow your ruler for a minute?\nB: ______ I'm using it for my art homework, but you can have it in five minutes.","choices":["No problem, take it.","Yes, you could.","Sorry, not right now.","Sure, here you are."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B is still using the ruler and offers it 'in five minutes', so B is saying no for now: 'Sorry, not right now.' 'Sure, here you are' and 'No problem, take it' give the ruler immediately, which contradicts the next sentence. 'Yes, you could' is not a natural reply to 'Could I …?' (we say 'Yes, you can' or 'Of course').","th":"B ยังใช้ไม้บรรทัดอยู่และบอกว่าให้ยืมได้ในอีกห้านาที แปลว่าตอนนี้ยังให้ไม่ได้ จึงตอบ Sorry, not right now. ส่วน Sure, here you are และ No problem, take it คือให้ยืมทันที ซึ่งขัดกับประโยคถัดไป และ Yes, you could ไม่เป็นธรรมชาติเมื่อตอบ Could I …? (ควรใช้ Yes, you can หรือ Of course)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Antonyms","th":"คำตรงข้าม"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"The opposite of \"ancient\" is ______.","choices":["modern","quiet","huge","old"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Ancient' means very old, from long ago, so its opposite is 'modern' (new, of the present time). 'Old' is a synonym, not an opposite.","th":"ancient แปลว่า เก่าแก่ โบราณ คำตรงข้ามคือ modern (ทันสมัย) ส่วน old มีความหมายใกล้เคียง ไม่ใช่คำตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Verb–noun collocations","th":"คำกริยาที่ใช้คู่กับคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Please ______ attention to the safety instructions before the experiment begins.","choices":["make","do","pay","take"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The fixed expression (collocation) is 'pay attention to'. We do not say 'make', 'do' or 'take' attention.","th":"วลีที่ใช้คู่กันเสมอ (collocation) คือ pay attention to แปลว่า ให้ความสนใจ ไม่ใช้ make, do หรือ take กับ attention"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Modals of obligation","th":"กริยาช่วยแสดงความจำเป็น"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"You ______ wear a helmet when you ride a motorbike. It's the law.","choices":["needn't","might","mustn't","must"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'It's the law' shows an obligation, so 'must' is right. 'Mustn't' means it is forbidden, 'needn't' means it is not necessary, and 'might' only shows possibility.","th":"\"It's the law\" แสดงว่าเป็นข้อบังคับ จึงใช้ must ส่วน mustn't แปลว่า ห้าม needn't แปลว่า ไม่จำเป็น และ might แสดงความเป็นไปได้เท่านั้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Gerund after look forward to","th":"V-ing หลัง look forward to"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"I'm really looking forward to ______ you at the camp next week.","choices":["seeing","be seen","saw","see"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, not part of an infinitive, so it is followed by a gerund (-ing): 'looking forward to seeing you'. Using the base form 'see' is a very common mistake.","th":"ในวลี look forward to คำว่า to เป็นบุพบท ไม่ใช่ส่วนของ infinitive จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ seeing การใช้ see เป็นข้อผิดพลาดที่พบบ่อย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Third-person -s in the present simple","th":"การเติม -s กับประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every morning my sister [[walk]] to school [[with]] her friends, [[but]] today she [[took]] a taxi.","choices":["walk","with","but","took"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'My sister' is third person singular and 'every morning' shows a habit, so the verb must be 'walks'. 'Took' is correct because 'today' describes something that already happened.","th":"my sister เป็นประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 และ every morning แสดงกิจวัตร จึงต้องใช้ walks ส่วน took ถูกต้องเพราะ today เล่าเหตุการณ์ที่เกิดขึ้นแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Uncountable nouns","th":"คำนามนับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My father gave me [[some]] useful [[advices]] [[about]] how to [[prepare]] for the exam.","choices":["some","advices","about","prepare"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Advice' is uncountable in English, so it has no plural: 'some useful advice' (or 'some useful pieces of advice'). Thai learners often add -s because they count each idea.","th":"advice เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ในภาษาอังกฤษ จึงเติม s ไม่ได้ ต้องเป็น some useful advice (หรือ pieces of advice) ผู้เรียนไทยมักเติม s เพราะนับเป็นข้อ ๆ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Prepositions after verbs (turn … into)","th":"บุพบทหลังกริยา (turn … into)"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["onto","at","with","into"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The pattern is 'turn something into something else' (change it). The rooftop was changed into a garden.","th":"โครงสร้าง turn A into B หมายถึง เปลี่ยน A ให้เป็น B ดาดฟ้าถูกเปลี่ยนเป็นสวนผัก จึงใช้ into"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Relative which referring to a whole idea","th":"which ขยายความทั้งข้อความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (4)?","choices":["what","where","who","which"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After a comma, a non-defining relative clause that refers back to a whole idea (turning waste into compost) uses 'which'. 'Who' is for people, 'where' for places, and 'what' cannot introduce a relative clause after a noun or idea.","th":"หลังเครื่องหมายจุลภาค อนุประโยคที่ขยายความทั้งข้อความก่อนหน้า (การทำปุ๋ยหมักจากเศษอาหาร) ใช้ which ส่วน who ใช้กับคน where ใช้กับสถานที่ และ what ใช้เป็นคำเชื่อมขยายคำนามไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","choices":["How scientists study the eyes of sea animals","How an octopus changes its appearance to hide or protect itself","Why octopuses are colour-blind","Where the mimic octopus lives"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Every paragraph explains part of the octopus's disguise: colour cells, skin bumps, copying other animals and how it may sense light. Colour-blindness, the mimic octopus's home and eyes are only small details.","th":"ทุกย่อหน้าอธิบายการพรางตัวของปลาหมึกยักษ์ ทั้งเซลล์สี ปุ่มบนผิวหนัง การเลียนแบบสัตว์อื่น และการรับรู้แสง ส่วนเรื่องตาบอดสี ถิ่นที่อยู่ของ mimic octopus และดวงตาเป็นเพียงรายละเอียดย่อย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"Why did the writer read aloud outside Mango's cage instead of trying to touch him?","choices":["Because she was not allowed to open the cage","To stop Mango from barking at other dogs","To let Mango get used to her without frightening him","Because she needed to finish her homework"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Mango 'doesn't trust people yet' and hid in the corner. By staying close but not touching him, she let him get used to her calmly — and he slowly came closer. Mango never barked, and nothing says she was forbidden to open the cage or doing homework.","th":"มังโก้ \"ยังไม่ไว้ใจคน\" และหลบอยู่มุมกรง การนั่งอยู่ใกล้ ๆ โดยไม่แตะต้องช่วยให้มันคุ้นเคยกับผู้เขียนโดยไม่ตกใจ และมันก็ค่อย ๆ เข้ามาใกล้ มังโก้ไม่ได้เห่า และไม่มีข้อความว่าห้ามเปิดกรงหรือผู้เขียนทำการบ้าน"}}]}},{"exam":"satit","set":2,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"satit-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Giving directions","th":"การบอกทาง"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"Tourist: Excuse me, could you tell me the way to the post office?\nYou: ______","choices":["It opens at half past eight.","The post office sells stamps and envelopes.","Yes, I could tell you yesterday.","Sure. Go straight on and take the second left. It's opposite the bank."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The tourist wants directions, so the helpful reply gives a route and a landmark. The other replies talk about the past, opening hours, or what the post office sells.","th":"นักท่องเที่ยวถามทาง คำตอบที่ช่วยได้คือบอกเส้นทางและจุดสังเกต คำตอบอื่นพูดถึงอดีต เวลาเปิด หรือสิ่งที่ไปรษณีย์ขาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Responding to a compliment","th":"การตอบรับคำชม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Your English has improved so much this year!\nB: ______","choices":["Thank you. I've been watching films in English every evening.","Yes, it's improved very much, isn't it?","Not at all. You're welcome.","Never mind. It doesn't matter."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The natural reply to a compliment is to thank the person, often with a little explanation: 'Thank you. I've been watching films in English every evening.' 'You're welcome' answers thanks, 'Never mind' answers an apology, and agreeing proudly with the compliment sounds rude.","th":"การตอบคำชมที่เป็นธรรมชาติคือขอบคุณ และอาจอธิบายเพิ่มเล็กน้อย Thank you. I've been watching films in English every evening. ส่วน You're welcome ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ Never mind ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษ และการเห็นด้วยกับคำชมตัวเองฟังดูไม่สุภาพ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Synonyms in context","th":"คำพ้องความหมายตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Tan was reluctant to lend his new bicycle to anyone. The word \"reluctant\" is closest in meaning to ______.","choices":["forced","unwilling","happy","able"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Reluctant' means not wanting to do something: unwilling. Tan did not want to lend his new bike.","th":"reluctant แปลว่า ไม่เต็มใจ ตรงกับ unwilling คือแทนไม่อยากให้ใครยืมจักรยานคันใหม่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a noun form","th":"การเลือกรูปคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Word formation","th":"การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Her ______ to help others made her popular with everyone in the village.","choices":["willing","willingness","will be","willingly"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"After the possessive 'Her' we need a noun as the subject: willingness. 'willing' is an adjective, 'willingly' an adverb, and 'will be' a verb form.","th":"หลังคำแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ Her ต้องเป็นคำนามทำหน้าที่ประธาน คือ willingness ส่วน willing เป็นคุณศัพท์ willingly เป็นกริยาวิเศษณ์ และ will be เป็นกริยา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Modals: lack of obligation","th":"กริยาช่วยแสดงว่าไม่จำเป็นต้องทำ"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"You ______ wear your school uniform on Fridays. It's optional.","choices":["don't have to","mustn't","can't","shouldn't"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'It's optional' means there is no obligation, which is expressed by 'don't have to'. 'mustn't' and 'can't' mean it is forbidden, and 'shouldn't' advises against it.","th":"\"It's optional\" แปลว่าไม่บังคับ จึงใช้ don't have to (ไม่จำเป็นต้อง) ส่วน mustn't และ can't แปลว่าห้าม และ shouldn't แปลว่าไม่ควร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Reported yes/no questions","th":"การรายงานคำถาม yes/no"},"topic":{"en":"Passive voice and reported speech","th":"ประโยค passive และ reported speech"},"passage":"","stem":"My aunt asked me ______ I had ever been to Japan.","choices":["what","whether","did","that"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A yes/no question ('Have you ever been to Japan?') is reported with 'if' or 'whether'. 'That' is used for reported statements, 'what' would need a missing object, and 'did' cannot introduce a reported question.","th":"คำถามแบบ yes/no (Have you ever been to Japan?) เมื่อรายงานต้องใช้ if หรือ whether ส่วน that ใช้รายงานประโยคบอกเล่า what ต้องมีสิ่งที่ถามขาดหายไป และ did ใช้ขึ้นต้นคำถามที่รายงานไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Base verb after does not","th":"กริยารูปเดิมหลัง doesn't"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My cousin [[doesn't]] [[likes]] spicy food, [[so]] we [[ordered]] something mild.","choices":["doesn't","likes","so","ordered"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"After 'doesn't' the main verb is in the base form: 'doesn't like'. The -s is already shown in 'does', so 'likes' is wrong.","th":"หลัง doesn't ต้องใช้กริยารูปเดิม คือ doesn't like เพราะ does แสดงการเติม s ไปแล้ว likes จึงผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Auxiliary agreement in questions","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยาช่วยในประโยคคำถาม"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses, agreement and connectors","th":"อนุประโยค ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา และคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[Does]] your parents know [[that]] you [[are]] going to the concert [[tonight]]?","choices":["Does","that","are","tonight"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The subject 'your parents' is plural, so the question needs 'Do': 'Do your parents know …?' The other parts are correct.","th":"ประธาน your parents เป็นพหูพจน์ จึงต้องใช้ Do คือ Do your parents know …? ส่วนอื่นถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Present perfect continuous","th":"present perfect continuous"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Every Saturday morning, a group of teenagers in Thonburi meet at an old wooden pier beside a canal. They have (1) ______ the canal for almost two years, and they are proud of what they have achieved. At first, local people thought the project would (2) ______ after a few weeks. \"Nobody believed us,\" says Mint, 14, (3) ______ started the group with her cousin. \"They said the rubbish would just come back.\"\nThe rubbish did come back, (4) ______ the teenagers did not give up. Instead, they began to ask where it came from. They discovered that most of it was plastic bags (5) ______ away at a market upstream. So they visited the market and offered the sellers cloth bags (6) ______ by students at their school.\nToday there is (7) ______ less rubbish in the water than before, and fish have returned to parts of the canal. \"We didn't really change the canal,\" Mint explains. \"We changed the habits of the people who live along it.\" Now the group meets students from three other schools who want to start similar projects on their own canals.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["being cleaned","clean","cleaning","been cleaning"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After 'have' and with 'for almost two years', we need the present perfect continuous: have been cleaning. 'have cleaning' and 'have clean' are not English, and 'have being cleaned' is not a correct verb form.","th":"หลัง have และมี for almost two years ต้องใช้ Present Perfect Continuous คือ have been cleaning ส่วน have cleaning, have clean และ have being cleaned ผิดรูปไวยากรณ์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Linking words for contrast","th":"คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้ง"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Every Saturday morning, a group of teenagers in Thonburi meet at an old wooden pier beside a canal. They have (1) ______ the canal for almost two years, and they are proud of what they have achieved. At first, local people thought the project would (2) ______ after a few weeks. \"Nobody believed us,\" says Mint, 14, (3) ______ started the group with her cousin. \"They said the rubbish would just come back.\"\nThe rubbish did come back, (4) ______ the teenagers did not give up. Instead, they began to ask where it came from. They discovered that most of it was plastic bags (5) ______ away at a market upstream. So they visited the market and offered the sellers cloth bags (6) ______ by students at their school.\nToday there is (7) ______ less rubbish in the water than before, and fish have returned to parts of the canal. \"We didn't really change the canal,\" Mint explains. \"We changed the habits of the people who live along it.\" Now the group meets students from three other schools who want to start similar projects on their own canals.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (4)?","choices":["because","or","but","so"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The two ideas contrast: the rubbish came back, yet they did not give up. 'but' shows contrast; 'so' and 'because' show result and reason.","th":"สองใจความขัดแย้งกัน ขยะกลับมาอีก แต่พวกเขาไม่ยอมแพ้ จึงใช้ but ส่วน so และ because แสดงผลและเหตุ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Why Teenagers Can't Fall Asleep Early\nMany parents complain that their teenage children stay up late and then cannot get out of bed in the morning. It is easy to blame phones or laziness, but scientists say the real story is more interesting.\nOur bodies have an internal clock that tells us when to feel sleepy. One of its signals is a hormone called melatonin, which the brain releases as evening falls. In young children, melatonin rises early, so they feel tired soon after dinner. During the teenage years, however, the release of melatonin shifts later, often by one to two hours. A fourteen-year-old who goes to bed at nine o'clock may simply lie awake, staring at the ceiling, until the hormone arrives.\nAt the same time, teenagers still need a lot of sleep: around eight to ten hours a night, according to most sleep researchers. If a student cannot fall asleep before eleven but must wake at six to catch the school bus, the numbers do not work. The result is a \"sleep debt\" that builds up during the week. Many teenagers try to repay it by sleeping until noon at weekends, which pushes their clock even later and makes Monday morning feel worse.\nScreens do not help. The bright light from a phone can delay melatonin even further. But turning off devices alone will not turn a teenager into a morning person.\nFor this reason, some schools in different countries have experimented with starting classes later. Several reported that students were less sleepy in lessons and that late arrivals fell. Critics point out that later starts can cause problems for parents' work schedules and for after-school sports. The debate continues, but one thing is clear: a sleepy teenager is not necessarily a lazy one.","stem":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","choices":["To describe in detail how the brain produces melatonin","To prove that every school should start classes later","To explain that teenagers' late sleeping is partly caused by changes in their body clock","To persuade parents to take away teenagers' phones at night"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The passage argues that the real cause is biological (melatonin is released later in teenagers), ending with 'a sleepy teenager is not necessarily a lazy one'. It says turning off phones alone will not fix the problem, presents later school starts as a debate, and mentions melatonin only briefly.","th":"บทความอธิบายว่าสาเหตุสำคัญมาจากร่างกาย (เมลาโทนินหลั่งช้าลงในวัยรุ่น) และจบว่า วัยรุ่นที่ง่วงไม่จำเป็นต้องขี้เกียจ บทความบอกว่าการปิดโทรศัพท์อย่างเดียวไม่พอ เสนอเรื่องการเข้าเรียนสายเป็นประเด็นที่ยังถกเถียง และพูดถึงการสร้างเมลาโทนินเพียงสั้น ๆ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The Last Move\nPloy had practised for the regional chess championship every evening for three months. She knew her openings by heart, and her coach, Mr Arun, said she had a real chance of reaching the final.\nIn the semi-final she faced a quiet boy from Chiang Mai who seemed to make every move without thinking. For two hours the game was even. Then, with only a few minutes left on her clock, Ploy saw it: a chance to take his queen. Her heart was pounding as she reached for her knight.\nSomewhere at the back of her mind, a small voice asked, \"Why would he leave his queen there?\" She ignored it. She took the queen, and three moves later she was checkmated.\nPloy shook the boy's hand, walked calmly out of the hall and then cried in the bathroom for ten minutes. When she came out, Mr Arun was waiting with two bottles of cold water.\n\"You lost because you stopped asking questions,\" he said gently. \"The moment you were sure you were winning, you stopped looking at his plan and only looked at yours.\"\nPloy wanted to argue, but she knew he was right. On the bus home she replayed the game in her notebook, writing a question mark next to every move she had made too quickly. There were seven.\nThe next year, Ploy did not win the championship either. She came second. But when people asked her what had changed, she did not talk about openings or tactics. She said, \"I learned to listen to the small voice.\"","stem":"Which detail shows that Ploy hid her disappointment in public at first?","choices":["She came second in the championship the next year.","She replayed the game in her notebook on the bus.","Her heart was pounding as she reached for her knight.","She walked calmly out of the hall before crying in the bathroom."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Walking calmly out of the hall, then crying alone in the bathroom, shows she controlled her feelings in front of others. The pounding heart is excitement before the move, and the notebook and the next year's result come later.","th":"การเดินออกจากห้องอย่างสงบแล้วไปร้องไห้คนเดียวในห้องน้ำ แสดงว่าเธอเก็บความรู้สึกต่อหน้าผู้อื่น ส่วนหัวใจเต้นแรงเป็นความตื่นเต้นก่อนเดินหมาก และสมุดบันทึกกับผลในปีต่อมาเกิดขึ้นภายหลัง"}}]}},{"exam":"satit","set":3,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 60 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 60 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ตามข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชา ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"satit-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Accepting an apology","th":"การตอบรับคำขอโทษ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I'm so sorry, I've spilled juice on your notebook.\nB: ______ It was only my rough notes.","choices":["How dare you!","Don't worry about it.","It's my pleasure.","Apology accepted, but you must buy me a new one."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"B's next sentence ('It was only my rough notes') shows that B is not upset, so B accepts the apology kindly: 'Don't worry about it.' 'How dare you!' is angry, 'It's my pleasure' answers thanks, and demanding a new notebook does not match notes that were not important.","th":"ประโยคถัดไปของ B (เป็นแค่สมุดจดร่าง) แสดงว่า B ไม่โกรธ จึงตอบรับคำขอโทษว่า Don't worry about it. ส่วน How dare you! แสดงความโกรธ It's my pleasure ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ และการเรียกร้องให้ซื้อเล่มใหม่ไม่สอดคล้องกับสมุดที่ไม่สำคัญ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Praising honesty","th":"การชื่นชมความซื่อสัตย์"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I told Mum that I was the one who broke her favourite vase. I feel much better now.\nB: ______","choices":["How careless of me!","What a pity! You should have hidden it.","Never mind. It's my fault.","Good for you. Honesty is the best policy."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A has told the truth and feels better, so B praises the decision: 'Good for you. Honesty is the best policy.' Suggesting A should have hidden it disagrees with A's good feeling, and 'It's my fault' and 'How careless of me!' wrongly make B the person who broke the vase.","th":"A ยอมบอกความจริงและรู้สึกดีขึ้น B จึงชื่นชมว่า Good for you. Honesty is the best policy. ส่วนการบอกว่าควรซ่อนไว้ขัดกับความรู้สึกของ A และ It's my fault กับ How careless of me! ทำให้ B กลายเป็นคนทำแจกันแตกเอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs in context","th":"กริยาวลีตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Our neighbours are going on holiday, so we've agreed to ______ their cat for a week.","choices":["look after","look up","look for","look into"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Look after' means to take care of, which is what you do for a neighbour's cat. 'Look up' means to find information (in a dictionary), 'look for' means to search, and 'look into' means to investigate.","th":"look after แปลว่า ดูแล เหมาะกับการดูแลแมวของเพื่อนบ้าน ส่วน look up แปลว่า ค้นหาข้อมูล (เช่น ในพจนานุกรม) look for แปลว่า มองหา และ look into แปลว่า สืบสวนตรวจสอบ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Verbs that look alike","th":"คำกริยาที่มีรูปคล้ายกัน"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"The doctor ______ some medicine for my cough and told me to rest for three days.","choices":["inscribed","described","prescribed","subscribed"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"A doctor 'prescribes' medicine (orders it for a patient). 'Described' means explained, 'subscribed' means signed up for something regularly, and 'inscribed' means carved or written on something.","th":"แพทย์ prescribe ยา หมายถึงสั่งจ่ายยา ส่วน describe คืออธิบาย subscribe คือสมัครรับเป็นประจำ และ inscribe คือสลักหรือจารึก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present perfect with since","th":"present perfect กับ since"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"My family ______ in Bangkok since 2019.","choices":["lives","lived","is living","has lived"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Since 2019' describes an action that started in the past and continues now, which needs the present perfect: has lived.","th":"since 2019 บอกเหตุการณ์ที่เริ่มในอดีตและยังดำเนินอยู่ถึงปัจจุบัน ต้องใช้ Present Perfect คือ has lived"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Modals of deduction","th":"กริยาช่วยแสดงการคาดคะเน"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"All the lights are off and nobody answers the door. They ______ be out.","choices":["needn't","must","can't","shouldn't"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The evidence (lights off, no answer) leads to a confident conclusion that they are out, so 'must' (logical certainty) fits. 'Can't be out' would mean the opposite.","th":"หลักฐาน (ไฟปิด ไม่มีคนเปิดประตู) ทำให้สรุปได้ค่อนข้างแน่ว่าไม่อยู่บ้าน จึงใช้ must แสดงการคาดคะเนที่มั่นใจ ส่วน can't be out มีความหมายตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with each of","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยากับ each of"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses, agreement and connectors","th":"อนุประโยค ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา และคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Each of the students [[have finished]] [[their]] project [[on time]] [[this term]].","choices":["have finished","their","on time","this term"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Each of the students' is singular, so the verb must be singular: 'has finished'. 'Their' is accepted in modern English to refer back to 'each', and the time phrases are correct.","th":"Each of the students เป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็นเอกพจน์ คือ has finished ส่วน their ใช้อ้างถึง each ได้ในภาษาอังกฤษปัจจุบัน และวลีบอกเวลาถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"be used to + -ing","th":"be used to + V-ing"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[Having]] grown up in Bangkok, I [[am used to]] [[live]] in a noisy city, [[so]] the traffic does not bother me.","choices":["Having","am used to","live","so"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"In 'be used to' (be familiar with), 'to' is a preposition, so it must be followed by -ing: 'I am used to living'. 'Having grown up' correctly shows the earlier reason.","th":"ใน be used to (เคยชินกับ) คำว่า to เป็นบุพบท จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ am used to living ส่วน Having grown up ถูกต้อง ใช้แสดงเหตุที่เกิดก่อน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Relative pronoun who","th":"ประพันธสรรพนาม who"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"In many cities around the world, people have started placing \"community fridges\" on the street. The idea is simple: anyone (1) ______ has extra food can leave it in the fridge, and anyone who needs food can take it, (2) ______ asking any questions. The first fridges of this kind appeared in Europe, and the idea soon (3) ______ to other countries.\n\nSupporters say the fridges help (4) ______ two problems at once. Huge amounts of food are thrown away every day, (5) ______ many families struggle to buy enough to eat. Bakeries and restaurants that would otherwise throw away bread or vegetables at the end of the day can leave them in the fridge instead.\n\nHowever, running a community fridge is not as easy as it sounds. Volunteers must check the food regularly to make sure nothing has gone (6) ______. Some neighbours also complain that the fridges attract crowds late at night. (7) ______ these difficulties, the number of community fridges keeps growing, and many people believe they show what ordinary people can do when they work together. Some cities now even publish online maps that show where the nearest fridge can be found.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["whom","whose","which","who"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The blank begins a relative clause describing 'anyone' (a person) and acts as its subject: anyone who has extra food.","th":"ช่องว่างขึ้นต้นอนุประโยคขยาย anyone (บุคคล) และทำหน้าที่ประธาน จึงใช้ who"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"help + bare infinitive","th":"help + กริยารูปเดิม"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"In many cities around the world, people have started placing \"community fridges\" on the street. The idea is simple: anyone (1) ______ has extra food can leave it in the fridge, and anyone who needs food can take it, (2) ______ asking any questions. The first fridges of this kind appeared in Europe, and the idea soon (3) ______ to other countries.\n\nSupporters say the fridges help (4) ______ two problems at once. Huge amounts of food are thrown away every day, (5) ______ many families struggle to buy enough to eat. Bakeries and restaurants that would otherwise throw away bread or vegetables at the end of the day can leave them in the fridge instead.\n\nHowever, running a community fridge is not as easy as it sounds. Volunteers must check the food regularly to make sure nothing has gone (6) ______. Some neighbours also complain that the fridges attract crowds late at night. (7) ______ these difficulties, the number of community fridges keeps growing, and many people believe they show what ordinary people can do when they work together. Some cities now even publish online maps that show where the nearest fridge can be found.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (4)?","choices":["solved","solves","solve","solution"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Help' is followed by a bare infinitive (or 'to' + verb): help solve two problems. 'Solution' is a noun and 'solves/solved' are finite forms.","th":"หลัง help ใช้กริยารูปพื้นฐาน (หรือ to + V) จึงเป็น help solve ส่วน solution เป็นคำนาม และ solves/solved ไม่ใช่รูปที่ตามหลัง help"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea or purpose","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญหรือจุดประสงค์"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"On a summer afternoon, the centre of a large city can be several degrees hotter than the countryside only a few kilometres away. Scientists call this an \"urban heat island\".\n\nThe main cause is the materials that cities are built from. Dark roads and roofs absorb most of the sunlight that falls on them and release the heat slowly, even after the sun has set. Concrete and brick store heat in the same way. In the countryside, by contrast, much of the ground is covered with plants and soil. Plants take up water through their roots and release it through their leaves, and this evaporation cools the air around them, rather like sweat cooling our skin.\n\nCities also produce heat of their own. Cars, factories and air conditioners all give out warm air. Ironically, the hotter a city becomes, the more people switch on their air conditioners, which pump even more heat into the streets outside.\n\nThe effect is not just uncomfortable. Hot nights make it harder for people to sleep and to recover from the heat of the day, and elderly people are especially at risk during heatwaves.\n\nFortunately, there are ways to cool a city down. Some cities paint roofs white so that they reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it. Others plant trees along streets or create \"green roofs\" covered with grass and small plants. In one study, streets lined with large trees were found to be noticeably cooler in summer than nearby streets without trees. Such solutions cost money, but supporters argue that they soon pay for themselves by reducing the need for air conditioning.","stem":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","choices":["To compare the weather in different countries","To persuade people to move from cities to the countryside","To explain what an urban heat island is, why it happens and how it can be reduced","To describe how air conditioners work"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The text defines the urban heat island, explains its causes (materials, city heat), its effects and finally ways to cool cities. It does not urge people to move or explain how air conditioners work.","th":"บทความให้ความหมายของปรากฏการณ์เกาะความร้อนในเมือง อธิบายสาเหตุ ผลกระทบ และวิธีลดความร้อน ไม่ได้ชักชวนให้ย้ายออกจากเมืองหรืออธิบายการทำงานของเครื่องปรับอากาศ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a literary text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่านวรรณกรรม"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"Kiet had never missed the last bus before. He stood at the empty stop, watching its red lights disappear around the corner, and felt his stomach drop. It was nearly ten o'clock, his phone battery had died an hour earlier, and home was eight kilometres away.\n\nHe thought about walking, but the road out of town had no pavement and almost no street lights. Instead, he went back to the small noodle shop where he had been studying with his friends. The owner, an old woman everyone called Auntie Lek, was stacking chairs.\n\n\"Forgotten something?\" she asked.\n\nKiet explained, feeling foolish. He had stayed late because he wanted to finish one more maths problem before his exam, and he had simply lost track of time.\n\nAuntie Lek did not laugh. She handed him her phone so that he could call his mother, then put a bowl of hot soup in front of him. \"Nobody studies well on an empty stomach,\" she said, \"and nobody walks that road at night. Not while I'm here.\"\n\nForty minutes later his mother's car pulled up outside. Kiet expected to be scolded, but his mother only hugged him and thanked Auntie Lek several times. On the way home she was quiet for a long while. Then she said, \"Next time, charge your phone before you leave. But I'm glad you made the sensible choice.\"\n\nThe following week, Kiet passed his maths exam with a better mark than he had hoped for. On his way home from school he stopped at the noodle shop and left a small card on the counter. Inside he had written just one line: \"Thank you for the soup — and for the phone.\"\n\nAuntie Lek pinned the card on the wall beside the menu, where it stayed for many years.","stem":"What does Auntie Lek's comment \"Not while I'm here\" suggest about her?","choices":["She is angry that Kiet came back so late.","She feels responsible for Kiet's safety and will not let him walk the road at night.","She wants Kiet to leave the shop quickly.","She thinks Kiet is lying about the bus."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"She has just said 'nobody walks that road at night'; 'not while I'm here' shows she will make sure he doesn't — she takes care of him like a responsible adult. She is kind, not angry or suspicious.","th":"ป้าเล็กเพิ่งพูดว่าไม่มีใครเดินถนนเส้นนั้นตอนกลางคืน และ Not while I'm here แสดงว่าตราบใดที่เธออยู่ เธอจะไม่ยอมให้เขาเดิน คือเธอรู้สึกรับผิดชอบต่อความปลอดภัยของเขา ไม่ได้โกรธหรือสงสัย"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":1,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":true,"file":"sk4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to a 'Would you mind…?' request","th":"การตอบรับคำขอที่ใช้ Would you mind…?"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Would you mind if I opened the window? It's really stuffy in here.\nB: ______ Go ahead.","choices":["Yes, I would.","Never mind.","No, thanks.","Not at all."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Would you mind…?\" asks whether you object, so \"Not at all\" (= I don't mind) agrees, which fits \"Go ahead\". \"Yes, I would\" refuses; \"Never mind\" dismisses a problem; \"No, thanks\" declines an offer.","th":"Would you mind…? ถามว่าเรารังเกียจหรือไม่ การตอบ Not at all แปลว่า ไม่รังเกียจเลย จึงเข้ากับ Go ahead ส่วน Yes, I would เป็นการปฏิเสธ Never mind ใช้บอกว่าไม่เป็นไร และ No, thanks ใช้ปฏิเสธข้อเสนอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Describing how something went","th":"การบอกผลว่าสิ่งใดเป็นอย่างไร"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: How did your presentation go this morning?\nB: ______ I forgot half of what I wanted to say.\nA: Oh no. I'm sure it wasn't as bad as you think.","choices":["It went like a dream.","I'm going to present it soon.","Not very well, to be honest.","It's going to be fine."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B forgot half the speech and A comforts B, so B must say it went badly: \"Not very well, to be honest\". \"It went like a dream\" means it went perfectly; the future forms ignore that the presentation is already over.","th":"B ลืมสิ่งที่จะพูดไปครึ่งหนึ่ง และ A ปลอบใจ จึงต้องตอบว่าไม่ค่อยดี Not very well, to be honest ส่วน It went like a dream แปลว่าราบรื่นมาก และรูปอนาคตไม่เข้ากับเหตุการณ์ที่ผ่านไปแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: fill in a form","th":"กริยาวลี fill in"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Please ______ the form and hand it to the officer at the counter.\"","choices":["fill up","fill in","fill on","fill with"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"We \"fill in\" (or fill out) a form, meaning write the required information. \"Fill up\" is for containers or petrol tanks; \"fill on\" does not exist; \"fill with\" needs a material (fill a box with books).","th":"กรอกแบบฟอร์มใช้ fill in (หรือ fill out) ส่วน fill up ใช้กับการเติมให้เต็ม เช่น ถังน้ำมัน fill on ไม่มีใช้ และ fill with ต้องตามด้วยสิ่งที่ใส่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Collocations with attention","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมกับ attention"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Although the documentary was very long, it never failed to ______ my attention.\"","choices":["pay","spend","hold","make"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Something interesting \"holds your attention\" (keeps you interested). We \"pay attention\" ourselves, but a film cannot pay my attention; \"spend\" and \"make\" do not collocate with attention.","th":"สิ่งที่น่าสนใจจะ hold your attention คือดึงความสนใจไว้ได้ เราเป็นผู้ pay attention เอง แต่สารคดีไม่สามารถ pay my attention ได้ ส่วน spend และ make ไม่ใช้กับ attention"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Past perfect with 'by the time'","th":"past perfect กับ by the time"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"By the time we arrived at the cinema, the film ______.\"","choices":["has already started","already starts","had already started","would already start"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The film started before we arrived, both in the past, so the earlier action takes the past perfect: \"had already started\". Present perfect and present simple do not fit a past time frame.","th":"หนังเริ่มก่อนที่เราจะมาถึง ซึ่งเป็นอดีตทั้งคู่ เหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนจึงใช้ past perfect คือ had already started ส่วน present perfect และ present simple ไม่เข้ากับกรอบเวลาอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Causative: have something done","th":"causative have something done"},"topic":{"en":"Passive and causative","th":"ประโยค passive และ causative"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My laptop screen was cracked, so I ______ at the shop near the station.\"","choices":["had it repaired","had repaired it","had it repair","repaired it by"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Someone at the shop fixed it for me, so we use the causative have + object + past participle: \"had it repaired\". \"Had repaired it\" says I fixed it myself; \"had it repair\" uses the wrong verb form.","th":"ช่างที่ร้านเป็นผู้ซ่อมให้ จึงใช้ causative have + กรรม + V3 คือ had it repaired ส่วน had repaired it หมายถึงซ่อมเอง และ had it repair ใช้รูปกริยาผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with 'neither of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยาเมื่อใช้ neither of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nAlthough they [[have visited]] Tokyo [[several times]], neither of my cousins [[speak]] Japanese [[well]].","choices":["have visited","several times","speak","well"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Neither of + plural noun\" takes a singular verb in careful English: \"neither of my cousins speaks\". The other parts are correct.","th":"neither of + คำนามพหูพจน์ ใช้กริยาเอกพจน์ จึงต้องเป็น speaks ไม่ใช่ speak ส่วนอื่นถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"'Look forward to' + -ing","th":"look forward to + V-ing"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, modals and reported speech","th":"รูปแบบกริยา modal และคำพูดรายงาน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nWhen your parents [[come]] to Bangkok [[next]] month, I'm really looking forward [[to meet]] [[them]].","choices":["come","next","to meet","them"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"In \"look forward to\", \"to\" is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund: \"looking forward to meeting them\".","th":"to ใน look forward to เป็นบุพบท จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ looking forward to meeting them"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: past simple for past habits","th":"การเติมคำ: past simple บอกสิ่งที่เคยเป็นในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Bangkok was once called the \"Venice of the East\" because its canals, or khlongs, (1) ______ as roads for boats carrying people and goods. Over the last century, (2) ______, many of these waterways were filled in to make room for cars. Today, the city is trying to bring some of them back to life.\n\nOne of the best-known projects is the restoration of Khlong Ong Ang, a canal near Chinatown that (3) ______ a symbol of pollution and neglect for decades. Workers removed tonnes of rubbish, and the stalls (4) ______ had crowded the banks were moved to a nearby market. The water is still far from clean, but the canal now attracts tourists, artists and families, (5) ______ come to enjoy the walking paths and colourful murals.\n\nSupporters say projects like this can (6) ______ the city cooler during the hot season, because water and trees lower the air temperature. Critics, however, point out that cleaning one canal is easy compared (7) ______ changing the habits of millions of residents who still pour waste into the water. If people (8) ______ these habits, they argue, even the most beautiful restoration will not last.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["were served","serving","served","had serve"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The canals did the job of roads in the past, so the active past simple \"served\" (= functioned) is needed. \"Were served\" is passive, \"serving\" has no main verb, and \"had serve\" is an incorrect form.","th":"คลองทำหน้าที่เป็นถนนในอดีต จึงใช้ past simple รูป active คือ served ส่วน were served เป็น passive serving ไม่มีกริยาแท้ และ had serve เป็นรูปที่ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'who' in non-defining clauses","th":"การเติมคำ: who ในอนุประโยคแบบไม่จำกัดความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Bangkok was once called the \"Venice of the East\" because its canals, or khlongs, (1) ______ as roads for boats carrying people and goods. Over the last century, (2) ______, many of these waterways were filled in to make room for cars. Today, the city is trying to bring some of them back to life.\n\nOne of the best-known projects is the restoration of Khlong Ong Ang, a canal near Chinatown that (3) ______ a symbol of pollution and neglect for decades. Workers removed tonnes of rubbish, and the stalls (4) ______ had crowded the banks were moved to a nearby market. The water is still far from clean, but the canal now attracts tourists, artists and families, (5) ______ come to enjoy the walking paths and colourful murals.\n\nSupporters say projects like this can (6) ______ the city cooler during the hot season, because water and trees lower the air temperature. Critics, however, point out that cleaning one canal is easy compared (7) ______ changing the habits of millions of residents who still pour waste into the water. If people (8) ______ these habits, they argue, even the most beautiful restoration will not last.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["which","that","who","whom"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"After a comma the clause refers to people (tourists, artists and families) and is the subject of \"come\", so \"who\". \"That\" cannot follow a comma, \"which\" is for things, and \"whom\" is an object form.","th":"หลังจุลภาคอนุประโยคนี้หมายถึงคน (tourists, artists, families) และเป็นประธานของ come จึงใช้ who ส่วน that ใช้หลังจุลภาคไม่ได้ which ใช้กับสิ่งของ และ whom ใช้เป็นกรรม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main purpose of an article","th":"การหาจุดประสงค์หลักของบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"Why Teenagers Can't Fall Asleep Early\n\nMany parents believe that teenagers stay up late simply because they are addicted to their phones or would rather chat with friends than rest. While screens certainly play a part, scientists now say that the main reason is biological. During puberty, the body begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, around two hours later than it did in childhood. As a result, a typical fifteen-year-old does not feel tired until eleven o'clock or even midnight, no matter how early he or she goes to bed.\n\nThis shift would not be a serious problem if teenagers could sleep later in the morning. Unfortunately, most secondary schools start their day between seven and eight o'clock, which means many students have to wake up at six or earlier. Doctors recommend that people aged thirteen to eighteen get between eight and ten hours of sleep a night, yet surveys in several countries show that fewer than one in three actually do. The missing hours add up quickly, and researchers call the result \"sleep debt\".\n\nSleep debt affects much more than how tired students feel. Studies have linked it to poorer memory, slower reaction times and difficulty controlling emotions. In some experiments, students who were short of sleep remembered far less of what they had learned the previous day than classmates who had slept well. There is also evidence that tired teenagers are more likely to have accidents on the road.\n\nIn response, a number of school districts in the United States have pushed back their start times to half past eight or later. The results have been encouraging: attendance has risen, fewer students fall asleep in class, and in some schools exam results have improved. Critics argue that later starts cause problems for parents who work early and for after-school sports, which must finish later in the evening. Nevertheless, supporters insist that these difficulties can be solved, whereas the effects of losing sleep cannot.\n\nFor students who cannot change their school timetable, experts suggest a few simple habits: keeping the same bedtime at weekends, avoiding bright screens for an hour before bed, and not drinking coffee or energy drinks after lunch.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the article?","choices":["To explain why teenagers sleep late and how this affects them","To argue that teenagers should stop using phones at night","To describe how the body produces melatonin in children","To show that American schools are better than other schools"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The article explains the biological cause of late sleep, the effects of sleep debt and possible responses. Phones are mentioned only as a minor factor, melatonin is one detail, and no school system is ranked.","th":"บทความอธิบายสาเหตุทางชีววิทยาที่วัยรุ่นนอนดึก ผลของการนอนไม่พอ และแนวทางแก้ไข ส่วนโทรศัพท์ถูกกล่าวถึงเป็นปัจจัยรองเท่านั้น เมลาโทนินเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด และไม่ได้จัดอันดับโรงเรียน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying eligibility conditions","th":"การนำเงื่อนไขคุณสมบัติไปใช้"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: notice and email","th":"การอ่าน: ประกาศและอีเมล"},"passage":"Subject: Summer Exchange Programme – application details\n\nDear Matthayom 4 students,\n\nOur partner school in Nagoya, Japan, has invited eight students from our school to join its Summer Science Camp from 12 to 26 July. Students who take part will live with Japanese host families, attend morning classes in English, and spend the afternoons on field trips to research centres, factories and a national park.\n\nTo be eligible, you must:\n• be a Matthayom 4 student in the English Program or the GATE Program in the current academic year;\n• have a grade point average of at least 3.25 in the last semester;\n• have no record of serious disciplinary problems.\n\nApplications must include a one-page letter in English explaining why you want to join and what you hope to contribute, a recommendation from your homeroom teacher, and a consent form signed by a parent or guardian. Please note that letters longer than one page will not be read.\n\nThe partner school will cover tuition, meals at school and all field-trip costs. Families will be responsible for airfare (approximately 18,000 baht), travel insurance and personal spending money. A limited number of grants are available for students whose families would find these costs difficult; if you wish to apply for one, tick the box on the consent form and our finance office will contact you privately.\n\nShortlisted applicants will be interviewed in English on 15 March. The interview will last about ten minutes and will include a short discussion of a science news article, which you will be given twenty minutes to read beforehand. Dictionaries will not be allowed.\n\nCompleted applications should be handed in to Room 305 no later than 4:00 p.m. on Friday, 28 February. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered under any circumstances.\n\nIf you have any questions, please contact me by email rather than by phone, as I am teaching for most of the day.\n\nBest wishes,\nMs Kanokwan Thepsiri\nInternational Programmes Coordinator","table":null,"stem":"Which student is eligible to apply?","choices":["A Matthayom 5 English Program student with a GPA of 3.80","A Matthayom 4 GATE student with a GPA of 3.40","A Matthayom 4 regular-programme student with a GPA of 3.50","A Matthayom 4 English Program student with a GPA of 3.10"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Applicants must be in Matthayom 4, in the EP or GATE Program, with a GPA of at least 3.25. Only the GATE student with 3.40 meets all three; the others are in the wrong year, the wrong programme, or below 3.25.","th":"ผู้สมัครต้องเรียน ม.4 ในโครงการ EP หรือ GATE และมีเกรดเฉลี่ยอย่างน้อย 3.25 นักเรียน GATE ที่ได้ 3.40 จึงผ่านทุกเงื่อนไข คนอื่นผิดชั้นปี ผิดโครงการ หรือเกรดต่ำกว่า 3.25"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":2,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"sk4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Agreeing to a request for help","th":"การตอบรับคำขอความช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Could you give me a hand with these boxes? They're heavier than they look.\nB: ______ Where do you want them?","choices":["No, I couldn't.","Help yourself.","Give me a break.","Sure, no problem."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"B then asks where to put the boxes, so B is agreeing: \"Sure, no problem\". \"No, I couldn't\" refuses, \"Help yourself\" invites someone to take something, and \"Give me a break\" means stop bothering me.","th":"B ถามต่อว่าจะให้วางกล่องที่ไหน แสดงว่ายินดีช่วย จึงตอบ Sure, no problem ส่วน No, I couldn't เป็นการปฏิเสธ Help yourself ใช้เชิญให้หยิบเอง และ Give me a break แปลว่าอย่ากวนใจ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Accepting criticism","th":"การยอมรับคำวิจารณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: You look exhausted. Have you been up all night?\nB: Pretty much. I had to finish my history report.\nA: Again? You really should stop leaving things until the last minute.\nB: ______ I'll start the next one the day it's set.","choices":["Mind your own business.","Speak for yourself.","So do I.","Point taken."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"B promises to start earlier next time, so B accepts the criticism: \"Point taken\" (= I understand and accept what you said). \"Mind your own business\" rejects it rudely, \"Speak for yourself\" says the comment applies to A, and \"So do I\" makes no sense here.","th":"B สัญญาว่าจะเริ่มทำงานเร็วขึ้น แสดงว่ายอมรับคำติ จึงตอบ Point taken (รับทราบและยอมรับ) ส่วน Mind your own business เป็นการปฏิเสธอย่างหยาบ Speak for yourself หมายถึงคนพูดเองต่างหาก และ So do I ไม่เข้ากับบริบท"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Collocations with 'decision'","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมกับ decision"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"We need to ______ a decision about the class trip by Friday.\"","choices":["do","make","give","put"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The usual collocation is \"make a decision\". Thai learners often choose \"do\", but we do homework or a job, not a decision; \"give\" and \"put\" do not collocate with decision.","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันคือ make a decision ผู้เรียนไทยมักใช้ do แต่ do ใช้กับ homework หรือ job ส่วน give และ put ไม่ใช้กับ decision"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: run into","th":"กริยาวลี run into"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I ran ______ an old classmate at the shopping centre yesterday; I hadn't seen him for years.\"","choices":["out of","into","over","up"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Run into someone\" means meet them by chance. \"Run out of\" means have none left, \"run over\" means hit with a vehicle, and \"run up\" means build up a debt or run towards.","th":"run into someone แปลว่าเจอโดยบังเอิญ ส่วน run out of แปลว่าหมด run over แปลว่าขับรถทับ และ run up แปลว่าก่อหนี้หรือวิ่งเข้าไปหา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"'Be going to' for predictions from evidence","th":"be going to สำหรับการคาดการณ์จากหลักฐาน"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Look at those dark clouds! It ______ rain any minute.\"","choices":["'s going to","will be","rains","is raining"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"We can see evidence now (dark clouds), so we use \"be going to\" for the prediction: \"It's going to rain\". \"Will be rain\" is ungrammatical, \"rains\" is a habit, and \"is raining\" means it has already started.","th":"เห็นหลักฐานในปัจจุบัน (เมฆดำ) จึงใช้ be going to ในการคาดการณ์ คือ It's going to rain ส่วน will be rain ผิดไวยากรณ์ rains เป็นนิสัยหรือความจริงทั่วไป และ is raining หมายถึงฝนตกอยู่แล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Causative: get something done","th":"causative get something done"},"topic":{"en":"Passive and causative","th":"ประโยค passive และ causative"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I'm going to ______ before the graduation ceremony.\"","choices":["get my hair cut","get cut my hair","get my hair to cut","have cut my hair"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A hairdresser will do it, so we use get + object + past participle: \"get my hair cut\". The object must come before the participle, \"to cut\" is wrong, and \"have cut my hair\" means I cut it myself (perfect tense).","th":"ช่างตัดผมเป็นผู้ตัดให้ จึงใช้ get + กรรม + V3 คือ get my hair cut กรรมต้องอยู่หน้า V3 ส่วน to cut ผิด และ have cut my hair เป็น perfect tense หมายถึงตัดเอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Agreement with 'each of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องเมื่อใช้ each of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nAfter they [[had completed]] the [[three-day]] workshop, each of the students [[were]] given [[a certificate]].","choices":["had completed","three-day","were","a certificate"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Each of the students\" is singular, so the verb must be \"was given\", not \"were given\".","th":"each of the students เป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น was given ไม่ใช่ were given"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past simple with finished time expressions","th":"past simple กับคำบอกเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nFirefighters [[are]] still [[investigating]] the cause of the fire that [[has destroyed]] the market [[last]] week.","choices":["are","investigating","has destroyed","last"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Last week\" is a finished past time, which cannot be used with the present perfect, so it should be \"destroyed\".","th":"last week เป็นเวลาในอดีตที่จบไปแล้ว ใช้กับ present perfect ไม่ได้ จึงต้องเป็น destroyed"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: verbs with dependent prepositions","th":"การเติมคำ: กริยาที่ต้องมีบุพบทตาม"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Millions of people around the world now (1) ______ language-learning apps, hoping that a few minutes a day will make them fluent. The appeal is easy to understand: the lessons are short, colourful and often free, and the apps reward users with points and badges (2) ______ they complete a lesson. But can an app really teach you a language?\n\nResearch suggests the answer is \"partly\". Apps are excellent for building vocabulary, because they show words again and again until they (3) ______ in long-term memory. They are less effective, (4) ______, at developing speaking skills. Talking to a phone is very different from having a real conversation, (5) ______ you must understand different accents, react quickly and deal with unexpected questions.\n\nAnother problem is motivation. Many users start with great enthusiasm but give up within a month. Some experts believe that the game-like features of apps can actually (6) ______ this problem, since learners begin to care more about keeping their daily \"streak\" than about understanding the language.\n\nThe most successful learners, it seems, are (7) ______ who use apps as only one part of their routine. They also watch films, read simple books and, most importantly, look for chances to speak with real people. As one teacher put it, \"An app can open the door, but you still have to (8) ______ through it yourself.\"","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["depend","count","believe","rely on"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Rely on\" takes an object (apps) and means depend on them. \"Depend\" and \"count\" need \"on\" before an object, and \"believe apps\" means think they are telling the truth.","th":"rely on ตามด้วยกรรม (apps) แปลว่าพึ่งพา ส่วน depend และ count ต้องมี on ก่อนกรรม และ believe apps แปลว่าเชื่อว่าแอปพูดความจริง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'where' for situations","th":"การเติมคำ: where ใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Millions of people around the world now (1) ______ language-learning apps, hoping that a few minutes a day will make them fluent. The appeal is easy to understand: the lessons are short, colourful and often free, and the apps reward users with points and badges (2) ______ they complete a lesson. But can an app really teach you a language?\n\nResearch suggests the answer is \"partly\". Apps are excellent for building vocabulary, because they show words again and again until they (3) ______ in long-term memory. They are less effective, (4) ______, at developing speaking skills. Talking to a phone is very different from having a real conversation, (5) ______ you must understand different accents, react quickly and deal with unexpected questions.\n\nAnother problem is motivation. Many users start with great enthusiasm but give up within a month. Some experts believe that the game-like features of apps can actually (6) ______ this problem, since learners begin to care more about keeping their daily \"streak\" than about understanding the language.\n\nThe most successful learners, it seems, are (7) ______ who use apps as only one part of their routine. They also watch films, read simple books and, most importantly, look for chances to speak with real people. As one teacher put it, \"An app can open the door, but you still have to (8) ______ through it yourself.\"","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["where","which","what","whom"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The clause after the gap is complete (you must understand…), and it describes a situation, a real conversation, so \"where\" (= in which) is right. \"Which\" and \"whom\" would need a missing subject or object, and \"what\" cannot follow a noun.","th":"อนุประโยคหลังช่องว่างสมบูรณ์แล้ว (you must understand…) และขยายสถานการณ์คือ a real conversation จึงใช้ where (= in which) ส่วน which และ whom ต้องมีประธานหรือกรรมที่หายไป และ what ใช้ตามหลังคำนามไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the best title","th":"การเลือกชื่อเรื่องที่เหมาะสมที่สุด"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"Farms in the Sky\n\nImagine a farm with no fields, no tractors and no sunshine. Instead, there are rows of shelves stacked twelve levels high inside a warehouse, each one covered with lettuce, basil or strawberries growing under purple LED lights. This is vertical farming, and its supporters believe it could change the way cities feed themselves.\n\nThe idea is simple. Rather than spreading crops across a large area of land, vertical farms grow them upwards, in a building where every condition can be controlled. Temperature, humidity and light are adjusted by computers twenty-four hours a day. Because the plants are grown indoors, there are few insects to fight, so farmers rarely need pesticides. Most vertical farms also use hydroponics, a method in which roots are fed by water containing nutrients rather than by soil. This system recycles the water, using up to ninety per cent less than a traditional farm.\n\nLocation is another advantage. A vertical farm can be built in the middle of a city, close to supermarkets and restaurants. Vegetables can be picked in the morning and sold the same afternoon, which means they are fresher and less food is wasted during transport.\n\nHowever, vertical farming is not the perfect solution it is sometimes made out to be. The biggest problem is energy. Sunlight is free, but LED lights and air-conditioning are not, and several well-funded vertical farming companies have gone out of business because their electricity bills were simply too high. Critics also point out that the method works well only for small, fast-growing plants such as salad leaves and herbs. Staple crops like rice, wheat and potatoes need far more space and light, and growing them indoors would be extremely expensive.\n\nFor now, then, vertical farms are unlikely to replace traditional agriculture. Instead, they may become a useful addition to it, especially in crowded, wealthy cities where land is scarce and consumers are willing to pay more for fresh, pesticide-free vegetables. If renewable energy becomes much cheaper, however, the picture could change dramatically.","table":null,"stem":"Which title best summarises the passage?","choices":["Vertical Farming: Promising but Limited","How to Grow Rice Indoors","The End of Traditional Agriculture","Why LED Lights Are Bad for Plants"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The passage presents advantages (water, pesticides, freshness) and serious limits (energy, crop types), concluding that vertical farms are a useful addition, not a replacement. It says rice is too expensive to grow indoors, rejects the end of traditional farming, and never says LEDs harm plants.","th":"บทความกล่าวทั้งข้อดี (ประหยัดน้ำ ไม่ใช้ยาฆ่าแมลง สดใหม่) และข้อจำกัด (พลังงาน ชนิดพืช) แล้วสรุปว่าเป็นส่วนเสริม ไม่ใช่การแทนที่ บทความบอกว่าการปลูกข้าวในอาคารแพงเกินไป ปฏิเสธว่าการเกษตรแบบดั้งเดิมจะหมดไป และไม่ได้บอกว่าไฟ LED เป็นผลเสียต่อพืช"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the purpose of an advertisement","th":"การระบุจุดประสงค์ของโฆษณา"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: notice and email","th":"การอ่าน: ประกาศและอีเมล"},"passage":"VOLUNTEERS WANTED\nCoastal Clean-Up Weekend — Saturday 14 and Sunday 15 June\n\nEvery year, tonnes of plastic wash up on the beaches south of Hua Hin, much of it carried by currents from rivers hundreds of kilometres away. This June, Blue Shore, a small non-profit group, is looking for 200 volunteers aged 14 and over to help clear a six-kilometre stretch of coastline.\n\nWhat you will do\nVolunteers will work in teams of ten, each led by an experienced team leader. As well as collecting rubbish, every team will sort and record what it finds, using a simple form on our mobile app. This information is shared with university researchers who are tracking where marine plastic comes from, so please be as accurate as you can—even a bottle cap counts!\n\nWhat we provide\nGloves, sacks, drinking water, sunscreen, lunch on both days and a free T-shirt. Volunteers who attend both days will also receive a certificate of participation, which many students have found useful for university applications.\n\nWhat you should bring\nA hat, a refillable water bottle, closed shoes (no sandals—broken glass is common) and a charged phone with our app installed.\n\nAccommodation and transport\nWe cannot provide accommodation. However, a free shuttle bus will leave from Hua Hin railway station at 6:30 a.m. each day and return at 4:30 p.m. Volunteers who miss the bus will need to make their own way to the meeting point, which is about 12 kilometres from the town centre.\n\nImportant\nVolunteers under 18 must be accompanied by a parent, teacher or other responsible adult, who is also welcome to volunteer. Work will stop at midday for two hours because of the heat. If there is a storm warning, the clean-up will be cancelled, and all registered volunteers will be informed by text message by 8:00 p.m. the evening before.\n\nTo register, scan the QR code below. Registration closes on 31 May or when all 200 places are filled, whichever comes first. Last year, all places were taken within ten days.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the advertisement?","choices":["To recruit people to help clean a beach","To sell T-shirts to raise money","To advertise hotels in Hua Hin","To report the results of university research"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The heading \"Volunteers wanted\" and the details about tasks and registration show it is recruiting volunteers. T-shirts are free, no accommodation is offered, and the research is only mentioned as a use of the data.","th":"หัวข้อ Volunteers wanted และรายละเอียดเรื่องงานและการลงทะเบียนแสดงว่าเป็นการรับอาสาสมัคร เสื้อยืดแจกฟรี ไม่มีที่พักให้ และงานวิจัยถูกกล่าวถึงเพียงว่านำข้อมูลไปใช้"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":3,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE and English Program) — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE และ EP) — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and English Program applicants take the same paper; EP applicants need a higher score. DeepDistrict practice format based on tutor summaries — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EP ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน ผู้สมัคร EP ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"sk4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to thanks","th":"การตอบรับคำขอบคุณ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Thanks so much for helping me move house last weekend.\nB: ______ That's what friends are for.","choices":["It doesn't matter.","Same here.","I'm afraid not.","Don't mention it."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Don't mention it\" is a standard reply to thanks. \"It doesn't matter\" answers an apology or a problem, \"Same here\" agrees with a feeling, and \"I'm afraid not\" is a polite no.","th":"Don't mention it เป็นคำตอบมาตรฐานเมื่อมีคนขอบคุณ ส่วน It doesn't matter ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษหรือบอกว่าไม่สำคัญ Same here ใช้บอกว่ารู้สึกเหมือนกัน และ I'm afraid not เป็นการปฏิเสธอย่างสุภาพ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Disagreeing in a formal register","th":"การแสดงความไม่เห็นด้วยแบบทางการ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Honestly, I don't see why we need to learn history. It's all in the past.\nB: ______ Understanding the past helps us avoid repeating the same mistakes.","choices":["I couldn't have put it better myself.","You took the words right out of my mouth.","Fair enough, you've got a point.","I'm afraid I'd have to disagree."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"B goes on to defend history, so B disagrees: \"I'm afraid I'd have to disagree\". The other three all agree with A, which contradicts B's next sentence.","th":"B กล่าวต่อเพื่อปกป้องวิชาประวัติศาสตร์ จึงไม่เห็นด้วย I'm afraid I'd have to disagree อีกสามตัวเลือกล้วนเห็นด้วยกับ A ซึ่งขัดกับประโยคถัดไปของ B"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: lay off","th":"กริยาวลี lay off"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The factory had to ______ two hundred workers when sales collapsed.\"","choices":["lay out","lay off","lay down","lay by"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Lay off\" means dismiss workers because there is not enough work, which matches collapsing sales. \"Lay out\" means arrange or spend, \"lay down\" means put down or establish rules, and \"lay by\" means save.","th":"lay off แปลว่าเลิกจ้างเพราะงานไม่พอ ตรงกับยอดขายตก ส่วน lay out แปลว่าจัดวางหรือใช้จ่าย lay down แปลว่าวางลงหรือกำหนดกฎ และ lay by แปลว่าเก็บออม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Meaning of formal adjectives in context","th":"ความหมายของคุณศัพท์ทางการตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning and formation","th":"ความหมายคำและการสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the meaning closest to \"ambiguous\" in the sentence.\n\"The minister's statement was deliberately ambiguous, so journalists could not tell whether the policy would change.\"","choices":["extremely detailed","openly hostile","open to more than one interpretation","completely false"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Journalists could not tell what the policy would be, so the statement could be understood in different ways: \"ambiguous\" means open to more than one interpretation. Detail, hostility or falseness would not cause this uncertainty.","th":"นักข่าวบอกไม่ได้ว่านโยบายจะเปลี่ยนหรือไม่ แสดงว่าคำแถลงตีความได้หลายทาง ambiguous จึงแปลว่ากำกวม ความละเอียด ความเป็นปรปักษ์ หรือความเท็จไม่ได้ทำให้เกิดความไม่แน่ใจแบบนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inverted third conditional","th":"เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 รูปสลับที่"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Had the government acted sooner, the flooding ______ so severe.\"","choices":["would not have been","would not be","will not be","had not been"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Had the government acted\" = \"If the government had acted\", an unreal past condition about a past flood, so the result is \"would not have been\". \"Would not be\" refers to the present, and the other forms are not conditional results.","th":"Had the government acted มีความหมายเท่ากับ If the government had acted เป็นเงื่อนไขที่ไม่จริงในอดีตเกี่ยวกับน้ำท่วมที่เกิดไปแล้ว ผลจึงเป็น would not have been ส่วน would not be หมายถึงปัจจุบัน และรูปอื่นไม่ใช่รูปผลของเงื่อนไข"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Mixed conditional: past condition, present result","th":"mixed conditional เงื่อนไขอดีต ผลปัจจุบัน"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"If I hadn't stayed up so late last night, I ______ so tired now.\"","choices":["wouldn't have been","won't be","hadn't been","wouldn't be"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The condition is past (hadn't stayed up last night) but the result is now, so this mixed conditional uses \"wouldn't be\". \"Wouldn't have been\" refers to a past result, and the other forms break the pattern.","th":"เงื่อนไขอยู่ในอดีต (hadn't stayed up last night) แต่ผลเป็นปัจจุบัน (now) จึงใช้ mixed conditional คือ wouldn't be ส่วน wouldn't have been หมายถึงผลในอดีต และรูปอื่นผิดโครงสร้าง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"'No sooner … than'","th":"No sooner … than"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nNo sooner [[had]] the teacher left the room [[when]] the students [[started]] [[talking]] loudly.","choices":["had","when","started","talking"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"No sooner\" is paired with \"than\", not \"when\": \"No sooner had the teacher left the room than…\". (\"Hardly/Scarcely\" go with \"when\".)","th":"No sooner ต้องคู่กับ than ไม่ใช่ when คือ No sooner had the teacher left the room than… (ส่วน Hardly/Scarcely คู่กับ when)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Prepositions of time: by vs until","th":"บุพบทบอกเวลา by กับ until"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, modals and reported speech","th":"รูปแบบกริยา modal และคำพูดรายงาน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nThe documents [[must]] be signed by both parents [[and]] [[handed]] to the school office [[until]] Friday.","choices":["must","and","handed","until"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A deadline uses \"by\" (no later than), not \"until\", which describes a continuing action: \"handed to the school office by Friday\".","th":"กำหนดส่งใช้ by (ไม่เกิน) ไม่ใช่ until ซึ่งใช้กับการกระทำที่ต่อเนื่องไปจนถึงเวลานั้น จึงต้องเป็น by Friday"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: reduced passive clauses","th":"การเติมคำ: อนุประโยค passive แบบลดรูป"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"For centuries, doctors have known that patients sometimes feel better after receiving a treatment that, chemically speaking, should do nothing at all. This phenomenon, (1) ______ as the placebo effect, is one of the strangest puzzles in medicine. In clinical trials, a sugar pill can reduce pain, nausea or anxiety in a surprising number of patients, provided they believe it is real medicine.\n\nScientists once dismissed the effect as (2) ______ imagination, but brain scans have shown that it produces measurable physical changes. When patients expect relief from pain, the brain releases natural chemicals which, according to several studies, (3) ______ in much the same way as some painkillers. Expectation, in other words, is not just a feeling; it can alter the body itself.\n\nEven more surprising is a series of experiments on \"open-label\" placebos. In these studies, patients were told honestly that the pills they (4) ______ contained no active ingredient. (5) ______ this, many of them still reported feeling better. Researchers think that the ritual of taking medicine—swallowing a pill at the same time every day, being cared for by a doctor—may be (6) ______ triggers the response.\n\nThe placebo effect has a darker twin: the nocebo effect. Patients who are warned about possible side effects sometimes develop those very symptoms, (7) ______ they have been given a harmless pill. This raises a difficult ethical question for doctors: how can they be honest about risks without making patients ill? Some suggest that the answer lies not in hiding information but in the way it is (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["knowing","to know","known","is known"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Known as\" is a reduced passive relative clause (= which is known as). \"Knowing\" is active, \"to know\" does not fit, and \"is known\" would create a second main verb in the sentence.","th":"known as เป็นอนุประโยค passive แบบลดรูป (= which is known as) ส่วน knowing เป็น active to know ไม่เข้ากับโครงสร้าง และ is known จะทำให้ประโยคมีกริยาแท้สองตัว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'despite' + noun phrase","th":"การเติมคำ: despite + นามวลี"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"For centuries, doctors have known that patients sometimes feel better after receiving a treatment that, chemically speaking, should do nothing at all. This phenomenon, (1) ______ as the placebo effect, is one of the strangest puzzles in medicine. In clinical trials, a sugar pill can reduce pain, nausea or anxiety in a surprising number of patients, provided they believe it is real medicine.\n\nScientists once dismissed the effect as (2) ______ imagination, but brain scans have shown that it produces measurable physical changes. When patients expect relief from pain, the brain releases natural chemicals which, according to several studies, (3) ______ in much the same way as some painkillers. Expectation, in other words, is not just a feeling; it can alter the body itself.\n\nEven more surprising is a series of experiments on \"open-label\" placebos. In these studies, patients were told honestly that the pills they (4) ______ contained no active ingredient. (5) ______ this, many of them still reported feeling better. Researchers think that the ritual of taking medicine—swallowing a pill at the same time every day, being cared for by a doctor—may be (6) ______ triggers the response.\n\nThe placebo effect has a darker twin: the nocebo effect. Patients who are warned about possible side effects sometimes develop those very symptoms, (7) ______ they have been given a harmless pill. This raises a difficult ethical question for doctors: how can they be honest about risks without making patients ill? Some suggest that the answer lies not in hiding information but in the way it is (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["Although","In spite","Even though","Despite"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Before a noun or pronoun (this) we need a preposition: \"Despite this\". \"Although\" and \"even though\" need a clause, and \"in spite\" needs \"of\".","th":"หน้าคำนามหรือสรรพนาม (this) ต้องใช้บุพบท คือ Despite this ส่วน Although, Even though ต้องตามด้วยอนุประโยค และ In spite ต้องมี of"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การหาใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"The Disappearing Night\n\nFor most of human history, the night sky was one of the great shared experiences of our species. On a clear night, almost anyone could look up and see thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way. Today, more than a third of the world's population can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live, and in some heavily developed regions the figure is far higher. The cause is light pollution: the excessive or badly directed artificial light that now spills from streetlights, buildings, billboards and car parks into the sky.\n\nAstronomers were among the first to raise the alarm, since a bright sky makes faint objects impossible to observe. But the consequences extend well beyond science. Many animals depend on the natural cycle of light and darkness to find food, navigate and reproduce. Newly hatched sea turtles, for example, find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, which on an undeveloped beach is the moonlit sea. On a beach backed by hotels, however, the hatchlings often crawl inland, where they die of exhaustion or are caught by predators. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, are drawn towards lit-up towers, and large numbers are killed each year in collisions.\n\nHumans are affected too. Exposure to bright light at night, particularly the blue-rich light produced by many LEDs, suppresses the production of melatonin, the hormone that regulates sleep. Some researchers have suggested links between long-term exposure and health problems, although these connections are not yet fully understood.\n\nUnlike many environmental problems, light pollution is remarkably easy to reverse. A polluted river may take decades to recover, but a light that is switched off stops polluting instantly. Simple measures—shielding streetlights so that they point downwards, using warmer colours, and dimming or turning off lights that are not needed late at night—can dramatically reduce the problem while also cutting energy bills. Several towns around the world have gained \"dark sky\" status by adopting such rules, and some now attract tourists who come specifically to see the stars.\n\nThe real obstacle, then, is not technology but awareness. Few people think of light as a pollutant at all; for most of us, a brightly lit street simply feels safe and modern. Changing that perception may prove harder than changing the bulbs.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","choices":["LED lights should be banned in all cities.","Astronomers are the main victims of light pollution.","Light pollution harms nature and people but could be reduced fairly easily.","Dark-sky towns have become popular tourist destinations."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The passage describes effects on astronomy, animals and humans, then stresses that the problem is \"remarkably easy to reverse\". It never calls for an LED ban, says the consequences go beyond astronomers, and mentions dark-sky tourism only as a detail.","th":"บทความอธิบายผลกระทบต่อดาราศาสตร์ สัตว์ และมนุษย์ แล้วเน้นว่าปัญหานี้แก้ไขได้ง่ายมาก บทความไม่ได้เรียกร้องให้ห้ามใช้ LED บอกว่าผลกระทบไปไกลกว่านักดาราศาสตร์ และกล่าวถึงการท่องเที่ยวชมดาวเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding rules in formal correspondence","th":"การเข้าใจกฎในจดหมายทางการ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: notice and email","th":"การอ่าน: ประกาศและอีเมล"},"passage":"Subject: Re: Request for refund – Young Engineers Summer Programme\n\nDear Mr Phanuwat,\n\nThank you for your email of 3 May regarding your son Thanakorn's place on the Young Engineers Summer Programme. I was sorry to hear that he has broken his wrist and will be unable to take part in the practical workshops.\n\nI have looked carefully at your request for a full refund of the 15,500-baht programme fee. As you may be aware, our terms and conditions, which were accepted at the time of booking, state that cancellations made less than 30 days before the start date are not normally eligible for a refund. As the programme begins on 20 May, your cancellation falls within this period.\n\nHowever, we recognise that your son's situation was entirely beyond his control, and we are keen to find a fair solution. I would therefore like to offer you a choice of the following options:\n\n1. Thanakorn could transfer his place, free of charge, to our next programme, which runs from 14 to 25 October. The content will be identical, and he would join a group of students of a similar age.\n\n2. Thanakorn could attend the theory sessions in May, which do not require the use of his hands, and receive a partial refund of 6,000 baht for the workshops he will miss. Please note that he would not receive the programme certificate, as this requires completion of all practical tasks.\n\n3. We could refund 50 per cent of the fee (7,750 baht), provided we receive a medical certificate from his doctor.\n\nIf none of these options is acceptable, you are, of course, entitled to make a formal appeal to the Faculty's Student Affairs Committee, which meets once a month. I should point out, though, that appeals of this kind are rarely successful when a reasonable alternative has already been offered.\n\nI would be grateful if you could let me know which option you prefer by 15 May, so that we can offer any unused place to a student on our waiting list.\n\nWith best wishes for Thanakorn's recovery,\n\nDr Sirinya Chaiyaporn\nProgramme Director","table":null,"stem":"Why is Mr Phanuwat not automatically entitled to a full refund?","choices":["He cancelled less than 30 days before the programme starts.","He did not accept the terms and conditions when booking.","The programme has already started.","He did not send a medical certificate."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The terms say cancellations less than 30 days before the start (20 May) are not normally refunded, and his email came on 3 May. He did accept the terms, the programme has not started, and the medical certificate is only needed for the 50 per cent option.","th":"เงื่อนไขระบุว่าการยกเลิกน้อยกว่า 30 วันก่อนเริ่มโครงการ (20 พฤษภาคม) โดยปกติจะไม่ได้รับเงินคืน และอีเมลของเขาส่งมาวันที่ 3 พฤษภาคม เขายอมรับเงื่อนไขแล้ว โครงการยังไม่เริ่ม และใบรับรองแพทย์ใช้เฉพาะกรณีขอคืนเงินครึ่งหนึ่ง"}}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":1,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, weighted like the Triam Udom paper towards cloze, reading and error identification (tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years; ours is a longer practice paper). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที เน้นเติมคำในบทความ การอ่าน และหาจุดผิด ตามแนวข้อสอบเตรียมอุดมฯ (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ ชุดนี้เป็นแบบฝึกที่ยาวกว่า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":true,"file":"tu4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to an apology or excuse","th":"การตอบรับคำขอโทษหรือข้ออ้าง"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I'm afraid I can't make it to the study group tonight — my grandmother is visiting.\nB: ______ We'll fill you in tomorrow.","explanation":{"en":"'No worries' is a friendly way to accept someone's excuse, and B then offers to update them. 'You're welcome' and 'Don't mention it' answer thanks, not an apology; 'It doesn't matter for me' is a common error (we say 'to me').","th":"'No worries' ใช้ตอบรับเมื่ออีกฝ่ายขอโทษหรือบอกว่ามาไม่ได้ แล้ว B ก็เสนอจะเล่าให้ฟังพรุ่งนี้ ส่วน 'You're welcome' และ 'Don't mention it' ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ ไม่ใช่ตอบคำขอโทษ และ 'It doesn't matter for me' ผิด (ต้องเป็น to me)"},"choices":["No worries.","You're welcome.","Don't mention it.","It doesn't matter for me."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Reassuring someone","th":"การปลอบใจ ให้กำลังใจ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: How did your presentation go?\nB: Honestly, I was so nervous that my mind went blank halfway through.\nA: ______ Everyone forgets a line now and then.","explanation":{"en":"A goes on to say that everyone forgets things, so A is comforting B: 'Don't be too hard on yourself.' 'You should have been more careful' blames B; 'Break a leg!' wishes someone luck before a performance; 'That's a piece of cake' means something is easy.","th":"ประโยคต่อมาของ A บอกว่าใคร ๆ ก็ลืมได้ แสดงว่า A กำลังปลอบใจ จึงตอบ 'Don't be too hard on yourself.' (อย่าโทษตัวเองมากเกินไป) ส่วน 'You should have been more careful' เป็นการตำหนิ 'Break a leg!' ใช้อวยพรก่อนขึ้นแสดง และ 'That's a piece of cake' แปลว่าง่ายมาก"},"choices":["You should have been more careful.","Don't be too hard on yourself.","That's a piece of cake.","Break a leg!"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Verb + object + to-infinitive","th":"กริยา + กรรม + to-infinitive"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"The new policy is designed to ______ students to read more widely by giving them points for every book they review.","explanation":{"en":"'Encourage someone to do something' fits the pattern and the meaning. 'Suggest' cannot be followed by 'someone to do' (a common error), 'insist' needs 'on', and 'prevent' means the opposite and takes 'from + -ing'.","th":"ใช้ 'encourage + คน + to + V' (สนับสนุนให้ทำ) ได้ทั้งรูปและความหมาย ส่วน suggest ใช้รูป suggest someone to do ไม่ได้ insist ต้องตามด้วย on และ prevent มีความหมายตรงข้ามและใช้ prevent … from + V-ing"},"choices":["suggest","encourage","insist","prevent"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs with 'give'","th":"กริยาวลีกับ give"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Many villagers had to ______ fishing when the river became polluted, and most of them turned to farming instead.","explanation":{"en":"'Give up' means stop doing something, which matches 'turned to farming instead'. 'Give in' means surrender, 'give off' means produce a smell or heat, and 'give away' means give something for free.","th":"give up แปลว่าเลิกทำ เข้ากับที่ว่าหันไปทำนาแทน ส่วน give in แปลว่ายอมแพ้ give off แปลว่าปล่อยกลิ่นหรือความร้อนออกมา และ give away แปลว่าแจกให้ฟรี"},"choices":["give in","give off","give away","give up"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Past perfect for an earlier past action","th":"past perfect กับเหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Tenses","th":"กาล (Tense)"},"passage":"","stem":"By the time the ambulance arrived, the injured man ______ consciousness.","explanation":{"en":"'By the time + past simple' shows that the other action was completed earlier, so the past perfect 'had already lost' is needed.","th":"'By the time + past simple' บอกว่าอีกเหตุการณ์หนึ่งเสร็จไปก่อนแล้ว จึงใช้ past perfect คือ had already lost"},"choices":["was already losing","has already lost","already loses","had already lost"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Despite vs although","th":"Despite กับ although"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses and connectors","th":"อนุประโยคและคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"______ the heavy traffic, we reached the airport in time for our flight.","explanation":{"en":"A noun phrase ('the heavy traffic') follows, so we need a preposition: 'Despite'. 'Although' needs a clause, 'In spite' needs 'of', and 'However' is an adverb that cannot join a noun to a clause.","th":"ตามด้วยนามวลี the heavy traffic จึงต้องใช้บุพบท Despite ส่วน Although ต้องตามด้วยประโยค In spite ต้องมี of และ However เป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์ที่เชื่อมนามวลีกับประโยคไม่ได้"},"choices":["Although","However","In spite","Despite"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with 'every one of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา: every one of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and word order","th":"ความสอดคล้องและการเรียงคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every one of the students [[who]] [[took]] the test [[were]] given a certificate [[at]] the end of the course.","explanation":{"en":"The subject is 'every one', which is singular, so the verb must be 'was given', not 'were given'.","th":"ประธานคือ every one ซึ่งเป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น was given ไม่ใช่ were given"},"choices":["who","took","were","at"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Dangling participle","th":"participle ที่ประธานไม่ตรงกัน (dangling participle)"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses and connectors","th":"อนุประโยคและคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[Having finished]] the essay, the computer [[suddenly]] crashed and she [[lost]] [[everything]].","explanation":{"en":"A participle clause takes the subject of the main clause, so this says the computer finished the essay. It should be 'After she had finished the essay, the computer …'.","th":"วลี participle ต้องมีประธานเดียวกับประโยคหลัก ประโยคนี้จึงแปลว่าคอมพิวเตอร์เขียนเรียงความเสร็จ ต้องแก้เป็น After she had finished the essay, the computer …"},"choices":["Having finished","suddenly","lost","everything"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Fixed phrase: known as","th":"วลีตายตัว known as"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Cities are often several degrees warmer than the countryside around them, a phenomenon (1) ______ as the 'urban heat island' effect. The main cause is simple: concrete and asphalt absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night, (2) ______ trees and soil stay relatively cool. In a city like Bangkok, where temperatures already go above 35°C for much of the year, even a small rise can have serious (3) ______ for health, especially among the elderly and outdoor workers. Planners have proposed several solutions. One is to paint roofs white, which reflects sunlight (4) ______ absorbing it. Another is to plant more trees along streets, since their shade and the water they release through their leaves can lower nearby temperatures noticeably. (5) ______, green spaces are expensive to maintain, and land in city centres is scarce and valuable. Some experts therefore argue that the most effective approach is to combine many small changes — cooler roofs, shaded bus stops, more fountains and canals — (6) ______ relying on a single large project that may take decades to finish.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","explanation":{"en":"'Known as' is the fixed phrase for giving a name. 'Called' and 'named' are not followed by 'as', and 'said as' is not English.","th":"known as เป็นวลีตายตัวที่ใช้บอกชื่อเรียก ส่วน called และ named ไม่ใช้ตามด้วย as และ said as ไม่มีใช้"},"choices":["said","named","called","known"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verb: stay up","th":"กริยาวลี stay up"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Many parents complain that their teenage children (7) ______ up late at night and refuse to get out of bed in the morning. It is tempting to blame laziness, but scientists now believe the problem is (8) ______ biological. During adolescence, the body begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, about two hours later than it (9) ______ in childhood. As a result, a typical sixteen-year-old may simply not feel tired until close to midnight, and one who is sent to bed earlier often lies awake for hours, which only adds to the frustration. If school starts at half past seven, that student is likely to be (10) ______ of sleep every weekday. The effects go beyond tiredness: too little sleep has also been linked to low mood and difficulty concentrating. Research in several countries has shown that when schools delay their start time by even forty-five minutes, attendance improves and students (11) ______ better in morning tests. Critics point out that a later start causes problems for parents' working hours and for after-school sports and clubs. Nevertheless, a growing number of education authorities are (12) ______ that the benefits outweigh the inconvenience.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (7)?","explanation":{"en":"'Stay up late' means not go to bed. 'Wake up late' and 'get up late' describe mornings, which would not fit 'late at night'.","th":"stay up late แปลว่านอนดึก ส่วน wake up และ get up หมายถึงตื่นนอน ซึ่งไม่เข้ากับ late at night"},"choices":["stay","keep","get","wake"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Main idea","th":"ใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่านบทความ"},"passage":"In a mountain on a remote Arctic island, a tunnel leads to a set of frozen chambers holding more than a million packets of seeds. The facility is often called a 'doomsday vault', but the people who run it dislike the name. Its purpose, they say, is not to prepare for the end of the world but to protect against the far more ordinary losses that happen every year.\n\nAround the world there are more than 1,700 seed banks, most of them national or regional. They store samples of crops such as rice, wheat and maize, along with their wild relatives. These collections matter because modern agriculture depends on a surprisingly small number of varieties. If a new disease attacks one of them, plant breeders need to find genes for resistance, and the most likely place to find them is in older or wild types of the same plant. A seed bank is, in effect, a library of genetic possibilities.\n\nYet many of these libraries are fragile. Some have lost their collections to floods, power cuts or simple lack of funding; others have been damaged by war. In 2015, researchers in the Middle East became the first to withdraw seeds from the Arctic vault after fighting forced them to abandon their own gene bank. Using the copies they had deposited years earlier, they were able to rebuild much of their collection in new locations.\n\nStoring seeds is not as simple as putting them in a freezer. Each sample must be dried carefully, sealed in foil packets and kept at around minus eighteen degrees Celsius. Even then, seeds do not last for ever. Their ability to germinate declines over time, so staff must regularly test samples and grow fresh plants to produce new seed. For some species, such as coconuts and many tropical fruits, freezing does not work at all, because their seeds die when they are dried. These must be conserved as living plants in fields or as tissue samples in laboratories.\n\nCritics argue that seed banks can create a false sense of security. A frozen seed, they point out, is cut off from the changing climate and the evolving pests that a plant in a farmer's field must adapt to. For this reason, many scientists believe that vaults should complement, not replace, the work of farmers who continue to grow traditional varieties.","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","explanation":{"en":"The text explains why seed banks matter (genetic resources, rebuilding lost collections) and then their limits (storage problems, seeds that cannot be frozen, a false sense of security). The vault and the war are only examples.","th":"บทความอธิบายว่าธนาคารเมล็ดพันธุ์สำคัญอย่างไร (แหล่งพันธุกรรม การกู้คืนคอลเลกชัน) และมีข้อจำกัดอะไร (การเก็บรักษา เมล็ดที่แช่แข็งไม่ได้ ความรู้สึกปลอดภัยที่ไม่จริง) ส่วนห้องนิรภัยและสงครามเป็นเพียงตัวอย่าง"},"choices":["How war in the Middle East destroyed the world's food supply","How an Arctic vault was built to survive a global disaster","Why farmers should stop growing traditional crop varieties","Why seed banks are valuable and what limits their usefulness"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Reference and paraphrase","th":"การอ้างถึงและการถอดความ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่านเรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"Ploy had never intended to enter the debate competition. Her English teacher, Mr Anan, had simply written her name on the list and told her afterwards, as if he were doing her a favour. 'You argue with me in every lesson,' he said. 'You might as well get a certificate for it.'\n\nThe motion was announced two weeks before the contest: 'Homework should be abolished.' Ploy's team was told to oppose it, which struck her as deeply unfair. She had complained about homework for most of her life, and now she was expected to defend it in front of an audience of strangers.\n\nFor the first few days she barely prepared. She told herself that the other team had the easier side and that losing would not be her fault. Then, one evening, she found her younger brother in tears over a page of fractions. She sat down to help him, intending to give him the answers so that they could both go to bed. Instead, she found herself asking him questions, and gradually he worked out the method himself. When he finished, he looked more pleased than she had seen him in weeks.\n\nPloy went to her room and opened her notebook. She did not write that homework was enjoyable, because she did not believe it. What she wrote was that practice done alone, without a teacher nearby, showed students what they actually understood rather than what they thought they understood. She added that the real problem was not homework itself but homework that was set without thought: pages of repetition that taught nothing new.\n\nOn the day of the competition her hands were shaking so badly that she left her notes on the table and spoke without them. She told the story of her brother and the fractions. She admitted that she had thought she was on the wrong side at first, and the audience laughed. Then she argued that abolishing homework would punish students like her brother, who needed quiet practice to build confidence, while doing nothing about the real issue — the quality of the tasks.\n\nHer team did not win. The judges praised their honesty but felt that the other side had used more evidence. On the bus home, Mr Anan asked if she was disappointed. Ploy thought for a moment. 'A little,' she said. 'But I think I changed my own mind, and that's harder than changing anyone else's.'","stem":"In paragraph 4, 'homework that was set without thought' refers to ______.","explanation":{"en":"The words after the colon explain it: 'pages of repetition that taught nothing new'. 'Without thought' describes the teacher who sets the work, not the students.","th":"คำหลังเครื่องหมาย : อธิบายไว้ว่าเป็น 'หน้ากระดาษที่ทำซ้ำ ๆ โดยไม่ได้สอนอะไรใหม่' และ without thought หมายถึงครูที่สั่งงานโดยไม่คิด ไม่ใช่นักเรียน"},"choices":["homework set only before competitions","repetitive tasks that do not teach anything new","tasks done alone without a teacher nearby","homework that students do without thinking"],"answer":1}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":2,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, weighted like the Triam Udom paper towards cloze, reading and error identification (tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years; ours is a longer practice paper). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที เน้นเติมคำในบทความ การอ่าน และหาจุดผิด ตามแนวข้อสอบเตรียมอุดมฯ (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ ชุดนี้เป็นแบบฝึกที่ยาวกว่า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"tu4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Agreeing to a request for help","th":"การตอบรับคำขอความช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Could you give me a hand with these boxes?\nB: ______ Where do you want them?","explanation":{"en":"'Give me a hand' means 'help me', and B's next question shows B is helping: 'Sure, no problem.' 'Give me your hand' takes the idiom literally, 'I'm afraid I can' is not a natural reply, and 'Help yourself' invites someone to take food or things.","th":"give me a hand แปลว่าช่วยหน่อย และคำถามต่อมาของ B แสดงว่ายินดีช่วย จึงตอบ 'Sure, no problem.' ส่วน 'give me your hand' แปลตามตัวอักษร 'I'm afraid I can' ไม่เป็นธรรมชาติ และ 'Help yourself' ใช้เชิญให้หยิบอาหารหรือของเอง"},"choices":["I'm afraid I can.","Sure, give me your hand.","Help yourself.","Sure, no problem."],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Offering to pay","th":"การเสนอเป็นฝ่ายจ่าย"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Shall we try that new Korean place for dinner?\nB: I'd love to, but I'm a bit short of cash this week.\nA: ______ It's my treat — you paid last time.","explanation":{"en":"A says 'It's my treat', meaning A will pay, so A tells B not to worry about money. 'Let's go Dutch' means each person pays for themselves, which contradicts 'my treat'; 'Keep the change' is said to a waiter or driver.","th":"A บอกว่า 'It's my treat' คือจะเลี้ยงเอง จึงบอกให้ B ไม่ต้องกังวลเรื่องเงิน ส่วน 'Let's go Dutch' แปลว่าต่างคนต่างจ่าย ซึ่งขัดกับการเลี้ยง และ 'Keep the change' พูดกับพนักงานหรือคนขับว่าไม่ต้องทอน"},"choices":["Keep the change.","Don't worry about that.","Let's go Dutch, then.","That's fair enough, let's not go."],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verb: figure out","th":"กริยาวลี figure out"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Scientists are still trying to ______ how the virus spreads from animals to humans.","explanation":{"en":"'Figure out' means understand or discover. 'Turn out' means prove to be, 'run out' means have none left, and 'make out of' means build from a material.","th":"figure out แปลว่าหาคำตอบ ทำความเข้าใจ ส่วน turn out แปลว่ากลายเป็น run out แปลว่าหมด และ make out of แปลว่าทำจากวัสดุ"},"choices":["figure out","run out","make out of","turn out"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Collocation: take responsibility","th":"คำปรากฏร่วม take responsibility"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"The minister refused to ______ responsibility for the failure of the project, blaming his advisers instead.","explanation":{"en":"The collocation is 'take responsibility for'. 'Make', 'do' and 'have' are not used with 'responsibility' in this meaning.","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมคือ take responsibility for (รับผิดชอบ) ไม่ใช้ make, do หรือ have"},"choices":["do","take","make","have"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present perfect continuous","th":"present perfect continuous"},"topic":{"en":"Tenses","th":"กาล (Tense)"},"passage":"","stem":"I ______ for this bus for forty minutes, and it still hasn't come!","explanation":{"en":"An action that started in the past and is still happening now, with 'for forty minutes', takes the present perfect continuous.","th":"การกระทำที่เริ่มในอดีตและยังดำเนินอยู่ตอนนี้ พร้อมกับ for forty minutes ใช้ present perfect continuous คือ have been waiting"},"choices":["had waited","have been waiting","was waiting","am waiting"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Double comparative","th":"โครงสร้าง the more … the more"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and word order","th":"ความสอดคล้องและการเรียงคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"The more you practise speaking, ______ you will become.","explanation":{"en":"The structure is 'the + comparative …, the + comparative …'. After 'become' we need an adjective, so 'the more confident'.","th":"โครงสร้างคือ the + comparative …, the + comparative … และหลัง become ต้องใช้คำคุณศัพท์ จึงเป็น the more confident"},"choices":["more confident","the most confident","the more confident","the more confidently"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with a compound subject","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยากับประธานสองตัว"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and word order","th":"ความสอดคล้องและการเรียงคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My sister and I [[usually]] [[goes]] swimming [[on]] Saturday mornings [[before]] breakfast.","explanation":{"en":"'My sister and I' is a plural subject, so the verb is 'go', not 'goes'.","th":"'My sister and I' เป็นประธานพหูพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น go ไม่ใช่ goes"},"choices":["usually","goes","on","before"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"so vs such","th":"so กับ such"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and word order","th":"ความสอดคล้องและการเรียงคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The film was [[such]] boring that [[most of]] the audience [[fell]] asleep [[before]] the end.","explanation":{"en":"Before an adjective alone we use 'so': 'so boring that'. 'Such' is used with a noun phrase ('such a boring film').","th":"หน้าคำคุณศัพท์ที่ไม่มีคำนามตามใช้ so คือ so boring that ส่วน such ใช้กับนามวลี เช่น such a boring film"},"choices":["such","most of","fell","before"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Countable quantity noun","th":"คำนามบอกปริมาณกับคำนามนับได้"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Every year the fashion industry produces more than 100 billion items of clothing, and a large (1) ______ of them are worn only a few times before being thrown away. This trend, often called 'fast fashion', has been made possible by cheap materials and by factories that can turn a new design into finished garments in (2) ______ of weeks. For shoppers the advantages are obvious: clothes have never been cheaper. The costs, however, are less visible. Producing a single cotton T-shirt can require more than 2,500 litres of water, and many synthetic fabrics release tiny plastic fibres every time they are washed. These fibres are too small to be (3) ______ by most water treatment plants, so they end up in rivers and oceans, where they are swallowed by fish and other sea creatures. Some companies have responded by launching recycling schemes (4) ______ customers can return old clothes in exchange for a discount. Critics argue that such schemes do little to (5) ______ the root of the problem, since only a tiny fraction of returned clothing is actually turned into new fabric, and the discounts simply encourage people to buy more. The most effective solution, they say, is also the simplest: buy fewer clothes, choose better quality, and wear (6) ______ for longer.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","explanation":{"en":"'A large proportion of them' works with a countable plural. 'A large amount of' and 'a great deal of' are used with uncountable nouns, and 'a large size of' is not English.","th":"a large proportion of them ใช้กับคำนามนับได้พหูพจน์ได้ ส่วน a large amount of และ a great deal of ใช้กับคำนามนับไม่ได้ และ a large size of ไม่มีใช้"},"choices":["deal","size","proportion","amount"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Preposition + -ing","th":"บุพบท + V-ing"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"When Malee retired from teaching in 2015, she had no intention of (7) ______ her days quietly at home. Her village in Nan province had no library, and the nearest bookshop was over an hour away by bus. So she turned the ground floor of her house into a reading room, filled it with her own books and (8) ______ a sign outside inviting anyone to come in. At first only a few children appeared, mostly out of curiosity. Within a year, however, the room was so crowded at weekends that Malee had to (9) ______ visitors to bring their own cushions. Donations began to arrive from her former students, many of (10) ______ had moved to Bangkok. Today the library holds more than four thousand books, and volunteers run storytelling sessions for young children and computer classes for older residents. Malee insists that she deserves (11) ______ credit. 'I only opened a door,' she says. 'The village did the rest.' Local officials, who had once doubted that such a small project could last, now describe it as a model (12) ______ other communities could follow.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (7)?","explanation":{"en":"'Of' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form: 'no intention of spending'.","th":"of เป็นบุพบท จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ no intention of spending"},"choices":["spent","to spend","spend","spending"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the best title","th":"การเลือกชื่อเรื่องที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่านบทความ"},"passage":"In the 1880s a German psychologist named Hermann Ebbinghaus carried out an unusual experiment on himself. He memorised long lists of nonsense syllables — combinations such as 'WID' or 'ZOF' that had no meaning — and then tested how many he could recall after different periods of time. The results, plotted on a graph, produced what is now called the 'forgetting curve'. Memory dropped steeply in the first hour, and within a day he had lost more than half of what he had learned. After that the decline slowed, but it never entirely stopped.\n\nEbbinghaus chose nonsense syllables deliberately. Real words come with associations, and he wanted to measure memory without the help of meaning. This makes his findings something of a worst case: we forget meaningful material more slowly. Still, later researchers have repeated his experiment many times, and the basic shape of the curve has held up remarkably well.\n\nThe more useful part of his work concerned how forgetting could be slowed. Ebbinghaus found that each time he reviewed a list, the curve became flatter; he forgot less, and more slowly. Crucially, reviews were most effective when they were spaced out rather than crowded together. Studying a list ten times in one evening produced weaker long-term memory than studying it three or four times over several days. This principle, known as spaced repetition, is the basis of many modern flashcard apps, which calculate when a learner is about to forget an item and show it again at just that moment.\n\nWhy does spacing work? One explanation is that the effort of retrieving a half-forgotten memory strengthens it more than re-reading something that is still fresh. When information feels easy, the brain does little work; when recalling it requires a struggle, the memory is rebuilt more firmly. This also explains why testing yourself is more effective than simply re-reading notes, even though re-reading feels more productive at the time.\n\nThe implications for students are uncomfortable. The common habit of studying intensively the night before an exam can be enough to pass the next day, but most of the material will have disappeared within weeks. For subjects that build on earlier knowledge, such as mathematics or languages, that loss is costly. A schedule of short, repeated reviews takes no more time in total, but it requires planning — and the willingness to feel, for a while, that one is learning less.","stem":"Which title best summarises the passage?","explanation":{"en":"The passage describes the forgetting curve and then explains how spaced reviews and self-testing slow forgetting. Ebbinghaus's life, nonsense syllables and apps are only parts of that story.","th":"บทความอธิบายเส้นโค้งการลืม แล้วอธิบายว่าการทบทวนแบบเว้นระยะและการทดสอบตนเองช่วยชะลอการลืมได้อย่างไร ส่วนชีวิตของ Ebbinghaus พยางค์ไร้ความหมาย และแอปเป็นเพียงส่วนย่อย"},"choices":["How Flashcard Apps Are Designed","The Life of Hermann Ebbinghaus","Why Nonsense Syllables Are Hard to Learn","Forgetting, and How to Slow It Down"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference","th":"การอนุมาน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่านบทความ"},"passage":"Subject: Proposal for a student-run repair café\n\nDear Dr Wanida,\n\nI am writing on behalf of the Student Council to propose a new project for the second semester: a monthly 'repair café', to be held in the school canteen on the first Saturday of each month.\n\nThe idea is simple. Students, parents and local residents would bring broken items — bicycles, clothes, small electrical appliances, school bags — and volunteers would help them fix these items rather than throw them away. Similar events have been running in many countries for over a decade, and a secondary school in Chiang Mai started one last year with considerable success. According to its organisers, more than 300 items were repaired in the first six months.\n\nWe believe the project would benefit the school in three ways. First, it would give students practical skills that are not part of the normal curriculum, from sewing to basic electronics. Second, it would reduce the amount of waste our community sends to landfill, which supports the environmental goals in the school's development plan. Third, it would strengthen links between the school and the neighbourhood, since visitors would see students in a role of service rather than simply as customers.\n\nWe are aware that the project raises some practical concerns, and we have tried to address them in advance. Safety is the most important. Electrical repairs would only be carried out under the supervision of Mr Prasert from the Design and Technology department, who has kindly agreed to take part, and no item connected to mains power would be tested until he had checked it. We would also ask every visitor to sign a short form accepting that repairs are made on a voluntary basis and cannot be guaranteed.\n\nAs for costs, we expect them to be modest. Most tools can be borrowed from the Design and Technology workshop, and a local hardware shop has offered to donate basic supplies such as glue, thread and screws. The Student Council would use part of its existing budget to pay for refreshments.\n\nWe would be grateful for the opportunity to present the plan in more detail at the next staff meeting. If you approve, we hope to hold a trial event in November and review the results before committing to a full programme.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nNatthapong Srisuk\nPresident, Student Council","stem":"What can be inferred from the paragraph about safety?","explanation":{"en":"The writer deals with safety 'in advance' and calls it 'the most important' concern, showing they expect it to be the director's first worry. Mr Prasert has agreed to help, and electrical items will be accepted under supervision.","th":"ผู้เขียนตอบเรื่องความปลอดภัยไว้ล่วงหน้าและบอกว่าเป็นเรื่องสำคัญที่สุด แสดงว่าคาดว่าผู้อำนวยการจะกังวลเรื่องนี้ ครูประเสริฐยินดีช่วย และรับของใช้ไฟฟ้าโดยมีผู้ควบคุม"},"choices":["Visitors will have to pay if a repair is unsuccessful.","Mr Prasert has refused to supervise repairs of electrical items.","The writer expects the director to be worried about the risks of electrical repairs.","Electrical items will not be accepted at the repair café."],"answer":2}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":3,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, weighted like the Triam Udom paper towards cloze, reading and error identification (tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years; ours is a longer practice paper). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที เน้นเติมคำในบทความ การอ่าน และหาจุดผิด ตามแนวข้อสอบเตรียมอุดมฯ (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ ชุดนี้เป็นแบบฝึกที่ยาวกว่า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"tu4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Idiom: cross one's mind","th":"สำนวน cross one's mind"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I heard you're thinking of leaving the school orchestra.\nB: ______ I haven't made up my mind yet, though.","explanation":{"en":"'It has crossed my mind' means 'I have thought about it', which fits 'I haven't made up my mind yet'. 'I've changed my mind' suggests a decision has been made, 'it never crossed my mind' denies the rumour completely, and 'out of my mind' means crazy.","th":"it has crossed my mind แปลว่าเคยคิดถึงเรื่องนี้อยู่ ซึ่งเข้ากับ 'ยังไม่ได้ตัดสินใจ' ส่วน I've changed my mind แสดงว่าตัดสินใจแล้ว it never crossed my mind คือปฏิเสธว่าไม่เคยคิดเลย และ out of my mind แปลว่าเสียสติ"},"choices":["Well, it has crossed my mind.","Well, it never crossed my mind.","Well, I've changed my mind about it.","Well, it's out of my mind."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Asking for and giving repetition","th":"การขอให้พูดซ้ำและการตอบ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Sorry, I didn't catch the last part. Could you run that by me again?\nB: ______ I said the deadline has been moved forward to Friday.","explanation":{"en":"'Run that by me again' asks B to repeat, and B does repeat the information, so 'I'll go over it again' fits. 'Run after you' means chase, and 'catch it later' misuses A's word 'catch'.","th":"run that by me again แปลว่าช่วยพูดอีกครั้ง และ B ก็พูดซ้ำจริง จึงตอบ I'll go over it again ส่วน run after you แปลว่าวิ่งตาม และ catch it later ใช้คำ catch ผิดความหมาย"},"choices":["Sure, I'll go over it again.","No, I didn't say anything to you.","Sure, I'll run after you.","Sorry, I'll catch it later."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Collocation: pose a threat","th":"คำปรากฏร่วม pose a threat"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Rising sea levels ______ a serious threat to low-lying coastal cities such as Bangkok.","explanation":{"en":"The collocation is 'pose a threat'. 'Raise' goes with questions or money, 'make up' means form or invent, and 'cause up' is not English.","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมคือ pose a threat (เป็นภัยคุกคาม) ส่วน raise ใช้กับคำถามหรือเงิน make up แปลว่าประกอบเป็นหรือแต่งขึ้น และ cause up ไม่มีใช้"},"choices":["make up","cause up","pose","raise"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Confusable adjectives: unfounded","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ที่สับสนง่าย: unfounded"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary","th":"คำศัพท์เชิงวิชาการ"},"passage":"","stem":"The company's claims about its 'eco-friendly' packaging turned out to be completely ______; independent tests found no difference from ordinary plastic.","explanation":{"en":"'Unfounded' means not based on facts, which the tests showed. 'Unfunded' means without money, 'founding' relates to starting an organisation, and 'profound' means deep.","th":"unfounded แปลว่าไม่มีมูลความจริง ซึ่งผลการทดสอบยืนยัน ส่วน unfunded แปลว่าไม่มีเงินทุน founding เกี่ยวกับการก่อตั้ง และ profound แปลว่าลึกซึ้ง"},"choices":["unfunded","founding","profound","unfounded"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Inversion: little did …","th":"การสลับที่: Little did …"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and word order","th":"ความสอดคล้องและการเรียงคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Little ______ that the decision would change her life for ever.","explanation":{"en":"Negative adverbs such as 'little' at the start of a sentence require inversion: 'Little did she know'.","th":"คำกริยาวิเศษณ์เชิงปฏิเสธ เช่น little เมื่ออยู่ต้นประโยคต้องสลับที่ จึงเป็น Little did she know"},"choices":["she knew","she did know","knew she","did she know"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inverted third conditional","th":"เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 แบบสลับที่"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","stem":"______ the instructions more carefully, he would not have broken the machine.","explanation":{"en":"'Would not have broken' is a third-conditional result, so the condition must be past perfect. Without 'if', it is inverted: 'Had he read'.","th":"would not have broken เป็นผลของเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 เงื่อนไขจึงต้องเป็น past perfect และเมื่อละ if ต้องสลับที่เป็น Had he read"},"choices":["If he read","Had he read","Did he read","Should he read"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"reach a decision (no preposition)","th":"reach a decision (ไม่ใช้บุพบท)"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns","th":"รูปแบบกริยา (gerund/infinitive)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The committee spent three hours [[discussing]] the proposal [[without]] [[reaching]] [[to]] a decision.","explanation":{"en":"'Reach' is transitive: we 'reach a decision', not 'reach to a decision'. (Note also 'discuss' with no 'about'.)","th":"reach เป็นกริยาที่ตามด้วยกรรมโดยตรง ใช้ reach a decision ไม่ใช่ reach to a decision"},"choices":["discussing","without","reaching","to"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"would rather … than + bare infinitive","th":"would rather … than + infinitive ไม่มี to"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns","th":"รูปแบบกริยา (gerund/infinitive)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: She would rather [[stay]] at home tonight [[than]] [[going]] to the party, [[which]] starts very late.","explanation":{"en":"After 'would rather … than' both verbs take the bare infinitive: 'than go to the party'.","th":"หลัง would rather … than กริยาทั้งสองต้องเป็น infinitive ไม่มี to จึงต้องเป็น than go"},"choices":["stay","than","going","which"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Fixed phrase: in danger of","th":"วลีตายตัว in danger of"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Of the roughly 7,000 languages spoken in the world today, linguists estimate that around half are (1) ______ danger of disappearing by the end of this century. A language rarely dies suddenly. (2) ______, it fades over several generations, as parents who speak it at home decide that their children's future lies in a more widely used language. The children grow up understanding their grandparents' speech but no longer using it, and their own children may not understand it at all. When the last fluent speaker dies, much more is lost than a set of words. Languages (3) ______ knowledge that is often found nowhere else: names for local plants and their medical uses, ways of describing the landscape, and stories that record events from centuries ago. Efforts to (4) ______ endangered languages have had mixed results. Some, such as Welsh and Hawaiian, have recovered remarkably, largely because they were introduced into schools and given official status. Others survive only in recordings and dictionaries, (5) ______ they are studied by researchers but spoken by no one. The lesson from the successful cases is that documentation, however valuable, is not enough. A language stays alive only if people have reasons to speak it in their daily lives — and that depends (6) ______ on linguists than on the choices of ordinary families.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","explanation":{"en":"The fixed phrase is 'in danger of + -ing'. ('At risk of' uses 'at', but not with 'danger'.)","th":"วลีตายตัวคือ in danger of + V-ing (ส่วน at ใช้กับ at risk of ไม่ใช้กับ danger)"},"choices":["on","in","at","under"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Noun after adjective: accuracy","th":"คำนามหลังคำคุณศัพท์: accuracy"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: article","th":"เติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Each spring and summer, a tree adds a new layer of wood beneath its bark. In good years, when rain is plentiful, this layer is wide; in years of drought it is narrow. Over time these layers form the rings that can be seen when a trunk is cut across. By counting them, scientists can determine a tree's age with remarkable (7) ______. But the rings reveal much more than age. Because trees in the same region respond to the same weather, their patterns of wide and narrow rings closely (8) ______ each other. By matching the patterns in living trees with those in old timber from buildings, and then with even older wood (9) ______ in bogs or riverbeds, researchers have built continuous records stretching back thousands of years. Such records have proved invaluable. They have allowed historians to date wooden objects precisely, and climate scientists to reconstruct droughts that occurred long before any instruments existed to measure rainfall. In Southeast Asia, studies of teak have revealed a series of severe droughts in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, (10) ______ some historians believe may have contributed to the decline of powerful kingdoms. The method is not without limitations. Trees in the tropics do not always form clear annual rings, and wood that has (11) ______ cannot be studied at all. (12) ______ these difficulties, tree rings remain one of the most reliable natural archives we have.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (7)?","explanation":{"en":"After 'with remarkable' we need a noun: 'with remarkable accuracy'. 'Accurate' is an adjective, 'accurately' an adverb and 'accustom' a verb.","th":"หลัง with remarkable ต้องใช้คำนาม คือ accuracy ส่วน accurate เป็นคำคุณศัพท์ accurately เป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์ และ accustom เป็นคำกริยา"},"choices":["accuracy","accurate","accurately","accustom"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Main idea","th":"ใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่านบทความ"},"passage":"For most of the history of medicine, the placebo — a treatment with no active ingredient — was regarded as a nuisance. In clinical trials it existed only to be beaten: a new drug was considered effective if patients who received it improved more than those given sugar pills. That patients on placebo often improved too was treated as background noise, a statistical problem to be subtracted.\n\nOver the past few decades, however, researchers have begun to take that noise seriously. Brain-imaging studies have shown that when people in pain receive a placebo they believe to be a painkiller, their brains release natural pain-relieving chemicals. The improvement, in other words, is not merely imagined; it has a measurable physical basis. The size of the effect also depends on details that have nothing to do with chemistry. Two pills tend to work better than one, injections better than pills, and expensive placebos better than cheap ones. Even the colour of a tablet can influence how patients describe its effects.\n\nWhat these findings suggest is that the experience of being treated — the ritual of the consultation, the confidence of the doctor, the expectation of relief — is itself a kind of treatment. This helps to explain a puzzling observation: in some trials of treatments for pain, the effect of placebo appears to have grown stronger over the years, particularly in countries where advertising has made patients more familiar with the idea of medicines that work.\n\nThe obvious ethical difficulty is deception. A doctor who prescribes a sugar pill while claiming it is a powerful drug is lying to the patient, and most medical codes forbid this. Surprisingly, though, deception may not always be necessary. In a number of small studies, patients with conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome were given pills openly labelled as placebos and told honestly that they contained no medicine. Many still reported significant improvement compared with patients who received no pills at all.\n\nThese 'open-label' results should be treated with caution. The studies were small, and symptoms such as pain and tiredness are partly subjective, which makes them especially sensitive to expectation. No one suggests that a placebo can shrink a tumour or cure an infection. But the research has shifted the question that doctors ask. Instead of asking only which chemical a patient needs, they are beginning to ask how the way treatment is delivered might make every medicine, real or not, work a little better.","stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","explanation":{"en":"The passage moves from the old view (a nuisance, 'noise') to evidence that the effect is physical, and ends with how this changes the questions doctors ask. It never claims placebos beat real drugs.","th":"บทความเล่าจากมุมมองเดิม (สิ่งรบกวน) ไปสู่หลักฐานว่าผลของยาหลอกมีพื้นฐานทางกาย และจบด้วยการที่แพทย์เริ่มตั้งคำถามใหม่ ไม่ได้อ้างว่ายาหลอกดีกว่ายาจริง"},"choices":["Clinical trials no longer need to compare new drugs with placebos.","The placebo effect, once dismissed, is now seen as real and as having lessons for medical practice.","Doctors should prescribe sugar pills instead of painkillers.","Placebos are more effective than most modern drugs."],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Detail","th":"รายละเอียด"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่านเรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The letter arrived on the morning my grandfather turned eighty, which my mother later said was either a coincidence or the cruellest joke the post office had ever played. It was addressed to him in careful, old-fashioned handwriting, and the stamp was from a country he had not visited for sixty years.\n\nHe did not open it at breakfast. He placed it beside his plate, squared its edges with the side of his hand, and went on eating his rice porridge as though it were an ordinary bill. My cousins and I, who had been told almost nothing about his life before he came to Thailand, exchanged glances across the table. My aunt asked, in the bright voice she used for difficult subjects, whether he was going to read it. 'Later,' he said, and that was the end of the conversation.\n\nLater turned out to be three days. In that time the envelope moved from the dining table to the shelf above his radio, and from there to the pocket of the cardigan he wore in the garden regardless of the heat. I began to understand that he was not ignoring it. He was keeping it close in the way you might keep a stone that is too hot to hold, waiting for it to cool.\n\nOn the fourth evening he called me into his room. He was sitting on the edge of the bed with the letter open on his knee, and his glasses had slipped down his nose. 'My eyes are tired,' he said, which I knew was not true; he read the newspaper every morning without difficulty. 'Read it to me.'\n\nThe letter was from a woman named Hana, the younger sister of a boy he had gone to school with. Her brother had died that spring. Among his things she had found a photograph of two boys on a bridge, and on the back, in her brother's writing, my grandfather's name and the words: 'I should have gone with him.' She did not know what it meant, she wrote, but she thought he should have it.\n\nWhen I finished, my grandfather took the photograph from the envelope and looked at it for a long time. Then he said something I have thought about ever since. 'All these years I believed he was angry with me for leaving. It never occurred to me that he might have envied me.' He laughed softly, wiped his eyes with the back of his wrist, and asked me to fetch a pen.","stem":"Who wrote the letter?","explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 5: the letter was from Hana, 'the younger sister of a boy he had gone to school with'. The friend himself had died.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 5 บอกว่าจดหมายมาจากฮานะ น้องสาวของเพื่อนที่เคยเรียนด้วยกัน ส่วนเพื่อนคนนั้นเสียชีวิตแล้ว"},"choices":["The sister of the grandfather's old school friend","A woman the grandfather had once worked with","The grandfather's old school friend","The grandfather's own younger sister"],"answer":0}]}},{"exam":"top4","set":1,"title":{"en":"M.4 English entrance (top schools) — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 โรงเรียนดัง — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading — the parts most top schools use. DeepDistrict practice format — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวที่โรงเรียนดังส่วนใหญ่ใช้ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":true,"file":"top4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Making and answering requests","th":"การขอร้องและการตอบรับคำขอร้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: Could you keep an eye on my bag while I buy a drink?\nB: ______","choices":["Never mind, it doesn't matter.","Yes, my eyes are fine, thanks.","No problem, I'll drink it for you.","Sure, take your time."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Keep an eye on\" means \"watch or look after\", so agreeing to help with \"Sure, take your time\" is the natural reply. \"My eyes are fine\" takes the idiom literally, and \"Never mind, it doesn't matter\" answers an apology, not a request.","th":"\"keep an eye on\" แปลว่า ช่วยดูแลหรือเฝ้าให้ คำตอบที่เหมาะคือ \"Sure, take your time.\" (ได้เลย ไม่ต้องรีบ) ส่วน \"my eyes are fine\" แปลสำนวนตรงตัว และ \"Never mind, it doesn't matter\" ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษ ไม่ใช่คำขอร้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Asking for and giving advice","th":"การขอและการให้คำแนะนำ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: I've got a terrible headache, and I still have two chapters to read.\nB: ______","choices":["You'd better to read faster.","Why don't you reading them later?","If I were you, I'd take a short break first.","I'd rather you have a headache."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"If I were you, I'd …\" is a correct and natural way to give advice. \"You'd better\" is followed by the base form (not \"to read\"), \"Why don't you\" also takes the base form (not \"reading\"), and \"I'd rather you have a headache\" is not advice.","th":"\"If I were you, I'd …\" เป็นรูปแบบการให้คำแนะนำที่ถูกต้อง ส่วน \"You'd better\" ต้องตามด้วยกริยาช่องหนึ่ง ไม่ใช่ \"to read\" และ \"Why don't you\" ก็ตามด้วยกริยาช่องหนึ่ง ไม่ใช่ \"reading\" ส่วน \"I'd rather you have a headache\" ไม่ใช่คำแนะนำ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing the word that fits the context","th":"การเลือกคำให้เหมาะกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: meaning in context","th":"คำศัพท์: ความหมายตามบริบท"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The museum is closed today, so we'll have to ______ our visit until next week.\"","choices":["pretend","postpone","prevent","propose"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Postpone\" means \"move to a later time\", which matches \"until next week\". \"Prevent\" means \"stop something happening\", \"pretend\" means \"act as if\", and \"propose\" means \"suggest\".","th":"\"postpone\" แปลว่า เลื่อนออกไป เข้ากับ \"until next week\" ส่วน \"prevent\" แปลว่า ป้องกัน \"pretend\" แปลว่า แกล้งทำ และ \"propose\" แปลว่า เสนอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the word that fits the context","th":"การเลือกคำให้เหมาะกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: meaning in context","th":"คำศัพท์: ความหมายตามบริบท"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The results of the survey were ______: half the students liked the new timetable and half did not.\"","choices":["conclusive","unanimous","inevitable","inconclusive"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"An even split gives no clear answer, so the results were \"inconclusive\". \"Unanimous\" means everyone agreed, \"conclusive\" means clear and final, and \"inevitable\" means \"certain to happen\".","th":"ผลแบ่งครึ่งต่อครึ่งจึงสรุปไม่ได้ ใช้ \"inconclusive\" ส่วน \"unanimous\" แปลว่า เป็นเอกฉันท์ \"conclusive\" แปลว่า ชัดเจนสรุปได้ และ \"inevitable\" แปลว่า หลีกเลี่ยงไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present perfect vs past simple","th":"การใช้ present perfect กับ past simple"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: tenses and the passive","th":"ไวยากรณ์: กาลและประโยค passive"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I ______ to Phuket three times, but I've never been to Krabi.\"","choices":["have been","had gone","was going","went"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Three times\" with no finished time, plus \"I've never been\", shows life experience, so the present perfect \"have been\" is needed. \"Went\" needs a finished past time, and \"had gone\" needs a later past reference point.","th":"\"three times\" โดยไม่ระบุเวลาในอดีต และ \"I've never been\" แสดงประสบการณ์ จึงใช้ present perfect คือ \"have been\" ส่วน \"went\" ต้องมีเวลาในอดีตที่ชัดเจน และ \"had gone\" ต้องมีเหตุการณ์อดีตอีกเหตุการณ์มาอ้างอิง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Reported speech","th":"การเล่าคำพูด (reported speech)"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: conditionals, clauses and reported speech","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ประโยคเงื่อนไข อนุประโยค และ reported speech"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\nShe asked me, \"Where did you buy your shoes?\"\n\"She asked me where ______ my shoes.\"","choices":["I had bought","did I buy","I have bought","had I bought"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In a reported question the word order is like a statement (subject before verb), and the past simple moves back to the past perfect: \"where I had bought\". \"Did I buy\" and \"had I bought\" keep question word order.","th":"คำถามแบบ reported speech ต้องเรียงแบบประโยคบอกเล่า (ประธานก่อนกริยา) และ past simple เลื่อนเป็น past perfect จึงได้ \"where I had bought\" ส่วน \"did I buy\" และ \"had I bought\" ยังเรียงแบบคำถาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: agreement, articles, prepositions and comparison","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ความสอดคล้อง คำนำหน้านาม บุพบท และการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Everyone in the two classes [[were]] excited [[about]] the trip, [[which]] had been [[planned]] for months.","choices":["were","about","which","planned"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Everyone\" is singular, so the verb must be \"was\", not \"were\".","th":"\"Everyone\" เป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น \"was\" ไม่ใช่ \"were\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: agreement, articles, prepositions and comparison","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ความสอดคล้อง คำนำหน้านาม บุพบท และการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The number of students [[who]] cycle to school [[have]] risen [[sharply]] [[since]] the new path opened.","choices":["who","have","sharply","since"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The subject is \"the number\" (singular), so it should be \"has risen\", not \"have risen\".","th":"ประธานคือ \"the number\" ซึ่งเป็นเอกพจน์ จึงต้องใช้ \"has risen\" ไม่ใช่ \"have risen\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing vocabulary in a text","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์ในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"When the students of a secondary school in Nonthaburi first suggested turning their empty rooftop into a vegetable garden, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, windy and difficult to (1) ______, and nobody was sure who would look after it during the holidays. However, the students were determined. They (2) ______ a detailed plan, measured the space and asked a local farmer for advice.\n\nThe farmer explained that most vegetables need at least six hours of sunlight a day, (3) ______ was not a problem on the roof. The real challenge was water. (4) ______ carrying heavy buckets up four floors, the students designed a simple system that collects rainwater from the roof itself and stores it in large tanks.\n\nWithin six months, the garden was producing lettuce, morning glory and chillies. The school canteen now buys the vegetables at a fair price, and the money is used to (5) ______ seeds and tools. Teachers have noticed other benefits, too. Students who used to (6) ______ no interest in science lessons now talk enthusiastically about soil, insects and weather.\n\nThe project has not been (7) ______ difficulties. During the long dry season, the tanks sometimes run empty, and a storm last year damaged several plant beds. Yet each problem has taught the students something new. As one fifteen-year-old member put it, \"We didn't just grow vegetables. We grew (8) ______ in ourselves.\"","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["reach","get","access to","arrive"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Reach\" takes a direct object: \"difficult to reach (the roof)\". \"Arrive\" needs \"at\", \"get\" needs \"to\", and \"access to\" is not a verb phrase here.","th":"\"reach\" ตามด้วยกรรมได้ทันที \"difficult to reach\" (ขึ้นไปถึงได้ยาก) ส่วน \"arrive\" ต้องมี \"at\" \"get\" ต้องมี \"to\" และ \"access to\" ใช้เป็นกริยาในตำแหน่งนี้ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing grammatical forms in a text","th":"การเลือกรูปไวยากรณ์ในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"When the students of a secondary school in Nonthaburi first suggested turning their empty rooftop into a vegetable garden, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, windy and difficult to (1) ______, and nobody was sure who would look after it during the holidays. However, the students were determined. They (2) ______ a detailed plan, measured the space and asked a local farmer for advice.\n\nThe farmer explained that most vegetables need at least six hours of sunlight a day, (3) ______ was not a problem on the roof. The real challenge was water. (4) ______ carrying heavy buckets up four floors, the students designed a simple system that collects rainwater from the roof itself and stores it in large tanks.\n\nWithin six months, the garden was producing lettuce, morning glory and chillies. The school canteen now buys the vegetables at a fair price, and the money is used to (5) ______ seeds and tools. Teachers have noticed other benefits, too. Students who used to (6) ______ no interest in science lessons now talk enthusiastically about soil, insects and weather.\n\nThe project has not been (7) ______ difficulties. During the long dry season, the tanks sometimes run empty, and a storm last year damaged several plant beds. Yet each problem has taught the students something new. As one fifteen-year-old member put it, \"We didn't just grow vegetables. We grew (8) ______ in ourselves.\"","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["buys","buying","bought","buy"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"The money is used to buy\" is the passive of \"use something to do\", showing purpose, so the base form follows \"to\". Do not confuse it with \"be used to + -ing\" (be accustomed to).","th":"\"the money is used to buy\" เป็น passive ของ \"use something to do\" บอกจุดประสงค์ จึงตามด้วยกริยาช่องหนึ่ง อย่าสับสนกับ \"be used to + V-ing\" ที่แปลว่า เคยชิน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: informational article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Teenagers Can't Fall Asleep Early\n\nParents often complain that their teenage children stay up too late at night and then struggle to get out of bed in the morning. It is easy to blame smartphones or laziness, but scientists say the real story is more complicated.\n\nDuring adolescence, the body's internal clock shifts. Melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, is released about two hours later in teenagers than in younger children or adults. As a result, a fifteen-year-old who goes to bed at ten o'clock may simply lie awake, staring at the ceiling, for a long time. This change is biological, not a matter of choice, and it appears in teenagers all over the world.\n\nThe problem is that school timetables do not take this shift into account. Many schools start before eight o'clock, which means students must wake up while their bodies still think it is the middle of the night. Researchers estimate that most teenagers need between eight and ten hours of sleep, yet surveys suggest that the majority get fewer than seven on school nights. The consequences go beyond feeling tired. Lack of sleep has been linked to poor concentration, low mood and even a higher risk of road accidents among young drivers.\n\nSome schools have experimented with later start times. In one well-known study, a group of schools moved the start of the day from 7.50 to 8.30. Within a year, attendance improved, fewer students fell asleep in class, and average grades rose slightly. Not everyone was pleased, however. Some parents found it harder to organise their working day, and sports coaches complained that training sessions had to finish later in the evening.\n\nScreens do play a part, of course. The blue light from phones and tablets can delay the release of melatonin even further. Experts therefore suggest that teenagers switch off their devices at least an hour before bed. But they also warn that advice alone will not solve the problem. Unless schools and families recognise that teenage sleep patterns are different, young people will continue to start each day with a sleep debt they can never fully repay.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Smartphones are the main reason why teenagers go to bed late.","Teenagers need less sleep than adults because their bodies are still growing.","Teenagers' late sleeping is largely caused by natural changes in the body, which school schedules often ignore.","Schools that start later always produce students with much higher grades."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The passage explains that a biological shift in the body clock makes teenagers sleepy later, and that early school timetables do not take this into account. Phones are only \"part\" of the story, grades rose only \"slightly\", and teenagers need more sleep, not less.","th":"บทความอธิบายว่านาฬิกาชีวภาพของวัยรุ่นเปลี่ยนไปตามธรรมชาติ ทำให้ง่วงช้า แต่ตารางเรียนที่เริ่มเช้าไม่คำนึงถึงเรื่องนี้ ส่วนโทรศัพท์เป็นเพียงส่วนหนึ่งของปัญหา เกรดดีขึ้นเพียงเล็กน้อย และวัยรุ่นต้องการนอนมากกว่า ไม่ใช่น้อยกว่า"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding specific details","th":"การหารายละเอียดเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: email, notice or advertisement","th":"การอ่าน: อีเมล ประกาศ หรือโฆษณา"},"passage":"To: Ploy Chaiyasit\nFrom: Naree Suwan\nSubject: Your homestay in Sapporo — important information\n\nDear Ploy,\n\nThank you for confirming your place on the Winter Culture Exchange in Sapporo from 8 to 22 January. We are delighted that you will be joining twelve other students from Thailand, Vietnam and Malaysia.\n\nYour host family, the Tanakas, live about twenty minutes by subway from the language centre where your morning classes will take place. Mr and Mrs Tanaka have two children: Haruto, who is sixteen, and Yui, who is eleven. Haruto is a member of his school's English club and is very excited to practise with you. The family also has a cat, so please let us know as soon as possible if you have an allergy, as we would then need to find you another home.\n\nPlease remember that temperatures in Sapporo in January are often below minus five degrees, and snow is almost certain. Thick gloves, waterproof boots and a warm hat are essential. You do not need to buy an expensive coat before you leave, though; the programme will lend each student a winter jacket on arrival.\n\nIn the afternoons, you will take part in cultural activities such as calligraphy, a visit to a chocolate factory and the famous Snow Festival. On your final evening, each student will give a short presentation in English or Japanese about something they learned during the trip, so you may find it useful to keep a diary.\n\nFinally, a reminder that the second payment of 18,000 baht is due by 30 November. If it has not been received by then, we cannot guarantee your place, as several students are on our waiting list.\n\nIf you have any questions, please reply to this email or call me on 02 555 0147 between 9 a.m. and 5 p.m. on weekdays.\n\nBest wishes,\nNaree Suwan\nProgramme Coordinator","table":null,"stem":"Which of these does Ploy NOT need to take with her?","choices":["Waterproof boots","Thick gloves","A warm hat","A winter jacket"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Gloves, boots and a hat are \"essential\", but the programme \"will lend each student a winter jacket on arrival\".","th":"ถุงมือ รองเท้าบูต และหมวกเป็นของจำเป็น แต่โครงการจะให้ยืม \"winter jacket\" เมื่อไปถึง จึงไม่ต้องเตรียมเสื้อแจ็กเก็ตไปเอง"}}]}},{"exam":"top4","set":2,"title":{"en":"M.4 English entrance (top schools) — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 โรงเรียนดัง — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading — the parts most top schools use. DeepDistrict practice format — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวที่โรงเรียนดังส่วนใหญ่ใช้ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"top4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Offering help and responding to offers","th":"การเสนอความช่วยเหลือและการตอบรับ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: Those boxes look heavy. ______\nB: Thanks, that's really kind of you.","choices":["Can you help me carry them?","Why are you carrying them?","Shall I help you carry them?","Would you mind carrying them?"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B thanks A for being kind, so A must have offered help: \"Shall I help you carry them?\" \"Can you help me …?\" and \"Would you mind carrying them?\" are requests for help, not offers.","th":"B ขอบคุณที่ A มีน้ำใจ แสดงว่า A เสนอความช่วยเหลือ คือ \"Shall I help you carry them?\" ส่วน \"Can you help me …?\" และ \"Would you mind carrying them?\" เป็นการขอให้อีกฝ่ายช่วย ไม่ใช่การเสนอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Making and answering requests","th":"การขอร้องและการตอบรับคำขอร้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: ______\nB: Of course. It's 081 234 5678.","choices":["Could you tell me what time it is?","Could you give me your phone number, please?","Would you mind to give me your number?","Do you know where is the post office?"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"B gives a phone number, so A asked for it politely: \"Could you give me your phone number, please?\" \"Would you mind\" must be followed by \"giving\", and an embedded question uses statement order: \"where the post office is\".","th":"B ให้เบอร์โทรศัพท์ แสดงว่า A ขอเบอร์อย่างสุภาพ คือ \"Could you give me your phone number, please?\" ส่วน \"Would you mind\" ต้องตามด้วย \"giving\" และคำถามซ้อนต้องเรียงแบบบอกเล่า คือ \"where the post office is\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing the word that fits the context","th":"การเลือกคำให้เหมาะกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: meaning in context","th":"คำศัพท์: ความหมายตามบริบท"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The doctor gave me some ______ for my cough and told me to rest.\"","choices":["medical","receipt","recipe","medicine"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Something a doctor gives you for a cough is \"medicine\". A \"recipe\" tells you how to cook, a \"receipt\" proves you paid, and \"medical\" is an adjective.","th":"สิ่งที่หมอให้เพื่อรักษาอาการไอคือ \"medicine\" (ยา) ส่วน \"recipe\" คือ สูตรอาหาร \"receipt\" คือ ใบเสร็จ และ \"medical\" เป็นคำคุณศัพท์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Forming words with prefixes and suffixes","th":"การสร้างคำด้วยอุปสรรคและปัจจัย"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: word formation","th":"คำศัพท์: การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The instructions were so ______ that nobody knew what to do first.\"","choices":["confuse","confused","confusing","confusion"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Things that cause a feeling take the -ing adjective: the instructions were \"confusing\"; the people who read them felt \"confused\".","th":"สิ่งที่ทำให้เกิดความรู้สึกใช้คุณศัพท์ลงท้าย -ing คำแนะนำจึง \"confusing\" (ชวนสับสน) ส่วนคนที่อ่านจะรู้สึก \"confused\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past perfect and narrative tenses","th":"การใช้ past perfect และกาลในการเล่าเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: tenses and the passive","th":"ไวยากรณ์: กาลและประโยค passive"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"When we finally arrived at the cinema, the film ______, so we missed the beginning.\"","choices":["was already start","already starts","had already started","has already started"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The film started before we arrived (two past events), so the earlier one uses the past perfect: \"had already started\".","th":"หนังเริ่มก่อนที่เราจะไปถึง (สองเหตุการณ์ในอดีต) เหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนจึงใช้ past perfect คือ \"had already started\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Reported speech","th":"การเล่าคำพูด (reported speech)"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: conditionals, clauses and reported speech","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ประโยคเงื่อนไข อนุประโยค และ reported speech"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Don't touch the wet paint,\" the teacher told us.\n\"The teacher told us ______ the wet paint.\"","choices":["to not touching","not to touch","don't touch","not touching"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A negative command is reported with \"tell someone not to + base form\": \"told us not to touch\".","th":"คำสั่งเชิงปฏิเสธในการเล่าคำพูดใช้ tell someone not to + กริยาช่องหนึ่ง คือ \"told us not to touch\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: agreement, articles, prepositions and comparison","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ความสอดคล้อง คำนำหน้านาม บุพบท และการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My father [[doesn't]] [[like]] coffee, [[but]] my mother [[drink]] it every day.","choices":["doesn't","like","but","drink"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"My mother\" is third person singular, so the verb should be \"drinks\".","th":"\"my mother\" เป็นบุรุษที่สามเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น \"drinks\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Articles and countable/uncountable nouns","th":"คำนำหน้านามและนามนับได้/นับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: agreement, articles, prepositions and comparison","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ความสอดคล้อง คำนำหน้านาม บุพบท และการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[An]] university student [[who]] lives next door [[has]] [[offered]] to help me with my project.","choices":["An","who","has","offered"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"University\" begins with a /j/ sound (\"you\"), so it takes \"a\", not \"an\".","th":"\"university\" ออกเสียงขึ้นต้นด้วยเสียง /j/ (ยู) จึงใช้ \"a\" ไม่ใช่ \"an\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing vocabulary in a text","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์ในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"Few foods are as common in Thai kitchens as instant noodles, yet their history is surprisingly short. They were (1) ______ in Japan in 1958 by a businessman named Momofuku Ando. After the Second World War, food was scarce, and Ando had watched hungry people queuing in the cold for a bowl of noodle soup. He wanted to create a meal that was cheap, tasty and could be stored (2) ______ months.\n\nFor a whole year, he experimented in a small shed behind his house. (3) ______ many failures, he discovered that frying cooked noodles in hot oil removed the water and left tiny holes, which allowed them to soften quickly (4) ______ boiling water was added. His chicken-flavoured noodles were at first considered a luxury, because they cost (5) ______ than fresh noodles from a shop.\n\nOver the following decades, instant noodles spread across Asia and then the world. Today, more than a hundred billion servings are eaten every year, and each country has (6) ______ its own flavours, from tom yum in Thailand to spicy kimchi in Korea.\n\nNot everyone is a fan, however. Nutritionists point out that most instant noodles are high in salt and fat but low in fibre, so they should be eaten only (7) ______. Nevertheless, for students on a tight budget, a steaming bowl at midnight remains one of life's small (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["raised","invented","founded","grown"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A new product is \"invented\". We \"found\" a company or city, \"raise\" children or money, and \"grow\" plants.","th":"ผลิตภัณฑ์ใหม่ใช้คำว่า \"invented\" (ประดิษฐ์คิดค้น) ส่วน \"found\" ใช้กับการก่อตั้งบริษัทหรือเมือง \"raise\" ใช้กับการเลี้ยงเด็กหรือระดมเงิน และ \"grow\" ใช้กับการปลูกพืช"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing grammatical forms in a text","th":"การเลือกรูปไวยากรณ์ในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"Few foods are as common in Thai kitchens as instant noodles, yet their history is surprisingly short. They were (1) ______ in Japan in 1958 by a businessman named Momofuku Ando. After the Second World War, food was scarce, and Ando had watched hungry people queuing in the cold for a bowl of noodle soup. He wanted to create a meal that was cheap, tasty and could be stored (2) ______ months.\n\nFor a whole year, he experimented in a small shed behind his house. (3) ______ many failures, he discovered that frying cooked noodles in hot oil removed the water and left tiny holes, which allowed them to soften quickly (4) ______ boiling water was added. His chicken-flavoured noodles were at first considered a luxury, because they cost (5) ______ than fresh noodles from a shop.\n\nOver the following decades, instant noodles spread across Asia and then the world. Today, more than a hundred billion servings are eaten every year, and each country has (6) ______ its own flavours, from tom yum in Thailand to spicy kimchi in Korea.\n\nNot everyone is a fan, however. Nutritionists point out that most instant noodles are high in salt and fat but low in fibre, so they should be eaten only (7) ______. Nevertheless, for students on a tight budget, a steaming bowl at midnight remains one of life's small (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["most","less","more","cheaper"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"They were a \"luxury\", so they cost more: \"cost more than\". \"Less\" contradicts \"luxury\", \"cost cheaper\" is not correct English, and \"most\" cannot be used with \"than\".","th":"บะหมี่ถือเป็นของฟุ่มเฟือย จึงราคาแพงกว่า \"cost more than\" ส่วน \"less\" ขัดกับคำว่า luxury \"cost cheaper\" ไม่ถูกหลักภาษา และ \"most\" ใช้กับ \"than\" ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: informational article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"How to Remember What You Study\n\nIn the 1880s, a German scientist named Hermann Ebbinghaus carried out an unusual experiment on himself. He memorised long lists of nonsense syllables, such as \"DAX\" and \"BOK\", and then tested how many he could still recall after different periods of time. His results produced what is now called the \"forgetting curve\". It showed that we lose new information extremely quickly: within a day, much of what we have just learned can disappear unless we do something to keep it.\n\nThe good news is that the curve can be flattened. Each time we review information, we forget it more slowly afterwards. This is the idea behind \"spaced repetition\", a technique in which learners review material at increasing intervals — for example, one day after a lesson, then three days later, then a week later. Compared with reading the same notes five times in one evening, spreading the same five reviews over two weeks leads to far better long-term memory.\n\nAnother powerful method is \"retrieval practice\". Instead of simply rereading a textbook, learners close the book and try to recall the key points, perhaps by writing them down or explaining them to a friend. This feels harder, and students often believe it is less effective because it is uncomfortable. In fact, the effort of pulling information out of memory is exactly what makes it stick.\n\nWhy, then, do so many students still rely on last-minute cramming? Part of the answer is that cramming can work for a test the next morning. The problem is what happens afterwards. Information learned in a rush tends to fade within days, so students who cram must relearn the same material before every exam. Those who space out their study, by contrast, build knowledge that lasts.\n\nNone of these techniques requires expensive equipment. A pack of homemade flashcards and a simple calendar are enough to begin. What they do require is planning — and the patience to trust a method that feels slower at first but pays off in the end.","table":null,"stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","choices":["The dangers of studying late at night","The life and career of Hermann Ebbinghaus","Why nonsense syllables are easy to learn","Research-based ways to remember information for longer"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Ebbinghaus is only the starting point; most of the text explains spaced repetition and retrieval practice as ways to remember for longer.","th":"Ebbinghaus เป็นเพียงจุดเริ่มต้น เนื้อหาส่วนใหญ่อธิบายวิธี spaced repetition และ retrieval practice ซึ่งช่วยให้จำได้นานขึ้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากเนื้อความ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: email, notice or advertisement","th":"การอ่าน: อีเมล ประกาศ หรือโฆษณา"},"passage":"KHAO YAI YOUNG SCIENTISTS CAMP\nFive days. One forest. Endless questions.\n\nAre you aged 14 to 16 and curious about the natural world? Join us for five days of hands-on research in one of Thailand's best-known national parks.\n\nDates: Monday 12 – Friday 16 April\nLocation: Field Research Centre, Khao Yai (transport from Bangkok included)\nCost: 9,500 baht (includes meals, shared rooms and all equipment)\n\nWhat you'll do\n• Track wildlife using camera traps with researchers from our partner university\n• Test the quality of stream water and learn what it tells us about the health of the forest\n• Spend one night observing bats and insects with a professional guide\n• Join a workshop on wildlife photography and learn how scientists use photos as evidence\n• Present your findings to parents and scientists on the final afternoon\n\nWho should apply?\nYou don't need top grades in science — just enthusiasm, teamwork and a willingness to get muddy! Previous camping experience is not necessary. Activities are taught in both English and Thai, so the camp is also a great chance to use your English in a real setting.\n\nHow to apply\nComplete the online form on our website and write 150–200 words, in English or Thai, answering the question: \"What is one thing in nature you have always wanted to understand, and why?\" Applications close on 15 February. Places are limited to 30 students, and successful applicants will be informed by email by 1 March.\n\nAccommodation is in simple shared rooms with fans rather than air conditioning.\n\nScholarships\nTen partial scholarships covering half the cost are available for students who would otherwise be unable to attend. Tick the scholarship box on the form and attach a short letter from a teacher.\n\nPlease note: the camp involves walking up to 8 km a day on forest paths. Tell us in advance about any medical conditions so that we can make suitable arrangements.","table":null,"stem":"Which applicant is the camp most clearly looking for?","choices":["A student with the highest science grades in the school","A keen student who enjoys teamwork outdoors, even with average science grades","A student who prefers to work alone indoors","A student who only wants to practise English"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The advert says \"You don't need top grades in science — just enthusiasm, teamwork and a willingness to get muddy\".","th":"โฆษณาระบุว่า \"You don't need top grades in science — just enthusiasm, teamwork and a willingness to get muddy\" จึงต้องการคนที่กระตือรือร้น ทำงานเป็นทีม และไม่กลัวเลอะ แม้เกรดวิทยาศาสตร์ไม่สูงมาก"}}]}},{"exam":"top4","set":3,"title":{"en":"M.4 English entrance (top schools) — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 โรงเรียนดัง — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"60 questions in 75 minutes, six parts: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading — the parts most top schools use. DeepDistrict practice format — not an official or past paper.","th":"60 ข้อ 75 นาที 6 ตอน: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวที่โรงเรียนดังส่วนใหญ่ใช้ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":75,"kinds":{"m1":60},"subjects":{"eng":60},"free":false,"file":"top4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Asking for and giving permission","th":"การขออนุญาตและการอนุญาต"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: Excuse me, is this seat taken?\nB: ______","choices":["No, I'm not taking it anywhere.","No, it's free. Please sit down.","Yes, I took it yesterday.","Yes, please take it."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Is this seat taken?\" asks whether someone is already using it. \"No, it's free\" means A can sit there. \"Yes, please take it\" contradicts itself, because \"Yes\" means the seat is occupied.","th":"\"Is this seat taken?\" ถามว่ามีคนนั่งแล้วหรือยัง คำตอบที่ถูกคือ \"No, it's free. Please sit down.\" ส่วน \"Yes, please take it\" ขัดแย้งในตัว เพราะ \"Yes\" แปลว่า มีคนนั่งแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Asking for and giving advice","th":"การขอและการให้คำแนะนำ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday functions","th":"บทสนทนา: ภาษาที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the conversation.\nA: I can't decide whether to join the debate club or the robotics club.\nB: ______","choices":["How about to join the robotics club?","Why not try both for a few weeks and then decide?","You should to choose debate.","It's up to me."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Why not + base form\" is a natural way to suggest something. \"How about\" needs \"-ing\" (\"How about joining\"), \"should\" is followed by the base form without \"to\", and \"It's up to me\" should be \"It's up to you\".","th":"\"Why not + กริยาช่องหนึ่ง\" ใช้เสนอแนะได้อย่างเป็นธรรมชาติ ส่วน \"How about\" ต้องตามด้วย V-ing \"should\" ตามด้วยกริยาช่องหนึ่งโดยไม่มี to และ \"It's up to me\" ควรเป็น \"It's up to you\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Using natural collocations","th":"การใช้คำที่ใช้คู่กันอย่างเป็นธรรมชาติ"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำศัพท์: คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The new law will ______ effect on 1 January, so shops must be ready by then.\"","choices":["do","bring","take","make"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"A law \"takes effect\", meaning it starts to apply. \"Make\", \"do\" and \"bring\" do not form this expression.","th":"กฎหมาย \"takes effect\" แปลว่า เริ่มมีผลบังคับใช้ ส่วน \"make\", \"do\" และ \"bring\" ไม่ใช้ในสำนวนนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Using natural collocations","th":"การใช้คำที่ใช้คู่กันอย่างเป็นธรรมชาติ"},"topic":{"en":"Vocabulary: collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำศัพท์: คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"After months of argument, the two sides finally ______ an agreement.\"","choices":["made up","reached","won","arrived"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"We \"reach an agreement\". \"Arrive\" needs \"at\", \"make up\" means \"invent\" or \"become friends again\", and we \"win\" a game, not an agreement.","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันคือ \"reach an agreement\" (บรรลุข้อตกลง) ส่วน \"arrive\" ต้องมี \"at\" \"make up\" แปลว่า แต่งเรื่องหรือคืนดีกัน และ \"win\" ใช้กับการแข่งขัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Passive voice","th":"ประโยค passive"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: tenses and the passive","th":"ไวยากรณ์: กาลและประโยค passive"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The thief ______ by the police yesterday afternoon.\"","choices":["was caught","caught","is caught","has been caught"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The police caught the thief, so the passive is needed, and \"yesterday afternoon\" requires the past simple: \"was caught\". \"Has been caught\" cannot be used with a finished past time.","th":"ตำรวจเป็นผู้จับ ขโมยจึงเป็นผู้ถูกกระทำ ต้องใช้ passive และ \"yesterday afternoon\" ต้องใช้ past simple คือ \"was caught\" ส่วน \"has been caught\" ใช้กับเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้วไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Reported speech","th":"การเล่าคำพูด (reported speech)"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: conditionals, clauses and reported speech","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ประโยคเงื่อนไข อนุประโยค และ reported speech"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I'll call you tomorrow,\" he said.\n\"He said he ______ me the following day.\"","choices":["calls","would call","will call","had called"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"In reported speech after \"said\", \"will\" usually changes to \"would\", and \"tomorrow\" becomes \"the following day\": \"he would call me\".","th":"ในการเล่าคำพูดหลัง \"said\" คำว่า \"will\" มักเปลี่ยนเป็น \"would\" และ \"tomorrow\" เปลี่ยนเป็น \"the following day\" จึงได้ \"he would call me\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Conditional sentences","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไข (if-clauses)"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: conditionals, clauses and reported speech","th":"ไวยากรณ์: ประโยคเงื่อนไข อนุประโยค และ reported speech"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: If you [[will finish]] your homework [[early]], we [[can]] [[go]] swimming.","choices":["will finish","early","can","go"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In a first conditional the if-clause uses the present simple, so it should be \"If you finish\".","th":"ในประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1 if-clause ใช้ present simple จึงต้องเป็น \"If you finish\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Gerund or infinitive after verbs","th":"การใช้ gerund หรือ infinitive หลังกริยา"},"topic":{"en":"Grammar: gerunds, infinitives and modals","th":"ไวยากรณ์: gerund, infinitive และ modal verbs"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: She [[suggested]] that we [[visit]] the museum instead of [[stay]] [[at]] home.","choices":["suggested","visit","stay","at"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Instead of\" is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund: \"instead of staying\". \"Suggested that we visit\" is correct.","th":"\"instead of\" เป็นบุพบท ต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ \"instead of staying\" ส่วน \"suggested that we visit\" ถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing linking words in a text","th":"การเลือกคำเชื่อมในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"For most of human history, anyone who looked up on a clear night could see thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way. Today, (1) ______, more than a third of the world's population can no longer see it from where they live. The cause is light pollution: the glow (2) ______ by street lamps, advertising signs and buildings that stay lit all night.\n\nLight pollution is not only a problem for astronomers. Many animals depend (3) ______ darkness. Baby sea turtles, for example, find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, which used to be the moonlit sea. Near brightly lit beaches, they often crawl the wrong way and die before (4) ______ the water. Migrating birds can also become confused, circling lit towers until they are exhausted.\n\nHumans are affected too. Bright light at night can (5) ______ with the production of melatonin, the hormone that controls sleep. Some researchers believe this may (6) ______ to health problems over time.\n\nThe good news is that, unlike many other kinds of pollution, light pollution can be reversed almost instantly. Simply switching off unnecessary lights, or using lamps that point downwards instead of into the sky, makes a (7) ______ difference. Several towns around the world have already been recognised as \"dark sky communities\" after changing their street lighting. Had more cities followed their example, millions more children (8) ______ the Milky Way with their own eyes by now.","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["however","for example","therefore","in addition"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence contrasts the past (everyone could see the stars) with today (many cannot), so \"however\" fits.","th":"ประโยคนี้แสดงความขัดแย้งระหว่างอดีต (ทุกคนเห็นดาว) กับปัจจุบัน (หลายคนมองไม่เห็น) จึงใช้ \"however\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing vocabulary in a text","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์ในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: completing a connected text","th":"เติมคำในบทความต่อเนื่อง"},"passage":"For most of human history, anyone who looked up on a clear night could see thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way. Today, (1) ______, more than a third of the world's population can no longer see it from where they live. The cause is light pollution: the glow (2) ______ by street lamps, advertising signs and buildings that stay lit all night.\n\nLight pollution is not only a problem for astronomers. Many animals depend (3) ______ darkness. Baby sea turtles, for example, find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, which used to be the moonlit sea. Near brightly lit beaches, they often crawl the wrong way and die before (4) ______ the water. Migrating birds can also become confused, circling lit towers until they are exhausted.\n\nHumans are affected too. Bright light at night can (5) ______ with the production of melatonin, the hormone that controls sleep. Some researchers believe this may (6) ______ to health problems over time.\n\nThe good news is that, unlike many other kinds of pollution, light pollution can be reversed almost instantly. Simply switching off unnecessary lights, or using lamps that point downwards instead of into the sky, makes a (7) ______ difference. Several towns around the world have already been recognised as \"dark sky communities\" after changing their street lighting. Had more cities followed their example, millions more children (8) ______ the Milky Way with their own eyes by now.","table":null,"stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["stop","prevent","disturb","interfere"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Only \"interfere\" is followed by \"with\": \"interfere with the production\". \"Prevent\", \"disturb\" and \"stop\" take a direct object without \"with\".","th":"มีเพียง \"interfere\" ที่ใช้คู่กับ \"with\" คือ \"interfere with the production\" (รบกวน) ส่วน \"prevent\", \"disturb\" และ \"stop\" ตามด้วยกรรมโดยตรง ไม่มี with"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Finding specific details","th":"การหารายละเอียดเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The Last Ferry\n\nBy the time Anan reached the pier, the last ferry to the island was already pulling away. He waved and shouted, but the engine was too loud, and the boat grew smaller until it was only a white dot on the dark water.\n\nHe sat down heavily on his backpack. His phone battery had died an hour earlier, and the only other person in sight was an old woman packing up a stall that sold grilled squid. Anan had promised his mother he would be home before nine. Now it was almost eight, and the next ferry would not leave until six the following morning.\n\n\"Missed it?\" the woman called. Anan nodded, too embarrassed to speak. She studied him for a moment, then pointed at a small fishing boat tied to the end of the pier. \"My son goes back to the island at half past eight,\" she said. \"He won't mind one more passenger — if you help him carry the ice.\"\n\nThe ice turned out to be twelve heavy boxes. By the time the last one was on board, Anan's arms were shaking and his shirt was soaked. The woman's son, a quiet man called Wit, simply handed him a bottle of cold water and started the engine.\n\nThe crossing took forty minutes. Wit said almost nothing, but halfway across he switched off the lights and told Anan to look over the side. The water was full of tiny blue sparks, flashing wherever the boat disturbed it. \"Plankton,\" Wit said. \"They glow when something moves them.\" Anan had lived near the sea all his life and had never seen anything like it.\n\nWhen they reached the island, his mother was waiting on the jetty, her face tight with worry. She had been about to call the police. Anan expected to be told off, and he was — but only after she had hugged him for a very long time.\n\nYears later, Anan would tell the story often. Strangely, he never described it as the night he missed the ferry. He always called it the night the sea lit up.","table":null,"stem":"Why couldn't Anan contact his mother from the pier?","choices":["His phone battery had run out.","There was no phone signal on the pier.","He had left his phone on the ferry.","He did not know her number."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 2 says, \"His phone battery had died an hour earlier.\"","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 2 ระบุว่า \"His phone battery had died an hour earlier.\" คือ แบตเตอรี่โทรศัพท์หมด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"top4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Working out vocabulary from context","th":"การเดาความหมายคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: informational article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Cities Are Getting Hotter\n\nOn a hot afternoon in April, the temperature in central Bangkok can be several degrees higher than in the rice fields an hour's drive away. This difference is known as the \"urban heat island\" effect, and it is becoming one of the most serious challenges facing cities around the world.\n\nThe main cause is the material cities are built from. Concrete, asphalt and brick absorb the sun's energy during the day and release it slowly at night, so city streets often stay warm long after sunset. Dark surfaces, such as roads and roofs, are particularly effective at trapping heat. At the same time, cities have far fewer trees and open fields than the countryside. Plants cool the air naturally by releasing water through their leaves, a process called transpiration. When green spaces are replaced by buildings, this natural air conditioning disappears.\n\nHuman activity adds to the problem. Cars, factories and, ironically, air conditioners all release heat into the streets. The more people use air conditioning to escape the heat, the hotter the air outside becomes, which in turn encourages even more people to switch theirs on.\n\nThe consequences are not only uncomfortable. Extreme heat puts pressure on electricity supplies and can be dangerous for elderly people, young children and outdoor workers. Some studies also suggest that hot cities suffer from worse air quality, as heat speeds up the chemical reactions that form smog.\n\nFortunately, there are solutions. Painting roofs white or light grey can reflect much of the sunlight that would otherwise be absorbed. Singapore has encouraged \"green roofs\" and walls covered in plants, while Medellín in Colombia created a network of tree-lined \"green corridors\" that has reportedly lowered temperatures along them by around two degrees. Even small changes, such as planting shade trees in school playgrounds, can make a noticeable difference.\n\nNone of these measures can stop global temperatures from rising. However, they can make cities more pleasant and safer places to live — and as more of the world's population moves into urban areas, that matters more than ever.","table":null,"stem":"Why does the writer say that air conditioners \"ironically\" add to the problem?","choices":["Air conditioners are the main cause of smog.","Air conditioners are too expensive for most city residents.","Air conditioners stop working in very hot weather.","A machine used to escape the heat actually makes the outside air hotter."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Something is ironic when the result is the opposite of what is intended. Air conditioners are used to escape heat, but they release heat outside and make the city hotter.","th":"สิ่งที่ \"ironic\" คือ ผลที่ออกมาตรงข้ามกับที่ตั้งใจ เครื่องปรับอากาศใช้เพื่อหนีความร้อน แต่กลับปล่อยความร้อนออกไปทำให้เมืองร้อนขึ้น"}}]}}];
